{"parentUuid":"48b8f422-7ef6-4da3-8aa2-0382e374c087","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpKukWU9vUk2f8Yi7g9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TDi5iHDVQLCv8LqMm3HpXT","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md","description":"Read round-1 review"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":787,"cache_read_input_tokens":55752,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":787,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TDi5iHDVQLCv8LqMm3HpXT":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md","description":"Read round-1 review"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpKuSehcasgWkbc4ETL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"52375cea-416b-4d75-b817-f2fa13727c32","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:39.454Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"52375cea-416b-4d75-b817-f2fa13727c32","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TDi5iHDVQLCv8LqMm3HpXT","type":"tool_result","content":"# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6c (6.8.3-6.11), 19 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent falsification pass), 2026-10-03, Mac.\nScope: `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt` (19 files) in `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 363-379 (printed 352-368), plus PDF 288, 299, 300, 301, 305, 308 and 536.\n\n**What I opened as images** (200 dpi renders in `staging/_renders/r6c_p*.png`, 300 dpi crops for figures): PDF 288, 300 (lower half), 301, 308, 363, 364, 365 (with a 300 dpi crop of Fig 6.25), 366, 367, 368, 369, 370, 371, 372, 373, 374, 375 (with a 300 dpi crop of Fig 6.30), 376, 377, 378, 379, 536. PDF 305 was also opened as an image (4-12 weeks, 4-8 week blocks, 1-3 week latency, 23-25% all confirmed). PDF 299 was checked from the text layer only. Every value below that is read off a graph was measured against the gridlines.\n\n**Also checked:**\n- Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote against `docs/method-baseline.md` (lines 59, 60, 64, 74, 75, 76, 78, 101, 104, 105, 106, 114, 116, 117, 122, 123, 124, 142, 151, 153, 180, 182, 185, 186, 255). All quotes, line numbers and tags match.\n- Every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves today in the staging library or the vault, including the links into 6a and 6b.\n- Em dashes appear only in the section headers.\n- No entry contains review history.\n\n**Fig 6.28** (PDF 369) is a schematic with no numeric axes. No in-scope entry cites it, so there are no values to check.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum | FIX | (1) **Fig 6.30 block timing is misread.** The entry says the shaded block runs \"from about month 1.3 to about month 4.3\". On the 300 dpi crop (PDF 375 / p.364), the block runs from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5: left edge 57 px past the month-1 tick at about 98 px per month, right edge about half a month past month 4. The curve values are right: vertical jump about 7 then about 4.5 at month 8; stride about 11; ten jumps about 9-9.5. (2) **\"4-12 weeks\" has three referents, not two.** The entry's \"They are different quantities\" sentence names only PDF 374 (5-12 week loading stages) and PDF 375 (LDTE lasts 4-12 weeks). PDF 305 / p.294 also uses \"4-12 weeks\", for how long unidirectional training \"can be very effective\". The Addendum, PDF 536 / p.525, attaches 4-12 weeks to *stage length*: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\". That reading matches the PDF 374 stage figure better than the PDF 375 reading. The book's own rule that LDTE duration is about equal to stage length (T2 ≈ T1, stated on both PDF 375 and 536) reconciles 375 and 536. PDF 305 is a genuinely separate quantity. This is not grounds for `contested`; just keep each locator with its number. | (1) Replace with \"during a shaded block from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\". (2) Append to the When NOT sentence: \"The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things: how long unidirectional training stays effective (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), how long the LDTE lasts (PDF p. 375), and, in Verkhoshansky's Addendum, the length of the concentrated stages themselves (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525). Keep the locator with the number.\" Everything else checked: 5-12 weeks and the 4 citations at PDF 374 / p.363; Fig 6.29 shading 0 to about 2.47 months, dips to about 84, 92 and 95%, recross 100 at about month 2.05, month-4 values about 124, 118.5 and 111%; F1, F2 and F3 in Fig 6.31 at PDF 375; maintenance vs LDTE at PDF 377 / p.366 (italic). New angle versus 5c, 4.1.1 and Bompa is confirmed: the dose-response optimum and the maintenance distinction. The 1-3 week latency (PDF 305) and the 4-12 week duration are not restated differently. |\n| heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window | FIX | (1) **Wrong voice for the Addendum.** The entry writes \"The Addendum restates...\" and \"**Example** — The authors' own, from the Addendum\". The Addendum section is \"9.1 Laws of Sports Mastery: Principles of Training, Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky, Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\" (PDF 515 / p.504). It is one author's paper in a separate translation, not Siff and Verkhoshansky. (2) **Missing dedup link.** 6d's `heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block` (PDF 382-383) covers the same strength-then-technique question from the 6.12.2 passage. This entry's angle is different and keeps its place: the emergence-period volume rule, competition-period strength work, and SPP's dominant role. The two entries must link each other. **Contested: upheld.** I checked the image of PDF 536. It says \"volume concentrated loads are handled by the body relatively easily and without harm to the body, but intense loads negatively react to the work volume\". That is the reverse of PDF 375 (\"easy and painless ... to change to *intensive* loading, but it will react negatively to *extensive* work\", italics in the original). The Addendum's own triple-jumper example (a volume rise \"by only a few percent\" led to \"a fast decrease\") sides with Ch 6. The entry records both pages and labels the translation-slip idea as Inference, which is right under the brief. | (1) Replace \"The Addendum restates\" with \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515) restates\". Replace \"The authors' own, from the Addendum\" with \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\". Add to the Inference: \"the Addendum is a separate translation of Verkhoshansky's text, which makes a translation slip plausible\". Optional: the Addendum example names \"the athlete\" (singular) losing national-team selection, so \"triple jumpers ... lost both\" could read \"the athlete lost both\". (2) Add `[[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]` to Related, and suggest the reciprocal link to the 6d fixer. Checked: PDF 375-376 quotes; \"dominant role\" at PDF 376; baseline lines 105 and 186. |\n| claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training | FIX | **Unlabelled gloss on Susman.** The entry says: \"The authors cite this as evidence that load-induced homeostatic disturbance shows in psycho-physiological indicators, not as a documented LDTE.\" The source (PDF 377 / p.366) puts Susman 1978 directly after \"disturbance of homeostasis can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\". It then describes the indicators rising \"as the supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction) phase emerged\" after loads were cut. The authors never say \"not as a documented LDTE\". That framing is the writer's reading. | Reword to: \"The authors do not call this an LDTE; they give it after stating that disturbance of homeostasis 'can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators' (PDF p. 377). Inference: it is offered as a sea-level example of the dip-and-rebound pattern in non-aerobic measures, not as a documented LDTE in aerobic productivity.\" Checked against the PDF 376 image: 1700 m, 3 weeks, more than 4 weeks, peak about 1 week, 2250 m, 4-minute ergometer test, the hypobaric explanation and the \"no experimental data\" sentence. Title scope is correct. |\n| claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | (1) **Missing dedup links.** 6b's `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation` (6.8.1, Fig 6.23) is the passage PDF 371 points back to (\"see 6.6.1 and Fig 6.23\"). 6d's `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes` (PDF 379-381) makes the same qualification split for complex vs concentrated loading. This entry has a real new angle (the qualification split, with Kornelyuk's glycolytic data) but links neither. (2) **Italics scope is overstated.** The entry calls the whole conclusion \"italicised in the original\". On PDF 371 only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is italic; \"in the athlete's physical fitness\" is roman. **Contested: upheld.** The vault Issurin 2016 brief (lines 46-51) is paraphrased accurately. It targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model (line 51), finds the evidence insufficient for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, and places the model with narrow-quality sports. This is a disagreement about how far the evidence reaches, not a flat opposite. The Kornelyuk sprinter and jumper evidence sits outside Issurin's named domains; only the boxing study (Filimonov) is inside them, which the entry already notes as Inference. | (1) Add `[[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]]` and `[[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]` to Related. (2) Change to \"Their conclusion, partly italicised in the original:\". Optional: add to the contested note \"Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names.\" Checked: the distributed and concentrated definitions, \"both methods are successful\", Kornelyuk 1980, Levchenko 1986, Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982, Filimonov 1979, Mironenko & Antonova 1981, all at PDF 371 / p.360; baseline lines 106, 117, 182. |\n| heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast | FIX | **The contested reason uses a mis-scoped locator and misses the sharpest contradiction.** (1) The entry lists the PDF 301 bullets (\"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"; \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises\") as Chapter 5's fixed orders, set against a within-session rule. On the images of PDF 300-301, those bullets sit under \"**The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge:**\" (PDF 300 / p.289). They are stage-level rules, not session rules. (2) The real within-session statement is two paragraphs higher on PDF 300, and the entry misses it: \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification ... Least effective is the sequence of *strength* followed by *speed-strength* means during the same session.\" That directly opposes PDF 371's \"preserve the above order\" (barbell then jumping, barbell then plyometrics) and PDF 288 / p.277 (\"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\"). (3) PDF 371 alone (\"fairly unimportant\" ... \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\") is a ranking (order matters less than volume and contrast, but keep it). It is not a contradiction. **Contested: upheld, but on the corrected evidence.** | Rewrite the \"Contested inside the book\" paragraph. \"On PDF p. 371 order is ranked below volume and contrast but still to be kept (a hedge, not a contradiction). The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order. PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277: resistance plus plyometrics works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions'. PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288, novices: 'no statistically significant difference' between strength-first and speed-strength-first. PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289, novices and average qualification: 'Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session', which opposes p. 371's 'preserve the above order' (barbell then jumping). The PDF p. 301 bullets are stage-level 'principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period' (PDF p. 300), so they belong with [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], not here.\" Optional: under Why it works, add that PDF 438 also cites \"Fig 1.7\" for within-workout order, which suggests a session-order figure lost in renumbering (Inference). Checked: the six pairings and the italic sentence at PDF 371; the PDF 288 sentence in 5.2.4 (section starts PDF 278); the Fig 1.7 torque diagram at PDF 28-29. |\n| framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading | GO | None. | Checked against the images. PDF 371-372 / p.360-361: the \"inadequate ... contradictory\" caveat and \"so conditional\"; the three positive and two negative pairings (Volkov 1975); 2-3 days; 3-8 hours; ATP-CP deterioration (Nabatnikova, Volkov, Platonov). PDF 373 / p.362: the \"rest pause\" sentence, italic as claimed. The Example is labelled Inference. Baseline lines 114 and 116 match. |\n| heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive | FIX | (1) **Unlabelled writer reasoning.** \"The authors' own Fig 6.24 experiment used 3-month stages ..., so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum\" is the writer's conclusion. \"Bondarchuk agrees on the direction, from a different premise\" is the writer's judgment of another book. (2) **Missing explicit counter-rule.** The entry discusses Fig 5.18 (jumps first) as \"not a clean contradiction\". It does not cite the explicit stage-level rule on PDF 300-301: \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises\", stated for \"sequential use of training means over a given period\", from novices and average-qualification athletes. That is the text form of the jumps-first order, and it reads against p.372's \"submaximal resistance first, followed by explosive training\". | (1) Prefix \"Inference:\" to \"so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum\" and to \"Bondarchuk agrees on the direction\". (2) After the Fig 5.18 sentence add: \"Chapter 5 states this as a rule: among 'principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period', 'Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises' and 'Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before' (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290, drawn from novices and athletes of average qualification). Inference: 'speed-strength' there means jumping exercises (Fig 5.13), not the explosive training of p. 372, so the conflict turns on what the first stage contains.\" Checked against the PDF 372 image: 4-6 weeks, 2 weeks, \"transient accumulation\" (italic), the reverse sequence giving a negative effect. Fig 5.18 at PDF 308 reads jumping exercises, barbell exercises, jumps with weights, depth jumping. Baseline lines 106 and 180. |\n| framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 373 / p.362 image: both opening bullets; the concurrent populations (Baranov, Kuzovenkov, Malikov); the sequential order and citations; Yakovlev et al 1960 and Astrand & Rodahl 1970; \"A third viewpoint\" (italic), its populations and \"One can assume\". The p.370 cross-note is consistent with the literal p.370 reading. Baseline lines 117 and 124. |\n| claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality | FIX | **The \"Unclear wording\" paragraph mis-sorts the evidence.** It groups the PDF 372 negative pairing (\"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\") with PDF 373 as reading against the literal PDF 370 sentence (\"anaerobic work produces a positive effect only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work\"). It does not. The literal p.370 reading (anaerobic first) agrees with both p.372 lists: positive \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\", and negative \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\". Only p.373's stage-level sequential viewpoint (aerobic first) conflicts with it. The entry's second sentence (\"may mean 'is preceded by,' which would fit p. 373 but not ... p. 372\") already says this, so the paragraph contradicts itself. | Rewrite: \"**Unclear wording:** read literally, the p. 370 sentence (anaerobic volume helps 'only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work, thereby stimulating the aerobic reaction') fits the session-level lists on PDF p. 372, but conflicts with the stage-level sequential model on PDF p. 373, where aerobic work comes first and 'promotes the training-effect of anaerobic work'. Inference: if 'precedes' is a slip for 'is preceded by', it fits p. 373 and not p. 372. The citations (Serafimova 1974; Naumenko 1978) are the same ones p. 373 gives for its aerobic-first warning, which favours the slip reading, but the book does not resolve it.\" Checked against the PDF 370 / p.359 image: 3-4 months, up to 10 months, one month, HR 120-170, 2-3 months, five citations, about 4 months, four citations, the \"not definitive\" note. Baseline line 59. |\n| principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue | GO | None. | Checked against the images. PDF 369 / p.358: the homeostasis sentence; \"within optimal limits\"; the italic ligamentous-joint sentence (italic as claimed); the four sports; magnitude by qualification and its five citations. PDF 370 / p.359: intensity definition, the volume over time-to-maximum ratio, \"only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading\". Baseline lines 101, 142 and 153. |\n| framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 / p.367 image: three levels, \"one or more years\", \"up to 5-6 months\", \"not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels\", \"controllable\". I opened the PDF 379 / p.368 image: \"not especially complex\" and \"much smaller\" match. The entry's \"page image not opened\" note is honest; the fixer may change it to \"checked against the page image\" only after opening PDF 379. Baseline lines 64 and 255. |\n| claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification | GO | None. | Checked against the images of PDF 377-378 / p.366-367: 3 to 4 times a year; Pollock 1973, 3-4 months; twice a month; medium qualification (italic); the cause; the monocycle and bi-cycle patterns; highly qualified (italic). The Siff voice note is reasonable (Verkhoshansky is named in the third person). Baseline lines 59 and 60. |\n| case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 / p.367 image: the italic sentence (italic as claimed); Verkhoshansky 1988; the plantarflexor and thigh-extensor sentences; \"feels perfectly fit\"; the prescription. `untested` fits a single observation. Baseline lines 74 and 78. |\n| case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 / p.367 image: December and March-April; 800 m 4.8 vs 3.4 s; 1500 m 6.3 vs 4.6 s; the 400 m and 1200 m control runs; OBLA. The Zhdanovich link to PDF 374 is labelled Inference. |\n| case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers | GO | None. **Fig 6.24 values** (PDF 365 / p.354) were measured on the image and all agree within one gridline unit. Absolute strength: about 36/54, 50/45, 63/53. Explosive: about 54/102, 88/78, 123/96. Vertical jump: about 14/25, 15/14, 33/23. The end-state comparison (group 1 about level with or above group 3) is right. **Units problem described correctly.** The caption says \"% increase\" while the panels read kgf, kgf and cm. The caption also calls graph 3 \"height after a depth jump\" while the panel is titled \"Vertical Jump\"; the entry reports both. **Same-study link is properly labelled.** \"Inference: they may be the same study described twice or two studies; the book does not say.\" The surrounding comparison with PDF 299 (novices, weights then depth jumps, reverse, both in both stages) is factual. | Checked: the PDF 364 / p.353 design text; conclusions 1-3 on PDF 364-365; \"high jumpers of medium qualification\"; the Khodykin and Chernousov citations on PDF 364 and the Ch 5 list on PDF 299 (Tatyan/Tatian also overlaps); baseline line 180. |\n| case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers | FIX | **Three Fig 6.25 readings are about a unit high.** Measured on the 300 dpi crop of PDF 365 at about 11.2 px per percentage point: absolute strength GP1 about 7.0, GP2 about 18.1, GP3 about 16.7; speed GP1 about 18.1, GP2 about 11.2, GP3 about 13.6; power GP1 about 6.1, GP2 about 14.2, GP3 about 20.1. The entry gives power GP3 \"about 21\" (the bar ends exactly on the 20 gridline), power GP2 \"about 15\", and \"18-19\" for absolute GP2 and speed GP1. | In Figure 6.25 values, the Example and When NOT: power \"GP2 about 14, GP3 about 20\"; absolute strength \"GP2 about 18\"; speed \"GP1 about 18\". The Example becomes \"power rose about 20% in the 30-90% group against about 14% (70-90%) and about 6% (30-50%), while speed rose most, about 18%, with 30-50% loads\". The When NOT pair becomes \"about 17% vs about 18%\". Checked: 7 weeks; the 30-50%, 70-90% and 30-90% groups; \"metabolic cost\"; the caption (\"highly qualified jumpers\", \"Nedobyvailov\"); \"Group 1, graph 2\", all at PDF 365. Baseline line 76 quote and tag. |\n| framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect | GO | None. | Checked against the images of PDF 364-366 / p.353-355: \"Acute TE > Delayed TE > Cumulative TE\" with its 3 citations; the printed reversed parenthesis \"(i.e. the sum of the parts is greater than the whole)\" (a good catch, correctly labelled); partial and cumulative TE (italic); the four factors (Verkhoshansky 1970); the two criteria; \"(see 6.8)\". Fig 6.26 at PDF 366: short-term and long-term effects each carry a positive/negative box, and transient and accumulated each carry one. \"Somewhat tedious\". Baseline line 182. |\n| heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation | FIX | (1) **Unlabelled gloss** under Why it works: \"Removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal.\" The authors say premature intervention \"can hinder the body's supercompensatory ability\" (PDF 368); the \"signal\" framing is the writer's. (2) **Unlabelled judgment** under When NOT: \"the Kuchnyev result confounds restoration with a 15% rise in volume.\" The source says \"At the same time, the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%\" (PDF 368 / p.357) and does not say which group's volume rose. (3) **Format:** no blank line before `**Example**`. | (1) Prefix: \"Inference: removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal.\" (2) Replace with: \"The text says training volume rose by an average of 15% but not whether in both groups or only the restoration group; Inference: if only the restoration group, the result confounds restoration with extra volume.\" (3) Insert the blank line. Checked against the PDF 368 image: 24.2/18.9% vs 7.7/4.9%, 15%, Kuchnyev 1977, Platonov 1980, 2-4% and 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979), Yakovlev 1971, Siff & Yessis 1992, Yevgenyev et al 1975. Baseline lines 74, 151 and 153. |\n| claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks | GO | None. | Checked against the images of PDF 367-368 / p.356-357: the italic \"It is undoubtedly important...\" sentence (italic as claimed); \"more intense stimulus\"; 1:45:0 (printed that way); 10.6-10.7 s; at least 9 m for three jumps; 33-34 m for ten; Zhdanovich 1980; Verkhoshansky & Sirenko 1982; \"archaic viewpoint\". Baseline lines 122 and 123. |\n\n**Tally: GO 10, FIX 9, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Contested entries: is each a real contradiction?\n\n1. **Technique heuristic (PDF 375 vs 536).** Yes, literally. The Addendum sentence reverses Ch 6, but its own example agrees with Ch 6, and it is a separate translation (Yessis) of Verkhoshansky's paper. Keep `contested` and record both pages; the translation-slip reading stays labelled Inference.\n2. **Within-session order (PDF 371 vs 288, 299, 300, 301).** Yes, but not where the entry puts it. PDF 371 on its own is a hedge. The real conflict sits between chapters:\n   - PDF 288: resistance before plyometrics within a session.\n   - PDF 299: no difference for novices.\n   - PDF 300: strength then speed-strength in one session is least effective (novices and average qualification).\n   - PDF 371: keep barbell then jumps.\n\n   PDF 301 is stage-level and should leave this entry's contested reason.\n3. **Concentrated vs distributed (PDF 371 vs Issurin 2016).** Yes, as a disagreement about evidence scope. The Issurin brief is stated accurately and targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's own A-T-R.\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 363-379 that no entry captures)\n\n1. **Do not use highly stimulating means prematurely** (PDF 368-369 / p.357-358, \"Training Potential of the Loading\", Fig 6.28). Training potential is relative to current fitness and falls as special work capacity rises. Using highly stimulating means early, \"because the body is functionally unprepared for them\", causes \"excessive intensification of training and disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\". The 5.4.4 entry `heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades` covers replacing means, but not this warning. Add it there as a Ch 6 cross-reference, or write a short heuristic.\n2. **SPP means must exceed competition conditions for highly qualified athletes** (PDF 367 / p.356). To preserve training potential, means must \"not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions, with regard to maximum force, time of maximum force development and the power of the metabolic processes\". From this the authors derive the case for strength work. They also say external similarity is \"often overemphasized\" against the regime of muscular work and energy mechanism (squats justified). This may be covered conceptually by `principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed` (4.3.3) and the dynamic-correspondence entries. The new angle is the tie to training potential and qualification. A Related or cross-reference addition may be enough.\n3. **6.8.3: general qualities do not assemble into special preparedness** (PDF 363 / p.352). \"The emergence of specificity from an array of non-specific fitness qualities ... is not supported\". Muscle endurance from supplementary running will not directly help a rower or cyclist. This is probably covered conceptually by the Ch 1.3 specificity entries, but no Ch 6 entry carries the 6.8.3 statement. Confirm coverage or add a line to the nearest specificity entry.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions (for the 6b and 6d fixers; not required for this batch)\n\n- 6d `heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block` should link `heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window`.\n- 6b `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation` and 6d `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes` should link `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. **principle LDTE:** Fig 6.30 block about month 1.6 to 4.5; record all three \"4-12 weeks\" referents (PDF 305, 375, 536), noting that T2 ≈ T1 reconciles 375 and 536.\n2. **technique heuristic:** Addendum voice is \"Verkhoshansky's (9.1, trans. Yessis)\", not \"the authors\"; add the link to the 6d technique-work heuristic.\n3. **altitude claim:** reword and label the Susman \"not as a documented LDTE\" gloss.\n4. **concentrated claim:** add links to the 6b distributed-vs-concentrated framework and the 6d complex-training claim; \"partly italicised\".\n5. **within-session heuristic:** rewrite the contested reason. PDF 301 is stage-level (PDF 300 heading); add the PDF 300 novice/average \"least effective ... strength followed by speed-strength during the same session\"; call p.371 a hedge.\n6. **sequential heuristic:** label two Inferences; add the PDF 300-301 stage-level rule.\n7. **time-limits claim:** fix the mis-sorted p.372 evidence in \"Unclear wording\".\n8. **Nedobyvailov:** Fig 6.25 power GP3 about 20, GP2 about 14; absolute GP2 about 18; speed GP1 about 18.\n9. **restoration heuristic:** label two glosses as Inference; add the blank line before Example.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d8030315-57d7-45c5-aee8-6017b51ab1b6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:39.724Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Review: Supertraining Chapter 6c (6.8.3-6.11), 19 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent falsification pass), 2026-10-03, Mac.\nScope: `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt` (19 files) in `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx 363-379 (printed 352-368), plus PDF 288, 299, 300, 301, 305, 308 and 536.\n\n**What I opened as images** (200 dpi renders in `staging/_renders/r6c_p*.png`, 300 dpi crops for figures): PDF 288, 300 (lower half), 301, 308, 363, 364, 365 (with a 300 dpi crop of Fig 6.25), 366, 367, 368, 369, 370, 371, 372, 373, 374, 375 (with a 300 dpi crop of Fig 6.30), 376, 377, 378, 379, 536. PDF 305 was also opened as an image (4-12 weeks, 4-8 week blocks, 1-3 week latency, 23-25% all confirmed). PDF 299 was checked from the text layer only. Every value below that is read off a graph was measured against the gridlines.\n\n**Also checked:**\n- Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote against `docs/method-baseline.md` (lines 59, 60, 64, 74, 75, 76, 78, 101, 104, 105, 106, 114, 116, 117, 122, 123, 124, 142, 151, 153, 180, 182, 185, 186, 255). All quotes, line numbers and tags match.\n- Every `[[wikilink]]` in the 19 files resolves today in the staging library or the vault, including the links into 6a and 6b.\n- Em dashes appear only in the section headers.\n- No entry contains review history.\n\n**Fig 6.28** (PDF 369) is a schematic with no numeric axes. No in-scope entry cites it, so there are no values to check.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum | FIX | (1) **Fig 6.30 block timing is misread.** The entry says the shaded block runs \"from about month 1.3 to about month 4.3\". On the 300 dpi crop (PDF 375 / p.364), the block runs from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5: left edge 57 px past the month-1 tick at about 98 px per month, right edge about half a month past month 4. The curve values are right: vertical jump about 7 then about 4.5 at month 8; stride about 11; ten jumps about 9-9.5. (2) **\"4-12 weeks\" has three referents, not two.** The entry's \"They are different quantities\" sentence names only PDF 374 (5-12 week loading stages) and PDF 375 (LDTE lasts 4-12 weeks). PDF 305 / p.294 also uses \"4-12 weeks\", for how long unidirectional training \"can be very effective\". The Addendum, PDF 536 / p.525, attaches 4-12 weeks to *stage length*: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\". That reading matches the PDF 374 stage figure better than the PDF 375 reading. The book's own rule that LDTE duration is about equal to stage length (T2 ≈ T1, stated on both PDF 375 and 536) reconciles 375 and 536. PDF 305 is a genuinely separate quantity. This is not grounds for `contested`; just keep each locator with its number. | (1) Replace with \"during a shaded block from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\". (2) Append to the When NOT sentence: \"The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things: how long unidirectional training stays effective (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), how long the LDTE lasts (PDF p. 375), and, in Verkhoshansky's Addendum, the length of the concentrated stages themselves (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525). Keep the locator with the number.\" Everything else checked: 5-12 weeks and the 4 citations at PDF 374 / p.363; Fig 6.29 shading 0 to about 2.47 months, dips to about 84, 92 and 95%, recross 100 at about month 2.05, month-4 values about 124, 118.5 and 111%; F1, F2 and F3 in Fig 6.31 at PDF 375; maintenance vs LDTE at PDF 377 / p.366 (italic). New angle versus 5c, 4.1.1 and Bompa is confirmed: the dose-response optimum and the maintenance distinction. The 1-3 week latency (PDF 305) and the 4-12 week duration are not restated differently. |\n| heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window | FIX | (1) **Wrong voice for the Addendum.** The entry writes \"The Addendum restates...\" and \"**Example** — The authors' own, from the Addendum\". The Addendum section is \"9.1 Laws of Sports Mastery: Principles of Training, Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky, Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\" (PDF 515 / p.504). It is one author's paper in a separate translation, not Siff and Verkhoshansky. (2) **Missing dedup link.** 6d's `heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block` (PDF 382-383) covers the same strength-then-technique question from the 6.12.2 passage. This entry's angle is different and keeps its place: the emergence-period volume rule, competition-period strength work, and SPP's dominant role. The two entries must link each other. **Contested: upheld.** I checked the image of PDF 536. It says \"volume concentrated loads are handled by the body relatively easily and without harm to the body, but intense loads negatively react to the work volume\". That is the reverse of PDF 375 (\"easy and painless ... to change to *intensive* loading, but it will react negatively to *extensive* work\", italics in the original). The Addendum's own triple-jumper example (a volume rise \"by only a few percent\" led to \"a fast decrease\") sides with Ch 6. The entry records both pages and labels the translation-slip idea as Inference, which is right under the brief. | (1) Replace \"The Addendum restates\" with \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515) restates\". Replace \"The authors' own, from the Addendum\" with \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\". Add to the Inference: \"the Addendum is a separate translation of Verkhoshansky's text, which makes a translation slip plausible\". Optional: the Addendum example names \"the athlete\" (singular) losing national-team selection, so \"triple jumpers ... lost both\" could read \"the athlete lost both\". (2) Add `[[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]` to Related, and suggest the reciprocal link to the 6d fixer. Checked: PDF 375-376 quotes; \"dominant role\" at PDF 376; baseline lines 105 and 186. |\n| claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training | FIX | **Unlabelled gloss on Susman.** The entry says: \"The authors cite this as evidence that load-induced homeostatic disturbance shows in psycho-physiological indicators, not as a documented LDTE.\" The source (PDF 377 / p.366) puts Susman 1978 directly after \"disturbance of homeostasis can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\". It then describes the indicators rising \"as the supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction) phase emerged\" after loads were cut. The authors never say \"not as a documented LDTE\". That framing is the writer's reading. | Reword to: \"The authors do not call this an LDTE; they give it after stating that disturbance of homeostasis 'can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators' (PDF p. 377). Inference: it is offered as a sea-level example of the dip-and-rebound pattern in non-aerobic measures, not as a documented LDTE in aerobic productivity.\" Checked against the PDF 376 image: 1700 m, 3 weeks, more than 4 weeks, peak about 1 week, 2250 m, 4-minute ergometer test, the hypobaric explanation and the \"no experimental data\" sentence. Title scope is correct. |\n| claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes | FIX | (1) **Missing dedup links.** 6b's `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation` (6.8.1, Fig 6.23) is the passage PDF 371 points back to (\"see 6.6.1 and Fig 6.23\"). 6d's `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes` (PDF 379-381) makes the same qualification split for complex vs concentrated loading. This entry has a real new angle (the qualification split, with Kornelyuk's glycolytic data) but links neither. (2) **Italics scope is overstated.** The entry calls the whole conclusion \"italicised in the original\". On PDF 371 only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is italic; \"in the athlete's physical fitness\" is roman. **Contested: upheld.** The vault Issurin 2016 brief (lines 46-51) is paraphrased accurately. It targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model (line 51), finds the evidence insufficient for broad use in combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, and places the model with narrow-quality sports. This is a disagreement about how far the evidence reaches, not a flat opposite. The Kornelyuk sprinter and jumper evidence sits outside Issurin's named domains; only the boxing study (Filimonov) is inside them, which the entry already notes as Inference. | (1) Add `[[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]]` and `[[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]` to Related. (2) Change to \"Their conclusion, partly italicised in the original:\". Optional: add to the contested note \"Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names.\" Checked: the distributed and concentrated definitions, \"both methods are successful\", Kornelyuk 1980, Levchenko 1986, Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982, Filimonov 1979, Mironenko & Antonova 1981, all at PDF 371 / p.360; baseline lines 106, 117, 182. |\n| heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast | FIX | **The contested reason uses a mis-scoped locator and misses the sharpest contradiction.** (1) The entry lists the PDF 301 bullets (\"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"; \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises\") as Chapter 5's fixed orders, set against a within-session rule. On the images of PDF 300-301, those bullets sit under \"**The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge:**\" (PDF 300 / p.289). They are stage-level rules, not session rules. (2) The real within-session statement is two paragraphs higher on PDF 300, and the entry misses it: \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification ... Least effective is the sequence of *strength* followed by *speed-strength* means during the same session.\" That directly opposes PDF 371's \"preserve the above order\" (barbell then jumping, barbell then plyometrics) and PDF 288 / p.277 (\"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\"). (3) PDF 371 alone (\"fairly unimportant\" ... \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\") is a ranking (order matters less than volume and contrast, but keep it). It is not a contradiction. **Contested: upheld, but on the corrected evidence.** | Rewrite the \"Contested inside the book\" paragraph. \"On PDF p. 371 order is ranked below volume and contrast but still to be kept (a hedge, not a contradiction). The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order. PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277: resistance plus plyometrics works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions'. PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288, novices: 'no statistically significant difference' between strength-first and speed-strength-first. PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289, novices and average qualification: 'Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session', which opposes p. 371's 'preserve the above order' (barbell then jumping). The PDF p. 301 bullets are stage-level 'principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period' (PDF p. 300), so they belong with [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], not here.\" Optional: under Why it works, add that PDF 438 also cites \"Fig 1.7\" for within-workout order, which suggests a session-order figure lost in renumbering (Inference). Checked: the six pairings and the italic sentence at PDF 371; the PDF 288 sentence in 5.2.4 (section starts PDF 278); the Fig 1.7 torque diagram at PDF 28-29. |\n| framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading | GO | None. | Checked against the images. PDF 371-372 / p.360-361: the \"inadequate ... contradictory\" caveat and \"so conditional\"; the three positive and two negative pairings (Volkov 1975); 2-3 days; 3-8 hours; ATP-CP deterioration (Nabatnikova, Volkov, Platonov). PDF 373 / p.362: the \"rest pause\" sentence, italic as claimed. The Example is labelled Inference. Baseline lines 114 and 116 match. |\n| heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive | FIX | (1) **Unlabelled writer reasoning.** \"The authors' own Fig 6.24 experiment used 3-month stages ..., so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum\" is the writer's conclusion. \"Bondarchuk agrees on the direction, from a different premise\" is the writer's judgment of another book. (2) **Missing explicit counter-rule.** The entry discusses Fig 5.18 (jumps first) as \"not a clean contradiction\". It does not cite the explicit stage-level rule on PDF 300-301: \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises\", stated for \"sequential use of training means over a given period\", from novices and average-qualification athletes. That is the text form of the jumps-first order, and it reads against p.372's \"submaximal resistance first, followed by explosive training\". | (1) Prefix \"Inference:\" to \"so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum\" and to \"Bondarchuk agrees on the direction\". (2) After the Fig 5.18 sentence add: \"Chapter 5 states this as a rule: among 'principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period', 'Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises' and 'Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before' (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290, drawn from novices and athletes of average qualification). Inference: 'speed-strength' there means jumping exercises (Fig 5.13), not the explosive training of p. 372, so the conflict turns on what the first stage contains.\" Checked against the PDF 372 image: 4-6 weeks, 2 weeks, \"transient accumulation\" (italic), the reverse sequence giving a negative effect. Fig 5.18 at PDF 308 reads jumping exercises, barbell exercises, jumps with weights, depth jumping. Baseline lines 106 and 180. |\n| framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 373 / p.362 image: both opening bullets; the concurrent populations (Baranov, Kuzovenkov, Malikov); the sequential order and citations; Yakovlev et al 1960 and Astrand & Rodahl 1970; \"A third viewpoint\" (italic), its populations and \"One can assume\". The p.370 cross-note is consistent with the literal p.370 reading. Baseline lines 117 and 124. |\n| claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality | FIX | **The \"Unclear wording\" paragraph mis-sorts the evidence.** It groups the PDF 372 negative pairing (\"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\") with PDF 373 as reading against the literal PDF 370 sentence (\"anaerobic work produces a positive effect only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work\"). It does not. The literal p.370 reading (anaerobic first) agrees with both p.372 lists: positive \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\", and negative \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\". Only p.373's stage-level sequential viewpoint (aerobic first) conflicts with it. The entry's second sentence (\"may mean 'is preceded by,' which would fit p. 373 but not ... p. 372\") already says this, so the paragraph contradicts itself. | Rewrite: \"**Unclear wording:** read literally, the p. 370 sentence (anaerobic volume helps 'only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work, thereby stimulating the aerobic reaction') fits the session-level lists on PDF p. 372, but conflicts with the stage-level sequential model on PDF p. 373, where aerobic work comes first and 'promotes the training-effect of anaerobic work'. Inference: if 'precedes' is a slip for 'is preceded by', it fits p. 373 and not p. 372. The citations (Serafimova 1974; Naumenko 1978) are the same ones p. 373 gives for its aerobic-first warning, which favours the slip reading, but the book does not resolve it.\" Checked against the PDF 370 / p.359 image: 3-4 months, up to 10 months, one month, HR 120-170, 2-3 months, five citations, about 4 months, four citations, the \"not definitive\" note. Baseline line 59. |\n| principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue | GO | None. | Checked against the images. PDF 369 / p.358: the homeostasis sentence; \"within optimal limits\"; the italic ligamentous-joint sentence (italic as claimed); the four sports; magnitude by qualification and its five citations. PDF 370 / p.359: intensity definition, the volume over time-to-maximum ratio, \"only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading\". Baseline lines 101, 142 and 153. |\n| framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 / p.367 image: three levels, \"one or more years\", \"up to 5-6 months\", \"not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels\", \"controllable\". I opened the PDF 379 / p.368 image: \"not especially complex\" and \"much smaller\" match. The entry's \"page image not opened\" note is honest; the fixer may change it to \"checked against the page image\" only after opening PDF 379. Baseline lines 64 and 255. |\n| claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification | GO | None. | Checked against the images of PDF 377-378 / p.366-367: 3 to 4 times a year; Pollock 1973, 3-4 months; twice a month; medium qualification (italic); the cause; the monocycle and bi-cycle patterns; highly qualified (italic). The Siff voice note is reasonable (Verkhoshansky is named in the third person). Baseline lines 59 and 60. |\n| case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 / p.367 image: the italic sentence (italic as claimed); Verkhoshansky 1988; the plantarflexor and thigh-extensor sentences; \"feels perfectly fit\"; the prescription. `untested` fits a single observation. Baseline lines 74 and 78. |\n| case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners | GO | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 / p.367 image: December and March-April; 800 m 4.8 vs 3.4 s; 1500 m 6.3 vs 4.6 s; the 400 m and 1200 m control runs; OBLA. The Zhdanovich link to PDF 374 is labelled Inference. |\n| case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers | GO | None. **Fig 6.24 values** (PDF 365 / p.354) were measured on the image and all agree within one gridline unit. Absolute strength: about 36/54, 50/45, 63/53. Explosive: about 54/102, 88/78, 123/96. Vertical jump: about 14/25, 15/14, 33/23. The end-state comparison (group 1 about level with or above group 3) is right. **Units problem described correctly.** The caption says \"% increase\" while the panels read kgf, kgf and cm. The caption also calls graph 3 \"height after a depth jump\" while the panel is titled \"Vertical Jump\"; the entry reports both. **Same-study link is properly labelled.** \"Inference: they may be the same study described twice or two studies; the book does not say.\" The surrounding comparison with PDF 299 (novices, weights then depth jumps, reverse, both in both stages) is factual. | Checked: the PDF 364 / p.353 design text; conclusions 1-3 on PDF 364-365; \"high jumpers of medium qualification\"; the Khodykin and Chernousov citations on PDF 364 and the Ch 5 list on PDF 299 (Tatyan/Tatian also overlaps); baseline line 180. |\n| case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers | FIX | **Three Fig 6.25 readings are about a unit high.** Measured on the 300 dpi crop of PDF 365 at about 11.2 px per percentage point: absolute strength GP1 about 7.0, GP2 about 18.1, GP3 about 16.7; speed GP1 about 18.1, GP2 about 11.2, GP3 about 13.6; power GP1 about 6.1, GP2 about 14.2, GP3 about 20.1. The entry gives power GP3 \"about 21\" (the bar ends exactly on the 20 gridline), power GP2 \"about 15\", and \"18-19\" for absolute GP2 and speed GP1. | In Figure 6.25 values, the Example and When NOT: power \"GP2 about 14, GP3 about 20\"; absolute strength \"GP2 about 18\"; speed \"GP1 about 18\". The Example becomes \"power rose about 20% in the 30-90% group against about 14% (70-90%) and about 6% (30-50%), while speed rose most, about 18%, with 30-50% loads\". The When NOT pair becomes \"about 17% vs about 18%\". Checked: 7 weeks; the 30-50%, 70-90% and 30-90% groups; \"metabolic cost\"; the caption (\"highly qualified jumpers\", \"Nedobyvailov\"); \"Group 1, graph 2\", all at PDF 365. Baseline line 76 quote and tag. |\n| framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect | GO | None. | Checked against the images of PDF 364-366 / p.353-355: \"Acute TE > Delayed TE > Cumulative TE\" with its 3 citations; the printed reversed parenthesis \"(i.e. the sum of the parts is greater than the whole)\" (a good catch, correctly labelled); partial and cumulative TE (italic); the four factors (Verkhoshansky 1970); the two criteria; \"(see 6.8)\". Fig 6.26 at PDF 366: short-term and long-term effects each carry a positive/negative box, and transient and accumulated each carry one. \"Somewhat tedious\". Baseline line 182. |\n| heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation | FIX | (1) **Unlabelled gloss** under Why it works: \"Removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal.\" The authors say premature intervention \"can hinder the body's supercompensatory ability\" (PDF 368); the \"signal\" framing is the writer's. (2) **Unlabelled judgment** under When NOT: \"the Kuchnyev result confounds restoration with a 15% rise in volume.\" The source says \"At the same time, the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%\" (PDF 368 / p.357) and does not say which group's volume rose. (3) **Format:** no blank line before `**Example**`. | (1) Prefix: \"Inference: removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal.\" (2) Replace with: \"The text says training volume rose by an average of 15% but not whether in both groups or only the restoration group; Inference: if only the restoration group, the result confounds restoration with extra volume.\" (3) Insert the blank line. Checked against the PDF 368 image: 24.2/18.9% vs 7.7/4.9%, 15%, Kuchnyev 1977, Platonov 1980, 2-4% and 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979), Yakovlev 1971, Siff & Yessis 1992, Yevgenyev et al 1975. Baseline lines 74, 151 and 153. |\n| claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks | GO | None. | Checked against the images of PDF 367-368 / p.356-357: the italic \"It is undoubtedly important...\" sentence (italic as claimed); \"more intense stimulus\"; 1:45:0 (printed that way); 10.6-10.7 s; at least 9 m for three jumps; 33-34 m for ten; Zhdanovich 1980; Verkhoshansky & Sirenko 1982; \"archaic viewpoint\". Baseline lines 122 and 123. |\n\n**Tally: GO 10, FIX 9, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Contested entries: is each a real contradiction?\n\n1. **Technique heuristic (PDF 375 vs 536).** Yes, literally. The Addendum sentence reverses Ch 6, but its own example agrees with Ch 6, and it is a separate translation (Yessis) of Verkhoshansky's paper. Keep `contested` and record both pages; the translation-slip reading stays labelled Inference.\n2. **Within-session order (PDF 371 vs 288, 299, 300, 301).** Yes, but not where the entry puts it. PDF 371 on its own is a hedge. The real conflict sits between chapters:\n   - PDF 288: resistance before plyometrics within a session.\n   - PDF 299: no difference for novices.\n   - PDF 300: strength then speed-strength in one session is least effective (novices and average qualification).\n   - PDF 371: keep barbell then jumps.\n\n   PDF 301 is stage-level and should leave this entry's contested reason.\n3. **Concentrated vs distributed (PDF 371 vs Issurin 2016).** Yes, as a disagreement about evidence scope. The Issurin brief is stated accurately and targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's own A-T-R.\n\n## Missing (important ideas in PDF 363-379 that no entry captures)\n\n1. **Do not use highly stimulating means prematurely** (PDF 368-369 / p.357-358, \"Training Potential of the Loading\", Fig 6.28). Training potential is relative to current fitness and falls as special work capacity rises. Using highly stimulating means early, \"because the body is functionally unprepared for them\", causes \"excessive intensification of training and disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\". The 5.4.4 entry `heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades` covers replacing means, but not this warning. Add it there as a Ch 6 cross-reference, or write a short heuristic.\n2. **SPP means must exceed competition conditions for highly qualified athletes** (PDF 367 / p.356). To preserve training potential, means must \"not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions, with regard to maximum force, time of maximum force development and the power of the metabolic processes\". From this the authors derive the case for strength work. They also say external similarity is \"often overemphasized\" against the regime of muscular work and energy mechanism (squats justified). This may be covered conceptually by `principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed` (4.3.3) and the dynamic-correspondence entries. The new angle is the tie to training potential and qualification. A Related or cross-reference addition may be enough.\n3. **6.8.3: general qualities do not assemble into special preparedness** (PDF 363 / p.352). \"The emergence of specificity from an array of non-specific fitness qualities ... is not supported\". Muscle endurance from supplementary running will not directly help a rower or cyclist. This is probably covered conceptually by the Ch 1.3 specificity entries, but no Ch 6 entry carries the 6.8.3 statement. Confirm coverage or add a line to the nearest specificity entry.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions (for the 6b and 6d fixers; not required for this batch)\n\n- 6d `heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block` should link `heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window`.\n- 6b `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation` and 6d `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes` should link `claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes`.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired fixes:\n1. **principle LDTE:** Fig 6.30 block about month 1.6 to 4.5; record all three \"4-12 weeks\" referents (PDF 305, 375, 536), noting that T2 ≈ T1 reconciles 375 and 536.\n2. **technique heuristic:** Addendum voice is \"Verkhoshansky's (9.1, trans. Yessis)\", not \"the authors\"; add the link to the 6d technique-work heuristic.\n3. **altitude claim:** reword and label the Susman \"not as a documented LDTE\" gloss.\n4. **concentrated claim:** add links to the 6b distributed-vs-concentrated framework and the 6d complex-training claim; \"partly italicised\".\n5. **within-session heuristic:** rewrite the contested reason. PDF 301 is stage-level (PDF 300 heading); add the PDF 300 novice/average \"least effective ... strength followed by speed-strength during the same session\"; call p.371 a hedge.\n6. **sequential heuristic:** label two Inferences; add the PDF 300-301 stage-level rule.\n7. **time-limits claim:** fix the mis-sorted p.372 evidence in \"Unclear wording\".\n8. **Nedobyvailov:** Fig 6.25 power GP3 about 20, GP2 about 14; absolute GP2 about 18; speed GP1 about 18.\n9. **restoration heuristic:** label two glosses as Inference; add the blank line before Example.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"52375cea-416b-4d75-b817-f2fa13727c32","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d8030315-57d7-45c5-aee8-6017b51ab1b6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpKukWU9vUk2f8Yi7g9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U13aML36bUkubb9GeX9moq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md","description":"Read fix log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":787,"cache_read_input_tokens":55752,"output_tokens":303,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":77},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":787},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":303,"cache_read_input_tokens":55752,"cache_creation_input_tokens":787,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":787,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01U13aML36bUkubb9GeX9moq":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md","description":"Read fix log"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffpKuSehcasgWkbc4ETL","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1878ee0d-700a-4e46-8174-6cb2db61e600","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:40.058Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1878ee0d-700a-4e46-8174-6cb2db61e600","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U13aML36bUkubb9GeX9moq","type":"tool_result","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 6.8.3-6.11 (Ch6c), 19 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 9 FIX rows, 9 numbered fixes, 3 missing ideas, reciprocal-link suggestions).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 288, 299-301, 305, 363, 365, 367-377, 514, 515 and 536 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f6c_text.txt`). I opened these page images (150 dpi renders in `staging/_renders/f6c_p<N>.png`): PDF 288, 299, 300, 301 (top crop), 305, 363, 367, 368, 369, 370, 371, 372, 373, 377, 515 (top crop) and 536. For PDF 365 and 375 I opened only the 300 dpi figure crops (`f6c_p365_fig625.png` for Fig 6.25, `f6c_p375_top.png` for Figs 6.30-6.31). The 375 crop stops above point 5, so the 375 \"4-12 weeks\" and T2/T1 text was checked from the text layer. Not opened as images: PDF 374 and 376 (the sentences cited from them are unchanged text) and the PDF 35/38 section headings (text layer only, labelled so in the entry). Full-page renders of 365, 374 and 375 exist but were not opened. Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt`. I edited the 9 FIX entries plus 2 GO entries (`principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`, `claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks`) to place the missing ideas. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (19 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 6b or 6d file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md | **Fix 1.** (1) Fig 6.30 block now \"from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5 (read from a 300 dpi render of the page image)\". (2) When NOT adds the three 4-12 week referents: PDF 305 / p.294 (unidirectional training effective, checked against the page image), PDF 375 (LDTE duration), and \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515)\", quoting \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" (PDF 536 / p.525, checked against the page image). It says T2 about equal to T1, stated on both 375 and 536, reconciles those two, and PDF 305 is a separate quantity. \"Keep the locator with the number.\" |\n| heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md | **Fix 2.** (1) \"The Addendum restates\" is now \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, \"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky, Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\"; PDF p. 515 / printed p. 504, checked against the page image) restates\". The Inference now reads: \"its own example fits Chapter 6, and the Addendum is a separate translation of Verkhoshansky's text, which makes a translation slip plausible. The book prints both.\" The example quote in When NOT now quotes the page's own words (\"insignificant increase ... (by only a few percent)\") rather than \"triple jumpers whose ... volume rose\". (2) The Example is now \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\". It says \"the text says its Figure 8 shows\" (Figure 8 itself is not on PDF 536 and was not opened) and quotes \"in triple jumpers\" and records that the page switches to the singular (\"as believed by the athlete\"; \"the athlete lost his chance to be selected ... for a place on the national team\"). (3) Related adds [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]] (file confirmed present before linking). `contested` kept. |\n| claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training.md | **Fix 3.** The Susman gloss is replaced with the review's wording: \"The authors do not call this an LDTE; they give it after stating that disturbance of homeostasis 'can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators' (PDF p. 377). Inference: it is offered as a sea-level example of the dip-and-rebound pattern in non-aerobic measures, not as a documented LDTE in aerobic productivity.\" |\n| claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md | **Fix 4.** (1) \"Their conclusion, partly italicised in the original (only 'concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement' is in italics):\". (2) Related adds [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]] and [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] (both confirmed present at 13:51). (3) Optional applied: \"Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names.\" `contested` kept. |\n| heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast.md | **Fix 5.** The \"Contested inside the book\" paragraph is rewritten. PDF 371 is called \"a hedge, not a contradiction\". The cross-chapter conflict is laid out with locators, each checked against the page image: PDF 288 (resistance before plyometrics \"in individual sessions\"), PDF 299 (novices, \"no statistically significant difference\"), and PDF 300 (\"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\"). The opposition to p.371 is labelled Inference (see Disagreements 1). The PDF 301 bullets are moved out as stage-level, quoting the PDF 300 heading, with a pointer to the sequential heuristic. The PDF 372 across-stage sentence and the Issurin and endurance sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive.md | **Fix 6.** (1) \"; Inference: so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum\" and \"Inference: Bondarchuk agrees on the direction\". (2) After the Fig 5.18 sentence, the stage-level rule from PDF 300-301 is added, checked against the page images, with the scope \"the list follows the research with novices and athletes of average qualification\" (unquoted: on PDF 300 that phrase introduces the three bulleted conclusions, and the 301 list follows them). The Inference adds that \"Fig 5.13 on PDF p. 300 labels the first-stage means 'Jumps'\", so \"speed-strength\" there means jumping exercises, and the conflict turns on what the first stage contains. |\n| claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality.md | **Fix 7.** \"Unclear wording\" is rewritten. Read literally, p.370 fits both p.372 session lists (quoted) but conflicts with the p.373 stage-level aerobic-first model. \"Inference: if 'precedes' is a slip for 'is preceded by,' it fits p. 373 and not p. 372.\" Citations: \"Both of p. 370's citations (Serafimova 1974; Naumenko 1978) appear among the four p. 373 gives for its aerobic-first warning (with Vavilov 1977 and Morzhevikov 1980)\". |\n| case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers.md | **Fix 8.** Fig 6.25 values are now: absolute strength GP1 about 7, GP2 about 18, GP3 about 17; speed GP1 about 18, GP2 about 11, GP3 about 14; power GP1 about 6, GP2 about 14, GP3 about 20 (\"the GP3 power bar ends on the 20 gridline\"). The When NOT pair is now \"about 17% ... about 18%\". The Example reads \"power rose about 20% ... against about 14% (70-90%) and about 6% (30-50%), while speed rose most, about 18%\". |\n| heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation.md | **Fix 9.** (1) \"Inference: removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal.\" (2) When NOT: \"The reported gains carry no sample sizes or statistics. The text says training volume rose 'by an average of 15%' but not whether in both groups or only the restoration group (PDF p. 368); Inference: if only the restoration group, the result confounds restoration with extra volume.\" (3) A blank line is added before **Example**. (4) Beyond the review: the Example's \"while also handling about 15% more volume\" gave the volume rise to the restoration group too. It now quotes the sentence and adds \"without saying in which group\". |\n| principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue.md | **Missing idea 1.** After the PDF 370 intensification sentence, a short addition quotes the training-potential passage: \"The training potential of the means employed decreases as the special work capacity increases\" (Fig 6.28), and \"it is inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely, because the body is functionally unprepared for them ... disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\" (PDF pp. 368-369, checked against the page images). Related annotates the existing 5.4.4 link and adds [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]. |\n| claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks.md | **Missing ideas 2 and 3.** A new paragraph says \"The passage follows directly from the authors' rule for choosing SPP means\". It quotes PDF 367 (external similarity \"often overemphasized\"; squats \"justified\"; means that \"not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions ... On this basis ... the strength component of the movement\") and PDF 363, 6.8.3 (\"cannot simply be used in a very general way\"; running endurance and the rower or cyclist; \"The emergence of specificity from an array of non-specific fitness qualities ... is not supported\"). Both pages were checked against the page image. Related adds the 4.3.3 effort principle, the dynamic-correspondence heuristic and the specificity entry. The link notes that PDF 363's \"discussed in 1.3\" points to specificity, which is section 1.4 in this edition (PDF p. 38, text layer). That mismatch is labelled Inference. |\n\n## Fig readings (my own, from 300 dpi renders)\n\n- **Fig 6.30 (PDF 375 / p.364).** On the render, the month-1 tick is at about x 697 and month 2 at about 800; months 4 and 5 are at about 978 and 1065. The shaded block's left edge is at about 755, so about month 1.6. Its right edge is at about 1025, so about month 4.5. On the y axis, 4 is at y 457 and 6 at y 381, about 38 px per percentage point. Vertical jump peaks at about 7 near month 3.7 and ends at about 4.5-4.6 at month 8. Stride length peaks at about 11.5 near month 7 and ends at about 11. Ten jumps peaks at about 9.7 and ends at about 9.5. This agrees with the review. The entry keeps \"about 11\" and \"about 9-9.5\".\n- **Fig 6.25 (PDF 365 / p.354).** On the y axis, 0 is at about y 462-465, 10 at about 351-354 and 20 at about 249-255, about 10.5 px per point. Absolute strength: GP1 about 7.0, GP2 about 18.0, GP3 about 16.7. Speed: GP1 about 18.0, GP2 about 11.2, GP3 about 13.6. Power: GP1 about 6.2, GP2 about 14.2, GP3 about 20.0 (on the gridline). This agrees with the review. The caption spells the name \"Nedobyvailov\".\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Do not use highly stimulating means prematurely (PDF 368-369) | Folded into 6c `principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue`. Its title already says \"Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\", and Fig 6.28 sits on the same page as its 6.9.4 opening. The 5.4.4 `heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades` was already in Related. It covers replacing means but not the premature-use warning, so that link is now annotated. |\n| 2 | SPP means must exceed competition conditions for highly qualified athletes (PDF 367) | Folded into 6c `claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks`. The PDF 367 passage leads straight into that entry's strength-for-endurance argument (\"On this basis ... strength component\"). The new angle, tying the rule to training potential and qualification, is not in 4.3.3. A Related link to [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]] (4.3.3) and the dynamic-correspondence heuristic was added. |\n| 3 | 6.8.3: general qualities do not assemble into SPP (PDF 363) | Folded into the same middle-distance entry, as one sentence group. No Ch 1 entry carries the 6.8.3 statement. `principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions` (1.4) covers narrow transfer, and the 4.4 `principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles` (already in Related) holds the skier version of the running example. A link to the 1.4 entry was added. The review's \"Ch1.3\" is section 1.4 here: 1.3 is \"A Philosophy of Physical Training\" (PDF 35), and 1.4 is \"Specificity in Training\" (PDF 38). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt` is unchanged (19 lines).\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Fix 5: the PDF 300 vs PDF 371 opposition is labelled Inference.** Neither page says barbell work is \"strength\" and jumping is \"speed-strength\". The review's rewrite stated the opposition as fact. The entry now says \"Inference: reading barbell work as 'strength' and jumping as 'speed-strength' (Fig 5.13 on the same page labels the jumping means 'Jumps'), this opposes p. 371's 'preserve the above order'\". Every other part of the review's rewrite held up on the page images.\n2. **Fix 7: \"the same ones\" is loosened.** The review says the p.370 citations \"are the same ones p. 373 gives\". PDF 373 gives four (Naumenko 1978; Vavilov 1977; Serafimova 1974; Morzhevikov 1980), and p.370 gives two of them. The entry says \"appear among the four\".\n3. **Fix 2, optional singular wording.** The review suggests \"the athlete lost both\". PDF 536 introduces Figure 8 \"in triple jumpers\" and then switches to \"the athlete\" and \"his chance\". The Example records both rather than picking one.\n4. **Optional items not applied.** I did not add the PDF 438 / \"Fig 1.7\" note to the within-session entry, because I did not open PDF 438, and it would have been an unverified locator. I did not change the \"page image not opened\" note in the GO entry `framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state`, because it was outside the flagged files.\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: the Fig 6.30 block edges, the Fig 6.25 values, the PDF 515 title block, the PDF 536 reversal and its example, the PDF 300 heading that makes the PDF 301 bullets stage-level, the partial italics on PDF 371, Susman's placement on PDF 377, the PDF 368 15% sentence, and the PDF 305 4-12 weeks.\n\n## Follow-up for the coordinator\n\n- **Addendum voice (PDF 514-590) in other Supertraining entries.** I grepped the staging book folder, the whole staging library and the vault library for \"PDF p(p). 5[1-9][0-9]\", \"536\" and \"Addendum\". **No other entry attaches \"the authors\" to an Addendum statement.** One near miss: `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md` is in both staging and the vault (the copies are identical). It cites PDF 536 as \"the Addendum says ...\" and labels it \"text layer\". The voice there is unlabelled rather than wrong. Recommend \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515)\" in both copies, and opening the page image. `_book.md` row 9 lists the Addendum, but it is a map, not an entry.\n- **Reciprocal links (not edited by me):** 6d `heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block` should link [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]. 6b `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation` and 6d `claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes` should link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]].\n- **6b link target timing.** `framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md` existed at 13:51 when I linked it (mtime 13:40, so the 6b fixer was mid-edit). If the 6b fixer renames it, the link in the concentrated claim must follow.\n- **New links into non-6c files** (all confirmed present): the 4.3.3 effort principle, the 5.1 dynamic-correspondence heuristic, the 1.4 specificity entry, and the conjugate-sequence framework.\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one or more lines per fixed file):\n\n```\nprinciple--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md:25:from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\nprinciple--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md:33:The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things\nprinciple--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum.md:33:in Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515)\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:27:Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1,\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:29:Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:29:the text says its Figure 8\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:27:which makes a translation slip plausible\nheuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window.md:31:[[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]]\nclaim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training.md:19:The authors do not call this an LDTE\nclaim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training.md:19:Inference: it is offered as a sea-level example\nclaim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md:16:partly italicised in the original\nclaim--concentra