{"parentUuid":"120781c4-4b2a-469c-8963-718e0f26e828","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpLNZJgeKcAHgUEkAHy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xt2NQoQfS7d71H43vafNKM","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read first three fixed entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2880,"cache_read_input_tokens":76766,"output_tokens":261,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":29},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2880},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":261,"cache_read_input_tokens":76766,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2880,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2880,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Xt2NQoQfS7d71H43vafNKM":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read first three fixed entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpLNJBWzLjLQPhB5wj2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"095c4ecb-7fc3-4f6c-b6f2-1610dcd11eea","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:45.719Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"095c4ecb-7fc3-4f6c-b6f2-1610dcd11eea","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xt2NQoQfS7d71H43vafNKM","type":"tool_result","content":"##### principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: principle\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.10\"\n     7\ttopic: long-term delayed training effect\n     8\tcategory: [delayed-training-effect, concentrated-loading, volume, periodization, speed-strength]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Long-Term Delayed Training Effect: Volume Well Above the Optimum Depresses Speed-Strength, Then a Timed Volume Cut Lets It Rise Above Baseline\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors report \"a steady decrease in speed-strength preparedness during individual training stages of different length (from 5-12 weeks)\" caused by \"voluminous strength loading,\" after which \"speed-strength returns to its initial level and significantly exceeds it\" (Nikitin 1977; Mironenko 1979; Antonova 1982; Levchenko 1982). Because this \"does not agree with established ideas which stipulate a continuous rise in special physical preparedness\" and \"is not accompanied by marked signs of overtraining,\" it became \"a subject of special study\" (PDF p. 374 / printed p. 363, checked against the page image). They then state a dose-response rule: \"The volume of the training load has a definite optimum level for each athlete, above or below which the body reacts with diminished response.\" \"If the volume of loading is lower than the optimum level, special work-capacity decreases after some initial rise. If the volume of loading significantly exceeds this level, a steady decrease in special work-capacity occurs which, however, rises intensely during the carefully-timed reduction in the volume. This intense rise in special work-capacity is the phenomenon known as the long-term delayed training effect (LDTE)\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\tTheir characterisation (Fig 6.31, PDF pp. 374-375 / printed pp. 363-364, checked against the page images), points 1, 2 and 4:\n    19\t1. \"Concentration of strength loading is the basic condition for the beginning of the LDTE.\"\n    20\t2. \"The lower the speed-strength levels fall (within an optimal range) during the concentration of strength loading stage, the higher they rise in the subsequent LDTE phase (graphs of F1 and F2). Excessive concentration of strength loading leads to a more significant drop in speed-strength and disruption of adaptation (graph F3).\" In the figure, F3 dips lowest and only creeps back toward the baseline by the end of T2; F1 and F2 end above it.\n    21\t4. \"A subsequent moderate volume of general developmental work, combined with special work of gradually increasing intensity contributes to eliciting the LDTE.\"\n    22\t\n    23\tPoints 3 (non-intensive means) and 5 (T2 about equal to T1, observed 4-12 weeks, individualised) are already quoted in [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]] and [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]].\n    24\t\n    25\t**Figures.** Fig 6.29 (sprinters, PDF p. 374, read from the image): a shaded rectangle marks the loading period from month 0 to about month 2.5; three curves (starting, explosive and absolute strength, the dips not labelled separately) fall to about 85-96% of initial, recross 100% at about month 2, and at month 4 sit at about 125% (starting), about 119% (explosive) and about 112% (absolute). Fig 6.30 (middle-distance runners, Zhdanovich 1980, PDF p. 375): during a shaded block from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5 (read from a 300 dpi render of the page image), vertical jump rises to about 7% and then falls to about 4.5% by month 8; stride length and ten standing long jumps keep rising after the block, to about 11% and about 9-9.5%. The text says vertical jump falls in the competition stage and offers a hedged reason: \"One may assume that the reason for this phenomenon is the increase in volume of intense distance work\" (PDF p. 374). LDTE after concentrated strength loading is also reported in swimming (Rudokene 1981) and ice-skating (Kabachkova et al 1982).\n    26\t\n    27\tTwo further points: athletes \"invariably do not allow or do not have sufficient time for recuperation after extensive strength loading,\" which minimises the LDTE and forces the body \"to unjustifiably deplete its current adaptation energy and sometimes draw upon its deep adaptation energy\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). And \"one must differentiate between maintenance of the training effect and the LDTE of loading\": maintenance is \"the retention of functional adaptation for a given period after training has ceased\"; the LDTE is \"the relatively prolonged increase in the functional indicators as a result of specially organised, concentrated volumes of loading\" (PDF p. 377 / printed p. 366, checked against the page image).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Why it works** — The authors say the volume-performance link is \"characteristically simple\" but give no physiological mechanism for the delayed rise in 6.10's strength material; they note that the \"physiological conditions, mechanisms and characteristics of the LDTE until recently have not been a subject of much research\" (PDF p. 374). Inference: the library holds two readings of the same curve shape, supercompensation or adaptive reconstruction ([[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]]) and fitness-fatigue, where fatigue masks fitness until volume drops ([[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]); the book does not settle between them here.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated strength block for an advanced athlete when the aim is a later rise in speed-strength, accepting a planned dip during the block. Inference: a dip in speed-strength during such a block is the expected path, not by itself a signal to stop. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the LDTE rests on one load-design move, the timed volume cut, so it fits that practice. Eric's baseline: \"The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds\" (line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: during a deliberate block, an mRSI drop below an athlete's usual band would be expected; the authors' own rule separates a dip \"within an optimal range\" from the F3 case, but gives no threshold for telling them apart.\n    32\t\n    33\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no numbers for \"optimum,\" \"within an optimal range\" or \"excessive,\" and says the effect is individual (PDF p. 375). Figs 6.29-6.31 carry no sample sizes or statistics; Fig 6.31 is a \"general scheme,\" not data. The 5-12 weeks on PDF p. 374 is the length of the loading stages; the 4-12 weeks on PDF p. 375 is how long the LDTE lasts. They are different quantities. The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things: how long unidirectional training stays effective (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image), how long the LDTE lasts (PDF p. 375), and, in Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515), the length of the concentrated stages themselves: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image). Both PDF p. 375 and the Addendum (PDF p. 536) say the LDTE lasts about as long as the loading stage (T2 about equal to T1), which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity. Keep the locator with the number. Inference: in-season, when performance is needed every week, a planned multi-week dip conflicts with Eric's baseline that \"in football you want to peak every Saturday\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)); this is an off-season tool. The book's claim that the dip comes without \"marked signs of overtraining\" sits beside Bompa's framing of concentrated loading as planned overreaching ([[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]); Inference: that is a difference of labels, not of the observed curve.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Example** — The authors' own: in sprinters (Fig 6.29), about 2.5 months of concentrated strength loading drove starting, explosive and absolute strength to about 85-96% of initial; by month 4 they stood at about 112-125% (values read from the image, PDF p. 374).\n    36\t\n    37\t**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training]], [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]] (maintenance, the other half of the 6.10 distinction), [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]\n##### heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.10\"\n     7\ttopic: technique timing after strength blocks\n     8\tcategory: [delayed-training-effect, technique, competition-period, concentrated-loading, sequencing]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Put Technical Training in the Delayed-Effect Window, and Keep Competition-Period Strength Work Short, Intense and Small\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Three linked rules from 6.10:\n    17\t1. **Strength first, technique in the rebound.** \"high-volume strength loads are detrimental to one's current level of speed-strength fitness and, consequently, to technical preparation. To avoid this, it is advisable in training high level athletes to structure the programme so that strength work precedes and does not interfere with technical training. Technical training should take place in the presence of the delayed training effect of the concentrated volume of strength work, i.e. when there is recovery of work capacity and an increase in the level of speed-strength.\" Technique then \"rapidly adjusts to the athlete's higher level of specialised work capacity and there is much less likelihood of reinforcing errors\" from the \"lowered functional state, caused by high-volume loads\" (PDF p. 376 / printed p. 365, checked against the page image).\n    18\t2. **Do not add volume while the effect emerges.** \"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image).\n    19\t3. **Competition period.** \"One should be especially cautious with strength loading during the competition period. At this time, intensive and short-term strength work can be used in a small volume to tone the neuromuscular system during competition preparation, as well as to maintain the achieved level of speed-strength preparedness if the length of the competition period exceeds the duration of the LDTE of strength loading\" (PDF pp. 375-376 / printed pp. 364-365).\n    20\t\n    21\tThe authors also state that \"the competitive action executed with maximal physical exertion represents the most specific of all the special training means,\" to be viewed \"simultaneously as a highly intense means of strength training,\" so a strength programme should rise in intensity and be \"combined with technique training so as to peak concurrently with the competitive act\" (PDF p. 376). They give \"the dominant role\" in the strength-technique interaction to \"special physical preparation, both in the yearly cycle and in long-term training\" (same page).\n    22\t\n    23\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: a strength-depressed state reinforces technical errors, while a higher work capacity lets the athlete \"concentrate his effort more effectively, increase range of movement, decrease the time of execution of movements, improve his stability, improve the strength of muscular control over all movements and increase his resistance to fatigue\" (PDF p. 376). For rule 2 they report that during emergence \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work,\" seen as \"slowed or even decreased growth of speed-strength\" (PDF p. 375).\n    24\t\n    25\t**When to apply** — After a concentrated strength block, schedule the heaviest technical emphasis for the weeks when speed-strength is coming back up, and hold volume flat through that period. In a long competition period, keep a small dose of intense, brief strength work. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume'\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: rule 3 says the same thing for the competition period. Eric's baseline: the in-season week has \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: that sits with rule 3's \"small volume\" and \"short-term\" strength work; the authors give no day-level timing.\n    26\t\n    27\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, \"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky, Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\"; PDF p. 515 / printed p. 504, checked against the page image) restates the emergence-period rule the other way round: \"In the period of LDTE realization, volume concentrated loads are handled by the body relatively easily and without harm to the body, but intense loads negatively react to the work volume\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image). Its own example then agrees with Chapter 6: an \"insignificant increase\" in specialised strength volume \"(by only a few percent)\" to \"maintain\" the achieved level \"led to a fast decrease in speed-strength and sports results\" (same page, checked against the page image). Inference: the Addendum sentence may be a translation slip: its own example fits Chapter 6, and the Addendum is a separate translation of Verkhoshansky's text, which makes a translation slip plausible. The book prints both. The authors give no number for \"small volume\" or for how long technical emphasis should wait. Inference: for skills that need daily practice (team-sport play), technique cannot simply wait for the strength block to end; the rule is written for \"high level athletes\" in events where the competitive action can be isolated, and they mention the conjugate system \"successively introduces different modes of loading ... rather than separating them in time\" (PDF p. 376), so the separation is a matter of emphasis.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Example** — Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image): the text says its Figure 8 shows that \"in triple jumpers,\" concentrated specialised strength loads in March-April were followed by a rise in speed-strength and results in May-June; raising strength volume \"by only a few percent\" to hold the gain then brought \"a fast decrease in speed-strength and sports results.\" The page switches from plural to singular here: the volume rise was \"as believed by the athlete,\" and \"the athlete lost his chance to be selected in the qualifying competitions for a place on the national team.\" Inference: an off-season strength block ending in March would hand over to technical and speed emphasis in April with strength volume held, not raised.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--realization-mesocycle-taper-prescription]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]] (6.12.2, the same strength-then-technique question from a later passage)\n##### claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.10\"\n     7\ttopic: delayed effect in endurance training\n     8\tcategory: [delayed-training-effect, endurance, altitude, monitoring, swimming]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# For Aerobic and Anaerobic Productivity, a Long-Term Delayed Effect Has Been Documented Only After Altitude Training\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under \"The Delayed Training Effect and Long Duration Work,\" the authors state: \"With respect to aerobic and anaerobic productivity, there is no experimental data that clearly documents occurrence of the LDTE phenomenon in cyclic distance training. The exceptions are cases of intense training under the lowered oxygen conditions of high altitude\" (PDF p. 376 / printed p. 365, checked against the page image). Their evidence:\n    17\t- **Swimmers (Vaitsekhovsky et al 1974).** \"significant increases\" in \"maximum aerobic capacity, the total volume of work which can be executed at the critical level of power, VO2 max and the onset of blood lactate accumulation.\" \"These changes persisted for more than 4 weeks after 3 weeks of training at an altitude of 1700m above sea level, with a peak response taking place about 1 week after the altitude training. All of the athletes in the study improved their results immediately after training at altitude.\"\n    18\t- **Skaters (Ivanov 1977).** A 4-minute cycle-ergometer test weekly for 3 weeks at 2250 m, then for 3 weeks at sea level. At altitude, mechanical efficiency fell and oxygen consumption, energy expenditure and lactate rose; back at sea level, \"the functional efficiency of the work exceeded the pre-experimental levels as a result of more economical metabolism and a significantly diminished amount of anaerobically produced ATP\" (same page).\n    19\t- **Not an altitude case: swimmers under large loads (Susman 1978; no altitude is mentioned).** The authors do not call this an LDTE; they give it after stating that disturbance of homeostasis \"can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" (PDF p. 377). Inference: it is offered as a sea-level example of the dip-and-rebound pattern in non-aerobic measures, not as a documented LDTE in aerobic productivity. Large loads slowed \"complex motor reactions,\" reduced precision of movement patterns and of \"tracking a moving object,\" and lowered work capacity, ECG-assessed cardiovascular state, muscle tonus and adrenal-cortex hormone levels. After loads were reduced and intensity altered, these indicators rose \"as the supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction) phase emerged\" (PDF p. 377 / printed p. 366, checked against the page image).\n    20\t\n    21\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for the altitude cases: hypobaric conditions gave \"a very demanding training influence which provoked persistent disturbance of the body's homeostasis and subsequent activation of its adaptive processes\" (PDF p. 376). For why the LDTE is not seen at sea level, they hedge: if it exists there, the disturbance \"will occur at the level of more subtle physiological systems\" not detectable by \"methods used for assessing the more gross aerobic and anaerobic processes,\" and \"adaptation apparently occurs at deeper cellular and molecular levels (e.g. in the mitochondria and endocrine system)\" (Yakovlev 1957, 1974; Viru 1969, 1981; Finogenov 1979), while \"disturbance of homeostasis can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" of the central nervous and neuromuscular systems (PDF pp. 376-377). This is a stated hypothesis, not a demonstrated mechanism.\n    22\t\n    23\t**When to apply** — When planning or interpreting altitude camps: the book's one quantified case puts the peak about 1 week after a 3-week stay, with changes lasting more than 4 weeks. When judging a heavy endurance block: the authors point to psycho-physiological and neuromuscular indicators as the place a hidden disturbance may show. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a neuromuscular marker of that kind is the sort of indicator the authors suggest, though they name reaction, precision and tracking tasks, not jump tests.\n    24\t\n    25\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no sample sizes, controls or statistics for any of the three studies, and the skater study has no non-altitude comparison group in the text. The authors say plainly that the LDTE in sea-level distance training is not documented; do not carry the strength-block LDTE timings ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]) over to aerobic work. The LDTE they do report in cyclic sports (middle-distance running, swimming, skating) follows concentrated strength loading and shows in strength-endurance measures, not in aerobic productivity (PDF p. 374). Inference: altitude camps are rare in American collegiate football, so this entry matters mainly for endurance or Olympic-sport programmes.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Example** — The authors' own: swimmers after 3 weeks at 1700 m improved immediately, peaked about 1 week after returning, and kept the gains for more than 4 weeks (Vaitsekhovsky et al 1974; PDF p. 376 / printed p. 365).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6134cbb3-e676-46a2-b1f3-be11649094c8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:45.963Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: principle\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.10\"\n     7\ttopic: long-term delayed training effect\n     8\tcategory: [delayed-training-effect, concentrated-loading, volume, periodization, speed-strength]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Long-Term Delayed Training Effect: Volume Well Above the Optimum Depresses Speed-Strength, Then a Timed Volume Cut Lets It Rise Above Baseline\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors report \"a steady decrease in speed-strength preparedness during individual training stages of different length (from 5-12 weeks)\" caused by \"voluminous strength loading,\" after which \"speed-strength returns to its initial level and significantly exceeds it\" (Nikitin 1977; Mironenko 1979; Antonova 1982; Levchenko 1982). Because this \"does not agree with established ideas which stipulate a continuous rise in special physical preparedness\" and \"is not accompanied by marked signs of overtraining,\" it became \"a subject of special study\" (PDF p. 374 / printed p. 363, checked against the page image). They then state a dose-response rule: \"The volume of the training load has a definite optimum level for each athlete, above or below which the body reacts with diminished response.\" \"If the volume of loading is lower than the optimum level, special work-capacity decreases after some initial rise. If the volume of loading significantly exceeds this level, a steady decrease in special work-capacity occurs which, however, rises intensely during the carefully-timed reduction in the volume. This intense rise in special work-capacity is the phenomenon known as the long-term delayed training effect (LDTE)\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\tTheir characterisation (Fig 6.31, PDF pp. 374-375 / printed pp. 363-364, checked against the page images), points 1, 2 and 4:\n    19\t1. \"Concentration of strength loading is the basic condition for the beginning of the LDTE.\"\n    20\t2. \"The lower the speed-strength levels fall (within an optimal range) during the concentration of strength loading stage, the higher they rise in the subsequent LDTE phase (graphs of F1 and F2). Excessive concentration of strength loading leads to a more significant drop in speed-strength and disruption of adaptation (graph F3).\" In the figure, F3 dips lowest and only creeps back toward the baseline by the end of T2; F1 and F2 end above it.\n    21\t4. \"A subsequent moderate volume of general developmental work, combined with special work of gradually increasing intensity contributes to eliciting the LDTE.\"\n    22\t\n    23\tPoints 3 (non-intensive means) and 5 (T2 about equal to T1, observed 4-12 weeks, individualised) are already quoted in [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]] and [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]].\n    24\t\n    25\t**Figures.** Fig 6.29 (sprinters, PDF p. 374, read from the image): a shaded rectangle marks the loading period from month 0 to about month 2.5; three curves (starting, explosive and absolute strength, the dips not labelled separately) fall to about 85-96% of initial, recross 100% at about month 2, and at month 4 sit at about 125% (starting), about 119% (explosive) and about 112% (absolute). Fig 6.30 (middle-distance runners, Zhdanovich 1980, PDF p. 375): during a shaded block from about month 1.6 to about month 4.5 (read from a 300 dpi render of the page image), vertical jump rises to about 7% and then falls to about 4.5% by month 8; stride length and ten standing long jumps keep rising after the block, to about 11% and about 9-9.5%. The text says vertical jump falls in the competition stage and offers a hedged reason: \"One may assume that the reason for this phenomenon is the increase in volume of intense distance work\" (PDF p. 374). LDTE after concentrated strength loading is also reported in swimming (Rudokene 1981) and ice-skating (Kabachkova et al 1982).\n    26\t\n    27\tTwo further points: athletes \"invariably do not allow or do not have sufficient time for recuperation after extensive strength loading,\" which minimises the LDTE and forces the body \"to unjustifiably deplete its current adaptation energy and sometimes draw upon its deep adaptation energy\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364). And \"one must differentiate between maintenance of the training effect and the LDTE of loading\": maintenance is \"the retention of functional adaptation for a given period after training has ceased\"; the LDTE is \"the relatively prolonged increase in the functional indicators as a result of specially organised, concentrated volumes of loading\" (PDF p. 377 / printed p. 366, checked against the page image).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Why it works** — The authors say the volume-performance link is \"characteristically simple\" but give no physiological mechanism for the delayed rise in 6.10's strength material; they note that the \"physiological conditions, mechanisms and characteristics of the LDTE until recently have not been a subject of much research\" (PDF p. 374). Inference: the library holds two readings of the same curve shape, supercompensation or adaptive reconstruction ([[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]]) and fitness-fatigue, where fatigue masks fitness until volume drops ([[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]); the book does not settle between them here.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated strength block for an advanced athlete when the aim is a later rise in speed-strength, accepting a planned dip during the block. Inference: a dip in speed-strength during such a block is the expected path, not by itself a signal to stop. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the LDTE rests on one load-design move, the timed volume cut, so it fits that practice. Eric's baseline: \"The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds\" (line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: during a deliberate block, an mRSI drop below an athlete's usual band would be expected; the authors' own rule separates a dip \"within an optimal range\" from the F3 case, but gives no threshold for telling them apart.\n    32\t\n    33\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no numbers for \"optimum,\" \"within an optimal range\" or \"excessive,\" and says the effect is individual (PDF p. 375). Figs 6.29-6.31 carry no sample sizes or statistics; Fig 6.31 is a \"general scheme,\" not data. The 5-12 weeks on PDF p. 374 is the length of the loading stages; the 4-12 weeks on PDF p. 375 is how long the LDTE lasts. They are different quantities. The book uses 4-12 weeks for three things: how long unidirectional training stays effective (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image), how long the LDTE lasts (PDF p. 375), and, in Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515), the length of the concentrated stages themselves: \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image). Both PDF p. 375 and the Addendum (PDF p. 536) say the LDTE lasts about as long as the loading stage (T2 about equal to T1), which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity. Keep the locator with the number. Inference: in-season, when performance is needed every week, a planned multi-week dip conflicts with Eric's baseline that \"in football you want to peak every Saturday\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)); this is an off-season tool. The book's claim that the dip comes without \"marked signs of overtraining\" sits beside Bompa's framing of concentrated loading as planned overreaching ([[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]); Inference: that is a difference of labels, not of the observed curve.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Example** — The authors' own: in sprinters (Fig 6.29), about 2.5 months of concentrated strength loading drove starting, explosive and absolute strength to about 85-96% of initial; by month 4 they stood at about 112-125% (values read from the image, PDF p. 374).\n    36\t\n    37\t**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]], [[claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training]], [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]], [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]] (maintenance, the other half of the 6.10 distinction), [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]\n##### heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.10\"\n     7\ttopic: technique timing after strength blocks\n     8\tcategory: [delayed-training-effect, technique, competition-period, concentrated-loading, sequencing]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Put Technical Training in the Delayed-Effect Window, and Keep Competition-Period Strength Work Short, Intense and Small\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Three linked rules from 6.10:\n    17\t1. **Strength first, technique in the rebound.** \"high-volume strength loads are detrimental to one's current level of speed-strength fitness and, consequently, to technical preparation. To avoid this, it is advisable in training high level athletes to structure the programme so that strength work precedes and does not interfere with technical training. Technical training should take place in the presence of the delayed training effect of the concentrated volume of strength work, i.e. when there is recovery of work capacity and an increase in the level of speed-strength.\" Technique then \"rapidly adjusts to the athlete's higher level of specialised work capacity and there is much less likelihood of reinforcing errors\" from the \"lowered functional state, caused by high-volume loads\" (PDF p. 376 / printed p. 365, checked against the page image).\n    18\t2. **Do not add volume while the effect emerges.** \"The volume of training work, therefore, should not be increased during the period of emergence of the LDTE\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image).\n    19\t3. **Competition period.** \"One should be especially cautious with strength loading during the competition period. At this time, intensive and short-term strength work can be used in a small volume to tone the neuromuscular system during competition preparation, as well as to maintain the achieved level of speed-strength preparedness if the length of the competition period exceeds the duration of the LDTE of strength loading\" (PDF pp. 375-376 / printed pp. 364-365).\n    20\t\n    21\tThe authors also state that \"the competitive action executed with maximal physical exertion represents the most specific of all the special training means,\" to be viewed \"simultaneously as a highly intense means of strength training,\" so a strength programme should rise in intensity and be \"combined with technique training so as to peak concurrently with the competitive act\" (PDF p. 376). They give \"the dominant role\" in the strength-technique interaction to \"special physical preparation, both in the yearly cycle and in long-term training\" (same page).\n    22\t\n    23\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: a strength-depressed state reinforces technical errors, while a higher work capacity lets the athlete \"concentrate his effort more effectively, increase range of movement, decrease the time of execution of movements, improve his stability, improve the strength of muscular control over all movements and increase his resistance to fatigue\" (PDF p. 376). For rule 2 they report that during emergence \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work,\" seen as \"slowed or even decreased growth of speed-strength\" (PDF p. 375).\n    24\t\n    25\t**When to apply** — After a concentrated strength block, schedule the heaviest technical emphasis for the weeks when speed-strength is coming back up, and hold volume flat through that period. In a long competition period, keep a small dose of intense, brief strength work. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume'\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: rule 3 says the same thing for the competition period. Eric's baseline: the in-season week has \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: that sits with rule 3's \"small volume\" and \"short-term\" strength work; the authors give no day-level timing.\n    26\t\n    27\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, \"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky, Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\"; PDF p. 515 / printed p. 504, checked against the page image) restates the emergence-period rule the other way round: \"In the period of LDTE realization, volume concentrated loads are handled by the body relatively easily and without harm to the body, but intense loads negatively react to the work volume\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image). Its own example then agrees with Chapter 6: an \"insignificant increase\" in specialised strength volume \"(by only a few percent)\" to \"maintain\" the achieved level \"led to a fast decrease in speed-strength and sports results\" (same page, checked against the page image). Inference: the Addendum sentence may be a translation slip: its own example fits Chapter 6, and the Addendum is a separate translation of Verkhoshansky's text, which makes a translation slip plausible. The book prints both. The authors give no number for \"small volume\" or for how long technical emphasis should wait. Inference: for skills that need daily practice (team-sport play), technique cannot simply wait for the strength block to end; the rule is written for \"high level athletes\" in events where the competitive action can be isolated, and they mention the conjugate system \"successively introduces different modes of loading ... rather than separating them in time\" (PDF p. 376), so the separation is a matter of emphasis.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Example** — Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, checked against the page image): the text says its Figure 8 shows that \"in triple jumpers,\" concentrated specialised strength loads in March-April were followed by a rise in speed-strength and results in May-June; raising strength volume \"by only a few percent\" to hold the gain then brought \"a fast decrease in speed-strength and sports results.\" The page switches from plural to singular here: the volume rise was \"as believed by the athlete,\" and \"the athlete lost his chance to be selected in the qualifying competitions for a place on the national team.\" Inference: an off-season strength block ending in March would hand over to technical and speed emphasis in April with strength volume held, not raised.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--keep-event-specific-competitive-stress-in-preparation]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--realization-mesocycle-taper-prescription]], [[principle--technical-mastery-needs-competition-intensity-practice]], [[heuristic--technique-work-during-and-after-a-concentrated-strength-block]] (6.12.2, the same strength-then-technique question from a later passage)\n##### claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.10\"\n     7\ttopic: delayed effect in endurance training\n     8\tcategory: [delayed-training-effect, endurance, altitude, monitoring, swimming]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# For Aerobic and Anaerobic Productivity, a Long-Term Delayed Effect Has Been Documented Only After Altitude Training\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under \"The Delayed Training Effect and Long Duration Work,\" the authors state: \"With respect to aerobic and anaerobic productivity, there is no experimental data that clearly documents occurrence of the LDTE phenomenon in cyclic distance training. The exceptions are cases of intense training under the lowered oxygen conditions of high altitude\" (PDF p. 376 / printed p. 365, checked against the page image). Their evidence:\n    17\t- **Swimmers (Vaitsekhovsky et al 1974).** \"significant increases\" in \"maximum aerobic capacity, the total volume of work which can be executed at the critical level of power, VO2 max and the onset of blood lactate accumulation.\" \"These changes persisted for more than 4 weeks after 3 weeks of training at an altitude of 1700m above sea level, with a peak response taking place about 1 week after the altitude training. All of the athletes in the study improved their results immediately after training at altitude.\"\n    18\t- **Skaters (Ivanov 1977).** A 4-minute cycle-ergometer test weekly for 3 weeks at 2250 m, then for 3 weeks at sea level. At altitude, mechanical efficiency fell and oxygen consumption, energy expenditure and lactate rose; back at sea level, \"the functional efficiency of the work exceeded the pre-experimental levels as a result of more economical metabolism and a significantly diminished amount of anaerobically produced ATP\" (same page).\n    19\t- **Not an altitude case: swimmers under large loads (Susman 1978; no altitude is mentioned).** The authors do not call this an LDTE; they give it after stating that disturbance of homeostasis \"can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" (PDF p. 377). Inference: it is offered as a sea-level example of the dip-and-rebound pattern in non-aerobic measures, not as a documented LDTE in aerobic productivity. Large loads slowed \"complex motor reactions,\" reduced precision of movement patterns and of \"tracking a moving object,\" and lowered work capacity, ECG-assessed cardiovascular state, muscle tonus and adrenal-cortex hormone levels. After loads were reduced and intensity altered, these indicators rose \"as the supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction) phase emerged\" (PDF p. 377 / printed p. 366, checked against the page image).\n    20\t\n    21\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for the altitude cases: hypobaric conditions gave \"a very demanding training influence which provoked persistent disturbance of the body's homeostasis and subsequent activation of its adaptive processes\" (PDF p. 376). For why the LDTE is not seen at sea level, they hedge: if it exists there, the disturbance \"will occur at the level of more subtle physiological systems\" not detectable by \"methods used for assessing the more gross aerobic and anaerobic processes,\" and \"adaptation apparently occurs at deeper cellular and molecular levels (e.g. in the mitochondria and endocrine system)\" (Yakovlev 1957, 1974; Viru 1969, 1981; Finogenov 1979), while \"disturbance of homeostasis can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" of the central nervous and neuromuscular systems (PDF pp. 376-377). This is a stated hypothesis, not a demonstrated mechanism.\n    22\t\n    23\t**When to apply** — When planning or interpreting altitude camps: the book's one quantified case puts the peak about 1 week after a 3-week stay, with changes lasting more than 4 weeks. When judging a heavy endurance block: the authors point to psycho-physiological and neuromuscular indicators as the place a hidden disturbance may show. Eric's baseline: \"Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a neuromuscular marker of that kind is the sort of indicator the authors suggest, though they name reaction, precision and tracking tasks, not jump tests.\n    24\t\n    25\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no sample sizes, controls or statistics for any of the three studies, and the skater study has no non-altitude comparison group in the text. The authors say plainly that the LDTE in sea-level distance training is not documented; do not carry the strength-block LDTE timings ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]) over to aerobic work. The LDTE they do report in cyclic sports (middle-distance running, swimming, skating) follows concentrated strength loading and shows in strength-endurance measures, not in aerobic productivity (PDF p. 374). Inference: altitude camps are rare in American collegiate football, so this entry matters mainly for endurance or Olympic-sport programmes.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Example** — The authors' own: swimmers after 3 weeks at 1700 m improved immediately, peaked about 1 week after returning, and kept the gains for more than 4 weeks (Vaitsekhovsky et al 1974; PDF p. 376 / printed p. 365).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--vo2max-alone-does-not-determine-endurance-performance]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"shared":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"095c4ecb-7fc3-4f6c-b6f2-1610dcd11eea","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
