{"parentUuid":"cc99b9de-9c68-4714-85fb-f6e5cdff983b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpLaFWont23dbNhx22u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SJY9U9tVSij6jRHt6EgcBE","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read next three fixed entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8467,"cache_read_input_tokens":79646,"output_tokens":824,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":577},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8467},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":824,"cache_read_input_tokens":79646,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8467,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8467,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SJY9U9tVSij6jRHt6EgcBE":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read next three fixed entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpLZxAmeH3WhBvYtnAB","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7bd8cbb2-70fb-464d-b8dd-cfae33aaa7c1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:52.975Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7bd8cbb2-70fb-464d-b8dd-cfae33aaa7c1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SJY9U9tVSij6jRHt6EgcBE","type":"tool_result","content":"##### claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.5\"\n     7\ttopic: concentrated vs distributed loading\n     8\tcategory: [concentrated-loading, periodization, qualification, speed-endurance, block-periodization]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Distributed and Concentrated Loading Both Work at Medium Qualification; Highly Qualified Athletes Need Concentration\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — For loading of one primary emphasis, the authors separate \"distributed loading,\" \"a relatively uniform distribution of means during an annual cycle,\" from \"concentrated loading,\" \"the concentration of means over definite training stages\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image). Their claim: \"Research indicates that both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification. The concentrated loading method is appropriate for the training of highly qualified athletes.\" The evidence named: \"distribution of glycolytic work over the annual cycle of highly qualified sprinters raises the loading volume, but does not increase the effectiveness of the loading. However, by concentrating the volume of glycolytic work at certain stages, the work can be executed at a smaller volume but with a more significant improvement in speed-endurance (Kornelyuk, 1980).\" \"Similar results\" are reported for concentrated special strength work in sprinters (Levchenko 1986), middle-distance runners (Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982), boxers (Filimonov 1979) and jumpers (Mironenko & Antonova 1981). Their conclusion, partly italicised in the original (only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is in italics): \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement in the athlete's physical fitness\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"The regular use of training with distributed loading which is dispersed over a prolonged period evokes only a transient functional reaction which does not enhance the development of long-term adaptation. Distributed loading initially can yield some rise in functional level but its training potential soon drops because of rapid adaptation of the body to the training stimuli\" (PDF p. 371). No physiological mechanism beyond that is given.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — For advanced athletes whose progress has stalled under an even, year-round spread of one kind of work, concentrate that work into defined stages. The authors list concentration of loading over time among their four organisational principles of special strength training (PDF pp. 370-371). Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... waving volume week to week ... is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: undulation is about week-to-week waves and concentration is about where a quality's volume sits across the year; the two can coexist, but the book's claim is that an evenly spread quality loses its effect for advanced athletes.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or design for any of the cited studies, and gives no definition here of \"medium\" versus \"highly qualified.\" **Contested across the library:** the vault brief of Issurin (2016) records that he judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, that the reviewed combat and team-sport studies improved fitness measures but did not report team-practice or competition outcomes, and that he placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], brief citing review pp. 6, 11; paraphrase, not Issurin's wording). The Supertraining evidence above comes from sprinters, middle-distance runners, boxers and jumpers, Inference: its boxing study (Filimonov 1979) is a combat-sport case Issurin's review would put in doubt. Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names. Inference: for football, where many qualities must be held at once, the claim applies to a single quality inside an otherwise mixed programme rather than to the whole plan. Eric's baseline: \"incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" and \"the rest are trained at a retention dose\" (line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own: highly qualified sprinters who concentrated glycolytic work into certain stages did less of it but improved speed-endurance more than when the same kind of work was spread over the year (Kornelyuk 1980; PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360). Inference: in an off-season, glycolytic conditioning could be placed in one or two short stages rather than a little every week; Eric's baseline notes that staff \"do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]] (6.8.1 and Fig 6.23, the passage PDF p. 371 points back to), [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] (the same qualification split for complex vs concentrated loading)\n##### heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.5\"\n     7\ttopic: within-session order of explosive means\n     8\tcategory: [explosive-strength, sequencing, complex-training, session-design, plyometrics]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Within One Session, the Order of Paired Explosive-Strength Means Matters Less Than Their Volume and Contrast\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — For explosive strength, the authors list \"brief combinations of training means\" that give \"a positive cumulative effect\": \"short and long jumping exercises\"; \"barbell exercises and jumping exercises\"; \"barbell exercises with 30% and 90% of maximum\"; \"barbell exercises and the plyometric method of developing explosive-strength\"; \"leaping with kettle-bells and jumping exercises\"; \"barbell exercises and leaping with kettle-bells\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image). Then: \"In a single training session the order of the above means, combined in a systematic complex is fairly unimportant, since the length of time for which the body maintains the after-effect of training stress exceeds the duration of the workout.\" In italics: \"concerning the development of explosive strength, the order of the means is not fundamentally as important as the volume of the loading and the degree of contrast between the different training means.\" Yet in the next sentence: \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order of combining means in each case, since this provides the favourable effect of a brief rise in fitness state, which the first exercise produces as the second one is executed (see Ch 5.3).\" Their delivery rule: \"employ them sequentially with active rest between each series,\" and when repeating a series, \"vary the exercises to avoid monotony and to maintain the optimal rest interval\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"During this session and the subsequent restoration period, a generalised summation of the after-effects of the entire complex of training influences of different primary emphasis occurs, which produces the same cumulative effect, generally regardless of their sequence\" (PDF p. 371). They add that \"it is still sensible to apply the recommended scheme of Figure 1.7 to minimise the risk of reducing skill and safety.\" Figure 1.7 in this edition is a torque diagram in 1.2 (PDF pp. 28-29, text layer). Inference: that cross-reference appears to be wrong and the intended scheme is unclear.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — Building a contrast or complex session for explosive strength from one of the listed pairs: settle the volume and the contrast between the two means first, then keep the listed order as a secondary choice. Inference: when equipment or space forces the order to flip on a given day, the authors' text says little is lost within the session. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** On PDF p. 371 order is ranked below volume and contrast but still to be kept (\"fairly unimportant,\" then \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"): a hedge, not a contradiction. The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order. PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277: resistance plus plyometrics works best \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (checked against the page image). PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288, novices: \"There was no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout (checked against the page image; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289, \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (checked against the page image). Inference: reading barbell work as \"strength\" and jumping as \"speed-strength\" (Fig 5.13 on the same page labels the jumping means \"Jumps\"), this opposes p. 371's \"preserve the above order\" for \"barbell exercises and jumping exercises.\" The PDF p. 301 bullets (\"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"; \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\") are stage-level: they sit under \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge\" (PDF p. 300), so they belong with [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], not here. Keep this within-session rule separate from the long-term sequence across stages, where the authors say order does matter: extensive submaximal resistance first, then explosive work, and \"The reverse sequence of this loading scheme leads to a negative cumulative effect\" (PDF p. 372). Inference: Issurin's session rules put the dominant or technical task first and add other modalities after it ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]), a stricter view of order than this passage. The authors say endurance interactions are a different case, where order clearly matters ([[framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading]]).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors list barbell exercises at 30% and 90% of maximum as one positive pairing (PDF p. 371). Inference: a session pairing heavy back squats with light jump squats, done as alternating series with active rest and with exercise variants rotated on repeat days, would follow the rule; the authors give no sets, reps or rest times here.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]], [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], [[case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers]], [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n##### heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.5\"\n     7\ttopic: stage length for sequential loading\n     8\tcategory: [sequencing, explosive-strength, block-periodization, stage-length, conjugate-sequence]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Sequential Stages Need 4-6 Weeks to Count: Extensive Submaximal Resistance First, Then Explosive Work\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"Sequential accumulation of the training effect of loading of different primary emphasis has a place in long-term preparation and is achieved by the action of preceding work on subsequent work\" (PDF p. 372 / printed p. 361, checked against the page image). For explosive strength, \"positive accumulation is achieved by means of a sequence which applies extensive (i.e. high volume) training with submaximal resistance first, followed by explosive training,\" and \"The reverse sequence of this loading scheme leads to a negative cumulative effect.\" The time rule: \"The mechanism of sequential accumulation is appropriate only if the training effect of the previous work has become relatively stable, which takes at least 4-6 weeks. When the strength means of different emphasis are changed over shorter time intervals, for example after 2 weeks, the body cannot differentiate between the specifics of their individual influence and the body's adaptive reaction as mediated by the transient accumulation process. Thus, a change after 2-week stages of use in the order of loading of different emphasis minimally alters the resulting training effect\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism for the order: \"the positive accumulation is achieved by the first loading regime producing a rise in the general energy potential of the neuromuscular system. This creates favourable conditions for further adaptation, facilitated by the subsequent loading\" (PDF p. 372). For the 4-6 week rule, their reason is that shorter stages blur into \"transient accumulation\" (the form defined in [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]]); no data or study is named for the 4-6 week figure.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — Planning an off-season run of strength and explosive stages: put the high-volume submaximal strength stage first, hold it at least 4-6 weeks, then switch to explosive emphasis. Inference: rotating emphasis every 2 weeks gives a mixed (transient) effect rather than a sequence, so if a coach wants a true sequence the stages should be longer; if a mixed effect is wanted, short rotation is acceptable. Eric's baseline: \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 180, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: that order matches the book's extensive-then-explosive sequence.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — The 4-6 week figure is stated without a cited study. The authors' own Fig 6.24 experiment used 3-month stages ([[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]]); Inference: so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum. The 5.4.4 stage sequence for a jumper starts with jumping exercises before barbell work, then jumps with weights, then depth jumps (Fig 5.18, PDF p. 308; [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]]). Inference: that is not a clean contradiction, since its first stage is low-intensity jumping rather than \"explosive training,\" but the two passages do not state the same first step. Chapter 5 states this as a rule: among \"principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period,\" \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises\" and \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\" (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290, checked against the page images; the list follows the research with novices and athletes of average qualification). Inference: \"speed-strength\" there means jumping exercises (Fig 5.13 on PDF p. 300 labels the first-stage means \"Jumps\"), not the explosive training of p. 372, so the conflict turns on what the first stage contains. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... waving volume week to week\" is Eric's preferred model (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: by this passage, week-to-week waves inside one emphasis are compatible with the rule, but changing the emphasis itself every two weeks would be the \"transient\" case. Inference: Bondarchuk agrees on the direction, from a different premise: stages shorter than the 2-3 months most athletes need to reach sports form cannot build a real base, and 1-3 week rotating stages give only \"current\" transfer ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]]). Inference: Issurin's accumulation blocks of up to about six weeks, and shorter ones to refresh ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]]), concern block length for a mixed set of targets, a different quantity from the 4-6 week stability threshold here.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own direction: extensive submaximal resistance work first, then explosive training; the reverse gives \"a negative cumulative effect\" (PDF p. 372). Inference: a 6-week high-volume squat and pull stage at moderate loads, then a 4-6 week jump and light-load speed stage, rather than alternating the two emphases every fortnight.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]], [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]], [[heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]] (6.8.1, block lengths for raising adaptation reserves, a different quantity from the 4-6 week stability threshold)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ff9ab1d6-a4e8-48d7-87dd-4c758c230ddd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:53.180Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.5\"\n     7\ttopic: concentrated vs distributed loading\n     8\tcategory: [concentrated-loading, periodization, qualification, speed-endurance, block-periodization]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Distributed and Concentrated Loading Both Work at Medium Qualification; Highly Qualified Athletes Need Concentration\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — For loading of one primary emphasis, the authors separate \"distributed loading,\" \"a relatively uniform distribution of means during an annual cycle,\" from \"concentrated loading,\" \"the concentration of means over definite training stages\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image). Their claim: \"Research indicates that both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification. The concentrated loading method is appropriate for the training of highly qualified athletes.\" The evidence named: \"distribution of glycolytic work over the annual cycle of highly qualified sprinters raises the loading volume, but does not increase the effectiveness of the loading. However, by concentrating the volume of glycolytic work at certain stages, the work can be executed at a smaller volume but with a more significant improvement in speed-endurance (Kornelyuk, 1980).\" \"Similar results\" are reported for concentrated special strength work in sprinters (Levchenko 1986), middle-distance runners (Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982), boxers (Filimonov 1979) and jumpers (Mironenko & Antonova 1981). Their conclusion, partly italicised in the original (only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is in italics): \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement in the athlete's physical fitness\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"The regular use of training with distributed loading which is dispersed over a prolonged period evokes only a transient functional reaction which does not enhance the development of long-term adaptation. Distributed loading initially can yield some rise in functional level but its training potential soon drops because of rapid adaptation of the body to the training stimuli\" (PDF p. 371). No physiological mechanism beyond that is given.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — For advanced athletes whose progress has stalled under an even, year-round spread of one kind of work, concentrate that work into defined stages. The authors list concentration of loading over time among their four organisational principles of special strength training (PDF pp. 370-371). Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... waving volume week to week ... is stated as the personally preferred model over strict linear progression\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: undulation is about week-to-week waves and concentration is about where a quality's volume sits across the year; the two can coexist, but the book's claim is that an evenly spread quality loses its effect for advanced athletes.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or design for any of the cited studies, and gives no definition here of \"medium\" versus \"highly qualified.\" **Contested across the library:** the vault brief of Issurin (2016) records that he judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, that the reviewed combat and team-sport studies improved fitness measures but did not report team-practice or competition outcomes, and that he placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], brief citing review pp. 6, 11; paraphrase, not Issurin's wording). The Supertraining evidence above comes from sprinters, middle-distance runners, boxers and jumpers, Inference: its boxing study (Filimonov 1979) is a combat-sport case Issurin's review would put in doubt. Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names. Inference: for football, where many qualities must be held at once, the claim applies to a single quality inside an otherwise mixed programme rather than to the whole plan. Eric's baseline: \"incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" and \"the rest are trained at a retention dose\" (line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own: highly qualified sprinters who concentrated glycolytic work into certain stages did less of it but improved speed-endurance more than when the same kind of work was spread over the year (Kornelyuk 1980; PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360). Inference: in an off-season, glycolytic conditioning could be placed in one or two short stages rather than a little every week; Eric's baseline notes that staff \"do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]] (6.8.1 and Fig 6.23, the passage PDF p. 371 points back to), [[claim--complex-training-falls-short-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] (the same qualification split for complex vs concentrated loading)\n##### heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.5\"\n     7\ttopic: within-session order of explosive means\n     8\tcategory: [explosive-strength, sequencing, complex-training, session-design, plyometrics]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Within One Session, the Order of Paired Explosive-Strength Means Matters Less Than Their Volume and Contrast\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — For explosive strength, the authors list \"brief combinations of training means\" that give \"a positive cumulative effect\": \"short and long jumping exercises\"; \"barbell exercises and jumping exercises\"; \"barbell exercises with 30% and 90% of maximum\"; \"barbell exercises and the plyometric method of developing explosive-strength\"; \"leaping with kettle-bells and jumping exercises\"; \"barbell exercises and leaping with kettle-bells\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image). Then: \"In a single training session the order of the above means, combined in a systematic complex is fairly unimportant, since the length of time for which the body maintains the after-effect of training stress exceeds the duration of the workout.\" In italics: \"concerning the development of explosive strength, the order of the means is not fundamentally as important as the volume of the loading and the degree of contrast between the different training means.\" Yet in the next sentence: \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order of combining means in each case, since this provides the favourable effect of a brief rise in fitness state, which the first exercise produces as the second one is executed (see Ch 5.3).\" Their delivery rule: \"employ them sequentially with active rest between each series,\" and when repeating a series, \"vary the exercises to avoid monotony and to maintain the optimal rest interval\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"During this session and the subsequent restoration period, a generalised summation of the after-effects of the entire complex of training influences of different primary emphasis occurs, which produces the same cumulative effect, generally regardless of their sequence\" (PDF p. 371). They add that \"it is still sensible to apply the recommended scheme of Figure 1.7 to minimise the risk of reducing skill and safety.\" Figure 1.7 in this edition is a torque diagram in 1.2 (PDF pp. 28-29, text layer). Inference: that cross-reference appears to be wrong and the intended scheme is unclear.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — Building a contrast or complex session for explosive strength from one of the listed pairs: settle the volume and the contrast between the two means first, then keep the listed order as a secondary choice. Inference: when equipment or space forces the order to flip on a given day, the authors' text says little is lost within the session. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** On PDF p. 371 order is ranked below volume and contrast but still to be kept (\"fairly unimportant,\" then \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"): a hedge, not a contradiction. The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order. PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277: resistance plus plyometrics works best \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (checked against the page image). PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288, novices: \"There was no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout (checked against the page image; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289, \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (checked against the page image). Inference: reading barbell work as \"strength\" and jumping as \"speed-strength\" (Fig 5.13 on the same page labels the jumping means \"Jumps\"), this opposes p. 371's \"preserve the above order\" for \"barbell exercises and jumping exercises.\" The PDF p. 301 bullets (\"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"; \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\") are stage-level: they sit under \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge\" (PDF p. 300), so they belong with [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], not here. Keep this within-session rule separate from the long-term sequence across stages, where the authors say order does matter: extensive submaximal resistance first, then explosive work, and \"The reverse sequence of this loading scheme leads to a negative cumulative effect\" (PDF p. 372). Inference: Issurin's session rules put the dominant or technical task first and add other modalities after it ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]]), a stricter view of order than this passage. The authors say endurance interactions are a different case, where order clearly matters ([[framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading]]).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors list barbell exercises at 30% and 90% of maximum as one positive pairing (PDF p. 371). Inference: a session pairing heavy back squats with light jump squats, done as alternating series with active rest and with exercise variants rotated on repeat days, would follow the rule; the authors give no sets, reps or rest times here.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]], [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], [[case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers]], [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n##### heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.5\"\n     7\ttopic: stage length for sequential loading\n     8\tcategory: [sequencing, explosive-strength, block-periodization, stage-length, conjugate-sequence]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Sequential Stages Need 4-6 Weeks to Count: Extensive Submaximal Resistance First, Then Explosive Work\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"Sequential accumulation of the training effect of loading of different primary emphasis has a place in long-term preparation and is achieved by the action of preceding work on subsequent work\" (PDF p. 372 / printed p. 361, checked against the page image). For explosive strength, \"positive accumulation is achieved by means of a sequence which applies extensive (i.e. high volume) training with submaximal resistance first, followed by explosive training,\" and \"The reverse sequence of this loading scheme leads to a negative cumulative effect.\" The time rule: \"The mechanism of sequential accumulation is appropriate only if the training effect of the previous work has become relatively stable, which takes at least 4-6 weeks. When the strength means of different emphasis are changed over shorter time intervals, for example after 2 weeks, the body cannot differentiate between the specifics of their individual influence and the body's adaptive reaction as mediated by the transient accumulation process. Thus, a change after 2-week stages of use in the order of loading of different emphasis minimally alters the resulting training effect\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors give a mechanism for the order: \"the positive accumulation is achieved by the first loading regime producing a rise in the general energy potential of the neuromuscular system. This creates favourable conditions for further adaptation, facilitated by the subsequent loading\" (PDF p. 372). For the 4-6 week rule, their reason is that shorter stages blur into \"transient accumulation\" (the form defined in [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]]); no data or study is named for the 4-6 week figure.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — Planning an off-season run of strength and explosive stages: put the high-volume submaximal strength stage first, hold it at least 4-6 weeks, then switch to explosive emphasis. Inference: rotating emphasis every 2 weeks gives a mixed (transient) effect rather than a sequence, so if a coach wants a true sequence the stages should be longer; if a mixed effect is wanted, short rotation is acceptable. Eric's baseline: \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 180, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: that order matches the book's extensive-then-explosive sequence.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — The 4-6 week figure is stated without a cited study. The authors' own Fig 6.24 experiment used 3-month stages ([[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]]); Inference: so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum, not a tested optimum. The 5.4.4 stage sequence for a jumper starts with jumping exercises before barbell work, then jumps with weights, then depth jumps (Fig 5.18, PDF p. 308; [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]]). Inference: that is not a clean contradiction, since its first stage is low-intensity jumping rather than \"explosive training,\" but the two passages do not state the same first step. Chapter 5 states this as a rule: among \"principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period,\" \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises\" and \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\" (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290, checked against the page images; the list follows the research with novices and athletes of average qualification). Inference: \"speed-strength\" there means jumping exercises (Fig 5.13 on PDF p. 300 labels the first-stage means \"Jumps\"), not the explosive training of p. 372, so the conflict turns on what the first stage contains. Eric's baseline: \"Undulating/non-linear periodization ... waving volume week to week\" is Eric's preferred model (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: by this passage, week-to-week waves inside one emphasis are compatible with the rule, but changing the emphasis itself every two weeks would be the \"transient\" case. Inference: Bondarchuk agrees on the direction, from a different premise: stages shorter than the 2-3 months most athletes need to reach sports form cannot build a real base, and 1-3 week rotating stages give only \"current\" transfer ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]]). Inference: Issurin's accumulation blocks of up to about six weeks, and shorter ones to refresh ([[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]]), concern block length for a mixed set of targets, a different quantity from the 4-6 week stability threshold here.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own direction: extensive submaximal resistance work first, then explosive training; the reverse gives \"a negative cumulative effect\" (PDF p. 372). Inference: a 6-week high-volume squat and pull stage at moderate loads, then a 4-6 week jump and light-load speed stage, rather than alternating the two emphases every fortnight.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]], [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]], [[heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]] (6.8.1, block lengths for raising adaptation reserves, a different quantity from the 4-6 week stability threshold)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7bd8cbb2-70fb-464d-b8dd-cfae33aaa7c1","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
