{"parentUuid":"18dab731-09d5-4315-81b4-3c7a9b91d744","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffpM7NGyMZypHbZJaLwp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U55ViqjrWxzLWHbDgV3CyA","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read three more fixed entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7809,"cache_read_input_tokens":88113,"output_tokens":623,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":390},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7809},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":623,"cache_read_input_tokens":88113,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7809,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01U55ViqjrWxzLWHbDgV3CyA":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; for f in claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation; do echo \"##### $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read three more fixed entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpM72C7VUmbRfuzvbvP","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d0c2d4b9-5530-4a1d-bfc7-b17471040213","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:59.175Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d0c2d4b9-5530-4a1d-bfc7-b17471040213","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U55ViqjrWxzLWHbDgV3CyA","type":"tool_result","content":"##### claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.4\"\n     7\ttopic: duration limits of one emphasis\n     8\tcategory: [stage-length, explosive-strength, aerobic, anaerobic-glycolytic, plateau]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# How Long One Emphasis Keeps Paying: Explosive Strength Plateaus in 3-4 Months, Aerobic Fitness in 2-3, Glycolytic Capacity Peaks at About 4\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors say the duration of a load \"can easily be miscalculated by the average coach,\" and that besides the overall limit set by the body's current adaptation reserves, \"there is also an upper limit to the duration of using any specialised training means with a primary emphasis, the exceeding of which is detrimental to the development of maximum and explosive strength, as well as to the growth of aerobic and anaerobic capacity. Exceeding this limit does not enhance fitness and wastes the athlete's time and energy\" (PDF p. 370 / printed p. 359, checked against the page image). They admit \"there is no special research directed at determining the optimal length of time,\" and offer \"indirect data\" (same page):\n    17\t- **Absolute strength.** A \"linear increase ... which slows down as the limit of this optimal time-span of training is reached\"; yet in highly qualified athletes \"absolute strength increases with little variation, rising continuously during the year's training.\"\n    18\t- **Explosive strength.** \"the sharper its growth, the earlier it plateaus. When one is emphasizing the development of explosive strength exclusively, the plateau appears within 3-4 months.\" Where it \"is not the key ability (e.g. in a mono-cycle of periodisation in the annual training of decathletes),\" it \"can continue (with some fluctuation) for up to 10 months.\"\n    19\t- **Aerobic.** \"significant increases in aerobic efficiency even within one month\" (Borisov 1979; Sirenko 1980). Aerobic fitness and aerobic-zone loading \"(at a HR of 120-170 beats/minute)\" rise together, \"linear over a 2-3 months period\"; after that, \"Despite increased loading volume, the aerobic fitness indicators do not increase significantly ... but fluctuate around the maximum level achieved\" (five citations, Zatsiorsky et al 1974 to Sirenko 1980).\n    20\t- **Anaerobic-glycolytic.** \"About 4 months are necessary to achieve the highest results in anaerobic-glycolytic productivity\" (four citations). Then: \"Increasing the volume of anaerobic work produces a positive effect only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work, thereby stimulating the aerobic reaction (Serafimova, 1974; Naumenko, 1978).\"\n    21\t\n    22\tThe scope note: the figures apply to \"the natural conditions of an athlete's preparation,\" with other tasks trained alongside, match the volumes \"highly qualified athletes use,\" and are \"not definitive. However, they offer a preliminary guide\" (PDF p. 370).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Why it works** — The authors tie the slowing of gains to \"exhaustion of the body's current adaptation energy (see 1.16)\" (PDF p. 370). No further mechanism is given per quality.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When to apply** — Setting the length of a stage that emphasises one quality, and deciding when a plateau means \"change the stimulus\" rather than \"add volume.\" Inference: an aerobic emphasis that has run 2-3 months without further gain is, by these figures, at its ceiling for now, and more volume is not the lever. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: these time limits give a rough expectation of when a plateau is due.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When NOT to apply** — The authors call the data indirect and the durations provisional. **Unclear wording:** read literally, the p. 370 sentence (anaerobic volume helps \"only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work, thereby stimulating the aerobic reaction\") fits the session-level lists on PDF p. 372 (positive: \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"; negative: \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\"; checked against the page image), but conflicts with the stage-level sequential model on PDF p. 373, where aerobic work comes first and \"promotes the training-effect of anaerobic work\" (checked against the page image). Inference: if \"precedes\" is a slip for \"is preceded by,\" it fits p. 373 and not p. 372. Both of p. 370's citations (Serafimova 1974; Naumenko 1978) appear among the four p. 373 gives for its aerobic-first warning (with Vavilov 1977 and Morzhevikov 1980), which favours the slip reading, but the book does not resolve it. These are times to plateau under one emphasis; they are not residual (detraining) durations, which Issurin gives separately ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]). Inference: the plateau figures come largely from track-and-field and endurance athletes; for football, where qualities are trained together, the decathlete case (explosive strength rising for up to 10 months when it is not the sole aim) may be the closer analogue.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Example** — The authors' own contrast: explosive strength trained exclusively plateaus within 3-4 months, while in decathletes on a mono-cycle, where it is one quality among many, it can keep rising for up to 10 months (PDF p. 370 / printed p. 359).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Related** — [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]], [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], [[framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development]], [[framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]\n##### case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: case-study\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.1\"\n     7\ttopic: light, heavy and combined loads\n     8\tcategory: [load-selection, power, speed-strength, cumulative-effect, jumpers]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Nedobyvailov (Fig 6.25): 30-50% 1RM Gave the Most Speed, 70-90% the Most Strength, Combined 30-90% the Most Power\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"over a 7-week period, group 1 used weights exercises with 30-50% of 1RM; group 2 used 70-90% of 1RM and group 3 used weights of 30-90% of 1RM concurrently (i.e. using the 30-50% and 70-90% regimes together). The general loading in all groups was equivalent in terms of the metabolic cost of the work\" (PDF p. 365 / printed p. 354, checked against the page image). The caption: \"The training effect (in % increase) of different loading schemes on highly qualified jumpers for the extensor muscles of both legs (Nedobyvailov).\" The authors point to \"how the cumulative phenomenon appears as a result of combination work with weights of 30-90% of 1RM regarding the power achieved in group 3, and how much higher it is relative to the partial TE obtained with means of single emphasis,\" and add that \"the greatest increase in speed was produced by training with loads of 30-50% of 1RM (Group 1, graph 2)\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Figure 6.25 values** (read by eye from a 300 dpi crop of PDF p. 365, % increase): absolute strength GP1 about 7, GP2 about 18, GP3 about 17; speed GP1 about 18, GP2 about 11, GP3 about 14; power GP1 about 6, GP2 about 14, GP3 about 20 (the GP3 power bar ends on the 20 gridline).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism; they present the result as an example of a cumulative effect being more than either partial effect. Inference: in these bars the combined group sits near the heavy group on strength and between the two on speed, so its power lead fits a product of both; that reading is not in the book.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — When power is the target, the authors' data favour mixing light (30-50%) and heavy (70-90%) loads over either alone; when pure speed is the target, light loads did best. Eric's baseline: \"The dynamic strength index (DSI ...) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21); the baseline tags it contested, \"one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data\"). Inference: this experiment is another case where mixing the heavy and light ends beat either single emphasis on power, which argues for a mixed default when the DSI read is ambiguous.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — Seven weeks, \"highly qualified jumpers,\" leg extensors only; no sample size, statistics, exercises or test methods are given, and \"speed\" and \"power\" are not defined. Volume was equated by \"metabolic cost,\" which the authors do not explain. Inference: group 3's absolute-strength gain (about 17%) was close to the heavy-only group's (about 18%), so in this figure mixing did not cost strength; that may not hold with less training time.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: power rose about 20% in the 30-90% group against about 14% (70-90%) and about 6% (30-50%), while speed rose most, about 18%, with 30-50% loads (Fig 6.25, PDF p. 365).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]], [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]], [[heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast]] (lists barbell work at 30% and 90% as a positive pairing), [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]], [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]], [[principle--force-velocity-relationship-and-training-specificity]]\n##### heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.3\"\n     7\ttopic: restoration means and adaptation\n     8\tcategory: [recovery, restoration, gpp, adaptation, monitoring]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Restoration Means Help When Organised With the Load, but Routine Artificial Restoration Can Blunt Adaptation\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors give GPP means a restorative role, important \"in those sports in which there are few supplementary training means and training is inherently monotonous ... (gymnastics, weightlifting and cyclic sports)\" (PDF p. 368 / printed p. 357, checked against the page image). Two reported gains: with \"the sole and systematic use of swimming restoration procedures during the execution of voluminous loading,\" absolute strength rose 24.2% and strength-endurance 18.9%, against 7.7% and 4.9% in swimmers without them, while training volume rose \"by an average of 15%\" (Kuchnyev 1977; Platonov 1980); and in highly qualified middle-distance runners before competition, \"low frequency vibromassage and psycho-regulatory training\" raised training-distance speed by 2-4% and volume at competition speed by 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979). They conclude that \"the organised combination of training loads and restorative procedures is an important prerequisite for intensifying and increasing the effectiveness of training\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\tThen the limit: \"restoration after work is a natural process which occurs in a definite sequence over a certain period\" (Yakovlev 1971). \"Artificially accelerating the restoration process as a routine can disturb the natural course of the body's long-term adaptation to intense muscular activity and therefore can be justified only in clear cases of over-exertion (Siff & Yessis, 1992).\" \"Frequent and regular application of non-specific means of restoration may hinder the adaptation process, especially when large training loads are used,\" and \"premature intervention during the course of restoration can hinder the body's supercompensatory ability to enhance this process by natural means (Yevgenyev et al, 1975).\" The loading-restoration process \"should be strictly regulated on the basis of scientific assessment of the athlete's fitness state\" (PDF p. 368).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"Increasing the volume and intensity of the loading disturbs homeostasis as a basic condition for promoting adaptation to loading and enhancement of the restoration processes\" (PDF p. 368). Inference: removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal. No study is described in enough detail to test the warning.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Deciding when to use restoration modalities: plan them as part of the load when the aim is to raise training volume, and avoid them as a daily routine after every hard session, keeping them for \"clear cases of over-exertion.\" Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a substitute for managing training stress and dose correctly\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred (transcript)). Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the authors' last sentence asks for restoration to be regulated on an assessment of fitness state, which the baseline says is not done; the mRSI read (line 74) is the nearest existing measure.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — The positive data and the warning sit side by side without a rule for telling \"organised combination\" from \"routine\" use. The reported gains carry no sample sizes or statistics. The text says training volume rose \"by an average of 15%\" but not whether in both groups or only the restoration group (PDF p. 368); Inference: if only the restoration group, the result confounds restoration with extra volume. Inference: the warning names \"non-specific means\" and \"artificially accelerating\" restoration; it does not address sleep or nutrition.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: swimmers using systematic restoration procedures during high-volume loading gained 24.2% in absolute strength and 18.9% in strength-endurance, against 7.7% and 4.9% without them; \"the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%,\" without saying in which group (Kuchnyev 1977; Platonov 1980; PDF p. 368 / printed p. 357).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--microcycle-construction-key-workout-rules]] (restorative procedures in the microcycle), [[framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"cf8f2a65-575f-4094-8f60-397c7db4b422","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:58:59.419Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.4\"\n     7\ttopic: duration limits of one emphasis\n     8\tcategory: [stage-length, explosive-strength, aerobic, anaerobic-glycolytic, plateau]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# How Long One Emphasis Keeps Paying: Explosive Strength Plateaus in 3-4 Months, Aerobic Fitness in 2-3, Glycolytic Capacity Peaks at About 4\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors say the duration of a load \"can easily be miscalculated by the average coach,\" and that besides the overall limit set by the body's current adaptation reserves, \"there is also an upper limit to the duration of using any specialised training means with a primary emphasis, the exceeding of which is detrimental to the development of maximum and explosive strength, as well as to the growth of aerobic and anaerobic capacity. Exceeding this limit does not enhance fitness and wastes the athlete's time and energy\" (PDF p. 370 / printed p. 359, checked against the page image). They admit \"there is no special research directed at determining the optimal length of time,\" and offer \"indirect data\" (same page):\n    17\t- **Absolute strength.** A \"linear increase ... which slows down as the limit of this optimal time-span of training is reached\"; yet in highly qualified athletes \"absolute strength increases with little variation, rising continuously during the year's training.\"\n    18\t- **Explosive strength.** \"the sharper its growth, the earlier it plateaus. When one is emphasizing the development of explosive strength exclusively, the plateau appears within 3-4 months.\" Where it \"is not the key ability (e.g. in a mono-cycle of periodisation in the annual training of decathletes),\" it \"can continue (with some fluctuation) for up to 10 months.\"\n    19\t- **Aerobic.** \"significant increases in aerobic efficiency even within one month\" (Borisov 1979; Sirenko 1980). Aerobic fitness and aerobic-zone loading \"(at a HR of 120-170 beats/minute)\" rise together, \"linear over a 2-3 months period\"; after that, \"Despite increased loading volume, the aerobic fitness indicators do not increase significantly ... but fluctuate around the maximum level achieved\" (five citations, Zatsiorsky et al 1974 to Sirenko 1980).\n    20\t- **Anaerobic-glycolytic.** \"About 4 months are necessary to achieve the highest results in anaerobic-glycolytic productivity\" (four citations). Then: \"Increasing the volume of anaerobic work produces a positive effect only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work, thereby stimulating the aerobic reaction (Serafimova, 1974; Naumenko, 1978).\"\n    21\t\n    22\tThe scope note: the figures apply to \"the natural conditions of an athlete's preparation,\" with other tasks trained alongside, match the volumes \"highly qualified athletes use,\" and are \"not definitive. However, they offer a preliminary guide\" (PDF p. 370).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Why it works** — The authors tie the slowing of gains to \"exhaustion of the body's current adaptation energy (see 1.16)\" (PDF p. 370). No further mechanism is given per quality.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When to apply** — Setting the length of a stage that emphasises one quality, and deciding when a plateau means \"change the stimulus\" rather than \"add volume.\" Inference: an aerobic emphasis that has run 2-3 months without further gain is, by these figures, at its ceiling for now, and more volume is not the lever. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: these time limits give a rough expectation of when a plateau is due.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When NOT to apply** — The authors call the data indirect and the durations provisional. **Unclear wording:** read literally, the p. 370 sentence (anaerobic volume helps \"only if it precedes a significant volume of aerobic work, thereby stimulating the aerobic reaction\") fits the session-level lists on PDF p. 372 (positive: \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"; negative: \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\"; checked against the page image), but conflicts with the stage-level sequential model on PDF p. 373, where aerobic work comes first and \"promotes the training-effect of anaerobic work\" (checked against the page image). Inference: if \"precedes\" is a slip for \"is preceded by,\" it fits p. 373 and not p. 372. Both of p. 370's citations (Serafimova 1974; Naumenko 1978) appear among the four p. 373 gives for its aerobic-first warning (with Vavilov 1977 and Morzhevikov 1980), which favours the slip reading, but the book does not resolve it. These are times to plateau under one emphasis; they are not residual (detraining) durations, which Issurin gives separately ([[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]). Inference: the plateau figures come largely from track-and-field and endurance athletes; for football, where qualities are trained together, the decathlete case (explosive strength rising for up to 10 months when it is not the sole aim) may be the closer analogue.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Example** — The authors' own contrast: explosive strength trained exclusively plateaus within 3-4 months, while in decathletes on a mono-cycle, where it is one quality among many, it can keep rising for up to 10 months (PDF p. 370 / printed p. 359).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Related** — [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]], [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], [[framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development]], [[framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/principle--accommodation-and-the-diminishing-returns-law]]\n##### case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: case-study\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.1\"\n     7\ttopic: light, heavy and combined loads\n     8\tcategory: [load-selection, power, speed-strength, cumulative-effect, jumpers]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Nedobyvailov (Fig 6.25): 30-50% 1RM Gave the Most Speed, 70-90% the Most Strength, Combined 30-90% the Most Power\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"over a 7-week period, group 1 used weights exercises with 30-50% of 1RM; group 2 used 70-90% of 1RM and group 3 used weights of 30-90% of 1RM concurrently (i.e. using the 30-50% and 70-90% regimes together). The general loading in all groups was equivalent in terms of the metabolic cost of the work\" (PDF p. 365 / printed p. 354, checked against the page image). The caption: \"The training effect (in % increase) of different loading schemes on highly qualified jumpers for the extensor muscles of both legs (Nedobyvailov).\" The authors point to \"how the cumulative phenomenon appears as a result of combination work with weights of 30-90% of 1RM regarding the power achieved in group 3, and how much higher it is relative to the partial TE obtained with means of single emphasis,\" and add that \"the greatest increase in speed was produced by training with loads of 30-50% of 1RM (Group 1, graph 2)\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Figure 6.25 values** (read by eye from a 300 dpi crop of PDF p. 365, % increase): absolute strength GP1 about 7, GP2 about 18, GP3 about 17; speed GP1 about 18, GP2 about 11, GP3 about 14; power GP1 about 6, GP2 about 14, GP3 about 20 (the GP3 power bar ends on the 20 gridline).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism; they present the result as an example of a cumulative effect being more than either partial effect. Inference: in these bars the combined group sits near the heavy group on strength and between the two on speed, so its power lead fits a product of both; that reading is not in the book.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — When power is the target, the authors' data favour mixing light (30-50%) and heavy (70-90%) loads over either alone; when pure speed is the target, light loads did best. Eric's baseline: \"The dynamic strength index (DSI ...) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21); the baseline tags it contested, \"one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data\"). Inference: this experiment is another case where mixing the heavy and light ends beat either single emphasis on power, which argues for a mixed default when the DSI read is ambiguous.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — Seven weeks, \"highly qualified jumpers,\" leg extensors only; no sample size, statistics, exercises or test methods are given, and \"speed\" and \"power\" are not defined. Volume was equated by \"metabolic cost,\" which the authors do not explain. Inference: group 3's absolute-strength gain (about 17%) was close to the heavy-only group's (about 18%), so in this figure mixing did not cost strength; that may not hold with less training time.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: power rose about 20% in the 30-90% group against about 14% (70-90%) and about 6% (30-50%), while speed rose most, about 18%, with 30-50% loads (Fig 6.25, PDF p. 365).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]], [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]], [[heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast]] (lists barbell work at 30% and 90% as a positive pairing), [[heuristic--speed-strength-loading-light-loads-maximal-acceleration-and-5-to-1-mix]], [[claim--power-output-peaks-then-declines-with-load-jump-vs-leg-press]], [[principle--force-velocity-relationship-and-training-specificity]]\n##### heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.3\"\n     7\ttopic: restoration means and adaptation\n     8\tcategory: [recovery, restoration, gpp, adaptation, monitoring]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Restoration Means Help When Organised With the Load, but Routine Artificial Restoration Can Blunt Adaptation\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors give GPP means a restorative role, important \"in those sports in which there are few supplementary training means and training is inherently monotonous ... (gymnastics, weightlifting and cyclic sports)\" (PDF p. 368 / printed p. 357, checked against the page image). Two reported gains: with \"the sole and systematic use of swimming restoration procedures during the execution of voluminous loading,\" absolute strength rose 24.2% and strength-endurance 18.9%, against 7.7% and 4.9% in swimmers without them, while training volume rose \"by an average of 15%\" (Kuchnyev 1977; Platonov 1980); and in highly qualified middle-distance runners before competition, \"low frequency vibromassage and psycho-regulatory training\" raised training-distance speed by 2-4% and volume at competition speed by 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979). They conclude that \"the organised combination of training loads and restorative procedures is an important prerequisite for intensifying and increasing the effectiveness of training\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\tThen the limit: \"restoration after work is a natural process which occurs in a definite sequence over a certain period\" (Yakovlev 1971). \"Artificially accelerating the restoration process as a routine can disturb the natural course of the body's long-term adaptation to intense muscular activity and therefore can be justified only in clear cases of over-exertion (Siff & Yessis, 1992).\" \"Frequent and regular application of non-specific means of restoration may hinder the adaptation process, especially when large training loads are used,\" and \"premature intervention during the course of restoration can hinder the body's supercompensatory ability to enhance this process by natural means (Yevgenyev et al, 1975).\" The loading-restoration process \"should be strictly regulated on the basis of scientific assessment of the athlete's fitness state\" (PDF p. 368).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"Increasing the volume and intensity of the loading disturbs homeostasis as a basic condition for promoting adaptation to loading and enhancement of the restoration processes\" (PDF p. 368). Inference: removing that disturbance too early removes part of the signal. No study is described in enough detail to test the warning.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Deciding when to use restoration modalities: plan them as part of the load when the aim is to raise training volume, and avoid them as a daily routine after every hard session, keeping them for \"clear cases of over-exertion.\" Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a substitute for managing training stress and dose correctly\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred (transcript)). Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the authors' last sentence asks for restoration to be regulated on an assessment of fitness state, which the baseline says is not done; the mRSI read (line 74) is the nearest existing measure.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — The positive data and the warning sit side by side without a rule for telling \"organised combination\" from \"routine\" use. The reported gains carry no sample sizes or statistics. The text says training volume rose \"by an average of 15%\" but not whether in both groups or only the restoration group (PDF p. 368); Inference: if only the restoration group, the result confounds restoration with extra volume. Inference: the warning names \"non-specific means\" and \"artificially accelerating\" restoration; it does not address sleep or nutrition.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: swimmers using systematic restoration procedures during high-volume loading gained 24.2% in absolute strength and 18.9% in strength-endurance, against 7.7% and 4.9% without them; \"the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%,\" without saying in which group (Kuchnyev 1977; Platonov 1980; PDF p. 368 / printed p. 357).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--microcycle-construction-key-workout-rules]] (restorative procedures in the microcycle), [[framework--supplementary-readiness-markers-hr-bp-ceff-palpation]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d0c2d4b9-5530-4a1d-bfc7-b17471040213","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
