ibutionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"618462c7-a67a-4a33-9f5b-73e8101cfaee","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:59:02.293Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"618462c7-a67a-4a33-9f5b-73e8101cfaee","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MG3JvnUKGk42hJqAVgjzHc","type":"tool_result","content":"##### principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: principle\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.4\"\n     7\ttopic: function of training volume\n     8\tcategory: [volume, intensity, long-term-adaptation, connective-tissue, qualification]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reaction, and then to the development of long-term adaptation of the body. Therefore, in each successive annual cycle athletes should execute larger volumes of loading (within optimal limits) to raise and maintain the special work-capacity\" (PDF p. 369 / printed p. 358, checked against the page image). Second, in sports with \"brief, highly concentrated efforts\": \"the mechanical integrity of the ligamentous-joint apparatus can become the limiting factor to the progress of sporting performance\" (italics in the original). \"If these connective tissues are not prepared by extensive work of moderate intensity, then the great dynamic overloading encountered at the level of peak performance can lead to severe joint injury. This occurs frequently in gymnastics, weightlifting, high jumping and triple jumping\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\tVolume alone is not enough: \"the influence of the volume can be determined correctly only if one also takes into consideration the magnitude of the loading, its duration and intensity\" (PDF p. 369). Magnitude is set \"first of all, on the basis of the athlete's qualification\": the higher the qualification, the larger the yearly and stage volume, and the share of \"specialised loading\" grows (Panov 1975; Zhikharevich 1976; Vankov 1977; Matveyev 1977; Gilyazova 1978). The annual volume is \"established individually\" from the preceding stages; \"A more serious task\" is the optimal volume per month, stage or phase (same page). On intensity: it \"determines the strength and specificity of its effect on the body,\" regulates \"the training potential of the given means,\" frequency and intervals, and \"the ratio of the volume of the loading divided by the time taken to reach the maximum load in a given stage,\" a measure of \"loading concentration relative to time.\" \"Intensification of loading is permitted during specific stages in the annual cycle and only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading (see Fig 6.1)\" (PDF p. 370 / printed p. 359, checked against the page image). The same caution closes the preceding passage on training potential: \"The training potential of the means employed decreases as the special work capacity increases,\" so more effective means must be introduced over time (Fig 6.28); \"However, it is inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely, because the body is functionally unprepared for them, a situation that can cause excessive intensification of training and disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\" (PDF pp. 368-369 / printed pp. 357-358, checked against the page images).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one quoted: prolonged homeostatic disturbance mobilises \"energy resources and plastic reserves\" and moves the body from an acute to a general, then long-term, adaptive reaction (PDF p. 369). For connective tissue they refer back to 1.6 and 1.7.2 and give no further mechanism here.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Building a multi-year plan (raise annual volume, within limits, as qualification rises) and opening each year with extensive moderate-intensity work before intensifying, especially in jumping and other high-impact events. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred (transcript)). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill ...)\" (line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' \"extensive low intensity loading\" before intensification is close to the work-capacity base of that hierarchy.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — \"within optimal limits\" is not quantified, and the rule to raise volume every year cannot run indefinitely; the authors elsewhere set a limit by current adaptation reserves (PDF p. 370) and an optimum above which the LDTE pattern appears (PDF p. 374, [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]). The joint-injury link is stated as frequent in four sports with no data. Inference: in American collegiate football, where many athletes are four- or five-year players, a rising-volume rule would need to be weighed against Eric's baseline that recovery is handled \"through training-load design alone\" (line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)), since nothing else would catch an overshoot. The book's 4.1.2 passage gives volume two different functions (a functional base and endurance development; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]); Inference: the two lists overlap on the \"base\" role and differ on the second, so read them together rather than as rival definitions.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: gymnastics, weightlifting, high jumping and triple jumping, where joint injury \"occurs frequently\" if connective tissue has not been prepared by extensive moderate-intensity work before peak dynamic loads (PDF p. 369 / printed p. 358). Inference: a jumper's year would open with weeks of moderate-volume, lower-impact jumping and strength work before depth jumps.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]], [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[claim--ligament-tendon-adaptation-and-detraining-response]], [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]], [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]], [[framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions]] (6.4, training potential as relative to current fitness), [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]] (5.4.4, replacing means as their effect fades; the warning against premature highly stimulating means is the Ch 6 counterpart), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]]\n##### claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.3\"\n     7\ttopic: strength for endurance athletes\n     8\tcategory: [endurance, middle-distance, strength-endurance, jumping, benchmarks]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Endurance Athletes Need Specialised Strength and Jump Work: The Authors' 800 m Benchmarks\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors argue that \"the importance of muscular adaptation to intense and prolonged work invariably is underestimated\" and that \"specialisation of the muscles, as the direct executor of the work, is of greater significance than development of the cardiovascular-respiratory component of endurance. A more intense stimulus is necessary for promoting adaptation of the muscles to endurance work than for producing cardiovascular and respiratory conditioning.\" Long-duration work alone can leave \"a definite imbalance\" between cardiorespiratory function and \"the working potential of the muscles,\" and then \"the anticipated sporting proficiency will not result\" (PDF pp. 367-368 / printed pp. 356-357, checked against the page images). Their illustration: \"This situation is very typical of the preparation of middle-distance runners. Elementary logic decrees that in order to run 800m in 1:45:0, it is necessary to be able to achieve a time of 10.6-10.7 seconds in the 100m. One needs to possess great strength potential to do this, e.g. the ability to execute three consecutive standing long jumps covering a total distance of at least 9m, or ten consecutive standing long jumps totalling 33-34m, for which middle-distance runners usually do not have the strength\" (PDF p. 368). The remedy: \"specialised strength and jump training which exerts a stronger influence on the muscles than distance training,\" raising local muscular endurance including \"'anaerobic' energy production,\" so that \"it is possible to reduce the volume of exhausting endurance work in the 'anaerobic' glycolytic zone (Zhdanovich, 1980; Verkhoshansky & Sirenko, 1982)\" (same page). Such work should \"firstly increase strength up to a definite optimum, and secondly, exploit the specific energy processes crucial for producing repeated motor actions\"; \"The main thing is to overcome the archaic viewpoint which regards strength training as a means of increasing absolute strength alone\" (same page). Earlier, in italics: \"It is undoubtedly important to increase strength, but this is not the fundamental and sole task of strength training\" (PDF p. 367).\n    17\t\n    18\tThe passage follows directly from the authors' rule for choosing SPP means (6.9.3, PDF p. 367 / printed p. 356, checked against the page image). \"In practice the external similarity of the training means to the fundamental sport exercise is often overemphasized, whereas the importance of the similarity of the means to the regime of muscular work and to the mechanism of its energy production is underemphasized\"; barbell squats \"bear little resemblance\" to running, throwing or basketball, but \"the use of squats is justified.\" Then: \"the need to preserve the training potential of the loading (especially for highly qualified athletes) requires using means that not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions, with regard to maximum force, time of maximum force development and the power of the metabolic processes which determine work-capacity. On this basis, it is easy to conclude that satisfaction of this requirement should be associated with improving the strength component of the movement\" (same page). Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side: \"supplementary or 'cross' training cannot simply be used in a very general way,\" \"muscle endurance gained through supplementary running training does not mean that it will directly improve the performance of a competitive rower or cyclist,\" and \"The emergence of specificity from an array of non-specific fitness qualities, however, is not supported by current physiological and biochemical research\" (PDF p. 363 / printed p. 352, checked against the page image).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: strength and jump work loads the muscles harder than distance running, \"thereby intensifying their adaptation to endurance work and activating the relevant functional reserves,\" enhancing \"the power and the capacity of the metabolic processes\" (PDF p. 368).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Endurance or mixed-demand athletes whose conditioning is mostly long-duration running: add specialised strength and jump work aimed at repeated efforts, and expect to need less glycolytic volume. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery ... not distance capacity\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors make the converse point for endurance athletes, that strength and power support the endurance event; together they argue against treating strength and conditioning as separate silos, as does the baseline's \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (line 122, Explicit).\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — The 800 m benchmarks are introduced as \"Elementary logic decrees\"; they are asserted, not measured, and no study links the jump distances to 800 m performance. The cited studies are named without data. Inference: the benchmarks are for elite men's 800 m (1:45.0) and say nothing about lower standards or women's events.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: an 800 m runner aiming at 1:45.0 should, by their reasoning, be able to run 100 m in 10.6-10.7 s and cover at least 9 m in three consecutive standing long jumps or 33-34 m in ten, which \"middle-distance runners usually do not have the strength\" to do (PDF p. 368 / printed p. 357).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (Fig 6.30, ten-jump gains in middle-distance runners after a strength block), [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]] (4.3.3, training effort should match or exceed the sport's), [[heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement]], [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (PDF p. 363 sends the reader to \"the different types of specificity discussed in 1.3\"; in this edition specificity is section 1.4, PDF p. 38, text layer, so Inference: the cross-reference is off by one section)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0e3cd187-a6a3-4253-ba78-ba499227b7dd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:59:02.494Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: principle\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.4\"\n     7\ttopic: function of training volume\n     8\tcategory: [volume, intensity, long-term-adaptation, connective-tissue, qualification]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reaction, and then to the development of long-term adaptation of the body. Therefore, in each successive annual cycle athletes should execute larger volumes of loading (within optimal limits) to raise and maintain the special work-capacity\" (PDF p. 369 / printed p. 358, checked against the page image). Second, in sports with \"brief, highly concentrated efforts\": \"the mechanical integrity of the ligamentous-joint apparatus can become the limiting factor to the progress of sporting performance\" (italics in the original). \"If these connective tissues are not prepared by extensive work of moderate intensity, then the great dynamic overloading encountered at the level of peak performance can lead to severe joint injury. This occurs frequently in gymnastics, weightlifting, high jumping and triple jumping\" (same page).\n    17\t\n    18\tVolume alone is not enough: \"the influence of the volume can be determined correctly only if one also takes into consideration the magnitude of the loading, its duration and intensity\" (PDF p. 369). Magnitude is set \"first of all, on the basis of the athlete's qualification\": the higher the qualification, the larger the yearly and stage volume, and the share of \"specialised loading\" grows (Panov 1975; Zhikharevich 1976; Vankov 1977; Matveyev 1977; Gilyazova 1978). The annual volume is \"established individually\" from the preceding stages; \"A more serious task\" is the optimal volume per month, stage or phase (same page). On intensity: it \"determines the strength and specificity of its effect on the body,\" regulates \"the training potential of the given means,\" frequency and intervals, and \"the ratio of the volume of the loading divided by the time taken to reach the maximum load in a given stage,\" a measure of \"loading concentration relative to time.\" \"Intensification of loading is permitted during specific stages in the annual cycle and only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading (see Fig 6.1)\" (PDF p. 370 / printed p. 359, checked against the page image). The same caution closes the preceding passage on training potential: \"The training potential of the means employed decreases as the special work capacity increases,\" so more effective means must be introduced over time (Fig 6.28); \"However, it is inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely, because the body is functionally unprepared for them, a situation that can cause excessive intensification of training and disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\" (PDF pp. 368-369 / printed pp. 357-358, checked against the page images).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one quoted: prolonged homeostatic disturbance mobilises \"energy resources and plastic reserves\" and moves the body from an acute to a general, then long-term, adaptive reaction (PDF p. 369). For connective tissue they refer back to 1.6 and 1.7.2 and give no further mechanism here.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Building a multi-year plan (raise annual volume, within limits, as qualification rises) and opening each year with extensive moderate-intensity work before intensifying, especially in jumping and other high-impact events. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred (transcript)). Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill ...)\" (line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the authors' \"extensive low intensity loading\" before intensification is close to the work-capacity base of that hierarchy.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — \"within optimal limits\" is not quantified, and the rule to raise volume every year cannot run indefinitely; the authors elsewhere set a limit by current adaptation reserves (PDF p. 370) and an optimum above which the LDTE pattern appears (PDF p. 374, [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]). The joint-injury link is stated as frequent in four sports with no data. Inference: in American collegiate football, where many athletes are four- or five-year players, a rising-volume rule would need to be weighed against Eric's baseline that recovery is handled \"through training-load design alone\" (line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)), since nothing else would catch an overshoot. The book's 4.1.2 passage gives volume two different functions (a functional base and endurance development; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]); Inference: the two lists overlap on the \"base\" role and differ on the second, so read them together rather than as rival definitions.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: gymnastics, weightlifting, high jumping and triple jumping, where joint injury \"occurs frequently\" if connective tissue has not been prepared by extensive moderate-intensity work before peak dynamic loads (PDF p. 369 / printed p. 358). Inference: a jumper's year would open with weeks of moderate-volume, lower-impact jumping and strength work before depth jumps.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]], [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[claim--ligament-tendon-adaptation-and-detraining-response]], [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]], [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]], [[framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions]] (6.4, training potential as relative to current fitness), [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]] (5.4.4, replacing means as their effect fades; the warning against premature highly stimulating means is the Ch 6 counterpart), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]]\n##### claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"6.9.3\"\n     7\ttopic: strength for endurance athletes\n     8\tcategory: [endurance, middle-distance, strength-endurance, jumping, benchmarks]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Endurance Athletes Need Specialised Strength and Jump Work: The Authors' 800 m Benchmarks\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors argue that \"the importance of muscular adaptation to intense and prolonged work invariably is underestimated\" and that \"specialisation of the muscles, as the direct executor of the work, is of greater significance than development of the cardiovascular-respiratory component of endurance. A more intense stimulus is necessary for promoting adaptation of the muscles to endurance work than for producing cardiovascular and respiratory conditioning.\" Long-duration work alone can leave \"a definite imbalance\" between cardiorespiratory function and \"the working potential of the muscles,\" and then \"the anticipated sporting proficiency will not result\" (PDF pp. 367-368 / printed pp. 356-357, checked against the page images). Their illustration: \"This situation is very typical of the preparation of middle-distance runners. Elementary logic decrees that in order to run 800m in 1:45:0, it is necessary to be able to achieve a time of 10.6-10.7 seconds in the 100m. One needs to possess great strength potential to do this, e.g. the ability to execute three consecutive standing long jumps covering a total distance of at least 9m, or ten consecutive standing long jumps totalling 33-34m, for which middle-distance runners usually do not have the strength\" (PDF p. 368). The remedy: \"specialised strength and jump training which exerts a stronger influence on the muscles than distance training,\" raising local muscular endurance including \"'anaerobic' energy production,\" so that \"it is possible to reduce the volume of exhausting endurance work in the 'anaerobic' glycolytic zone (Zhdanovich, 1980; Verkhoshansky & Sirenko, 1982)\" (same page). Such work should \"firstly increase strength up to a definite optimum, and secondly, exploit the specific energy processes crucial for producing repeated motor actions\"; \"The main thing is to overcome the archaic viewpoint which regards strength training as a means of increasing absolute strength alone\" (same page). Earlier, in italics: \"It is undoubtedly important to increase strength, but this is not the fundamental and sole task of strength training\" (PDF p. 367).\n    17\t\n    18\tThe passage follows directly from the authors' rule for choosing SPP means (6.9.3, PDF p. 367 / printed p. 356, checked against the page image). \"In practice the external similarity of the training means to the fundamental sport exercise is often overemphasized, whereas the importance of the similarity of the means to the regime of muscular work and to the mechanism of its energy production is underemphasized\"; barbell squats \"bear little resemblance\" to running, throwing or basketball, but \"the use of squats is justified.\" Then: \"the need to preserve the training potential of the loading (especially for highly qualified athletes) requires using means that not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions, with regard to maximum force, time of maximum force development and the power of the metabolic processes which determine work-capacity. On this basis, it is easy to conclude that satisfaction of this requirement should be associated with improving the strength component of the movement\" (same page). Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side: \"supplementary or 'cross' training cannot simply be used in a very general way,\" \"muscle endurance gained through supplementary running training does not mean that it will directly improve the performance of a competitive rower or cyclist,\" and \"The emergence of specificity from an array of non-specific fitness qualities, however, is not supported by current physiological and biochemical research\" (PDF p. 363 / printed p. 352, checked against the page image).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism: strength and jump work loads the muscles harder than distance running, \"thereby intensifying their adaptation to endurance work and activating the relevant functional reserves,\" enhancing \"the power and the capacity of the metabolic processes\" (PDF p. 368).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Endurance or mixed-demand athletes whose conditioning is mostly long-duration running: add specialised strength and jump work aimed at repeated efforts, and expect to need less glycolytic volume. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery ... not distance capacity\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors make the converse point for endurance athletes, that strength and power support the endurance event; together they argue against treating strength and conditioning as separate silos, as does the baseline's \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (line 122, Explicit).\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — The 800 m benchmarks are introduced as \"Elementary logic decrees\"; they are asserted, not measured, and no study links the jump distances to 800 m performance. The cited studies are named without data. Inference: the benchmarks are for elite men's 800 m (1:45.0) and say nothing about lower standards or women's events.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own: an 800 m runner aiming at 1:45.0 should, by their reasoning, be able to run 100 m in 10.6-10.7 s and cover at least 9 m in three consecutive standing long jumps or 33-34 m in ten, which \"middle-distance runners usually do not have the strength\" to do (PDF p. 368 / printed p. 357).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]], [[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]] (Fig 6.30, ten-jump gains in middle-distance runners after a strength block), [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]] (4.3.3, training effort should match or exceed the sport's), [[heuristic--dynamic-correspondence-means-matching-motor-characteristics-not-copying-the-sport-movement]], [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (PDF p. 363 sends the reader to \"the different types of specificity discussed in 1.3\"; in this edition specificity is section 1.4, PDF p. 38, text layer, so Inference: the cross-reference is off by one section)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"618462c7-a67a-4a33-9f5b-73e8101cfaee","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
