ntrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a90e8720-8e84-498c-852b-bb1762054c0b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a651eb06-5033-46b7-b765-1ac5222002ab","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KvDWjgFNRRPQJjvs7bdhsT","type":"tool_result","content":"SUPERTRAINING ADDENDUM\n9.1 Laws of Sports Mastery: Principles of Training\nDr. Yuri Verkhoshansky \nTranslated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis \nWith assistance from Natalia Verkhoshansky\nIntroduction\nAs established many years ago (Verkhoshansky, 13,18), the multi year Process of Achieving \nSports Mastery (PASM) was developed on a base of definite laws. Athletes can achieve the \nheights of sports mastery by different pathways and expend different amounts of time doing this. \nHowever, the path taken by each athlete will have general traits, inherent to all athletes. These \ngeneral characteristics are determined by the biological nature of man: genetically imposed abili­\nties of the body to accommodate to the changing conditions of his existence (Verkhoshansky, \n13,18, 26, 27). It is mainly because of this that specialists in the area of sports science have been \ngiving more attention to biological knowledge (Verkhoshansky, 17, 26, 27, Viru, 32, 291, \nYakovlev, 143, 144, Viru. Kirge, 35, Tschine, 277, Neuman, 253). They are striving to find the \nkey to further deepening the principles of preparation of high level athletes.\nRussian (Yakovlev, 143, 144, Viru, 32, 60, 62, Gippenreiter, 43, Medvedev, 73, Kasnacheev, 59, \nGarkavi. 42) and foreign (Booth, 160, Darnel et al., 186, Williams et al., 298, Underwood, Wil­\nliams, 278, Seedorf et al., 269, Galbo. 203) representatives of the biological sciences (including \nmolecular and cellular biology), see possibilities for using their achievements and methods to \n\n#### 514\nSUPERTRAINING ADDENDUM\n9. ADDENDUM\n503\n#### 536\nSUPERTRAINING ADDENDUM\ntremely concentrated loads lead to a more significant decrease in functional indices and to a \ndisruption in adaptation (graph fj).\nWhen using concentrated loads orientated to achieving the LDTE, the methods used should \nnot be extremely intense. Concentration, in one of the single purpose loads, is already in­\ntensification of the training effect and to increase it with highly intense means of training \ndoes not follow.\nRealization of LDTE of concentrated loads is served by moderate increases in work volume \nand gradually increasing intensity (B).\n. \nThe duration of the appearance of LDTE is determined by the volume and duration of the \nconcentrated loads used. In principle, the steady appearance of LDTE (t2 ) is equal to the du­\nration of the stage (ti). In real conditions of training high level athletes, the tendency has \nbeen for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks. It follows, however, to keep in mind \nthat the appearance of LDTE has an independent character and to a significant degree, de­\npends upon the ability of the athlete to withstand volume loads and the ability of his body \nfor recovery. The same volume loads in two athletes having the same qualifications can \nhave a different effect.\n>. \nIn the period of LDTE realization, volume concentrated loads are handled by the body rela­\ntively easily and without harm to the body, but intense loads negatively react to the work \nvolume. The latter is expressed by a slowdown in increases and even a decrease in the func­\ntional abilities. For example. Figure 8 shows that in triple jumpers, after the concentrated \nspecialized strength preparation loads in March-April, the LDTE acquired showed an in­\ncrease in speed-strength and increased sports results in May-June.\niowever, an insignificant increase in their volume in specialized strength preparation (by only a \new percent) in order to “maintain” the achieved level of specialized workability (as believed by \nhe athlete) led to a fast decrease in speed-strength and sports results. As a result of this, the ath- \nete lost his chance to be selected in the qualifying competitions for a place on the national team, \nfhe phenomenon of LDTE of concentrated loads should be looked at as an important base qual-\nFigure 7 - The principal scheme of the Long Lasting training effect of the concentrated strength loads:\nA - the stage of the concentrated loads; ti - duration of this stage;\nB - the stage of the specific training loads with the gradual increase of their intensity; U - duration of this stage; \nCurves fi, U and fs - the three possible variants of the athlete’s body response to the training loads influence \nthrough the change of the functional parameters level (f).\n525\n#### 305\n2-Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual worko \n3-but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has sho \n4:that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is \n5-gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle. Moreover, the effectiveness of the conjugate \n6-sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9e44fd4b-9515-4b4b-bcb1-35584d2adcf4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:59:39.239Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"SUPERTRAINING ADDENDUM\n9.1 Laws of Sports Mastery: Principles of Training\nDr. Yuri Verkhoshansky \nTranslated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis \nWith assistance from Natalia Verkhoshansky\nIntroduction\nAs established many years ago (Verkhoshansky, 13,18), the multi year Process of Achieving \nSports Mastery (PASM) was developed on a base of definite laws. Athletes can achieve the \nheights of sports mastery by different pathways and expend different amounts of time doing this. \nHowever, the path taken by each athlete will have general traits, inherent to all athletes. These \ngeneral characteristics are determined by the biological nature of man: genetically imposed abili­\nties of the body to accommodate to the changing conditions of his existence (Verkhoshansky, \n13,18, 26, 27). It is mainly because of this that specialists in the area of sports science have been \ngiving more attention to biological knowledge (Verkhoshansky, 17, 26, 27, Viru, 32, 291, \nYakovlev, 143, 144, Viru. Kirge, 35, Tschine, 277, Neuman, 253). They are striving to find the \nkey to further deepening the principles of preparation of high level athletes.\nRussian (Yakovlev, 143, 144, Viru, 32, 60, 62, Gippenreiter, 43, Medvedev, 73, Kasnacheev, 59, \nGarkavi. 42) and foreign (Booth, 160, Darnel et al., 186, Williams et al., 298, Underwood, Wil­\nliams, 278, Seedorf et al., 269, Galbo. 203) representatives of the biological sciences (including \nmolecular and cellular biology), see possibilities for using their achievements and methods to \n\n#### 514\nSUPERTRAINING ADDENDUM\n9. ADDENDUM\n503\n#### 536\nSUPERTRAINING ADDENDUM\ntremely concentrated loads lead to a more significant decrease in functional indices and to a \ndisruption in adaptation (graph fj).\nWhen using concentrated loads orientated to achieving the LDTE, the methods used should \nnot be extremely intense. Concentration, in one of the single purpose loads, is already in­\ntensification of the training effect and to increase it with highly intense means of training \ndoes not follow.\nRealization of LDTE of concentrated loads is served by moderate increases in work volume \nand gradually increasing intensity (B).\n. \nThe duration of the appearance of LDTE is determined by the volume and duration of the \nconcentrated loads used. In principle, the steady appearance of LDTE (t2 ) is equal to the du­\nration of the stage (ti). In real conditions of training high level athletes, the tendency has \nbeen for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks. It follows, however, to keep in mind \nthat the appearance of LDTE has an independent character and to a significant degree, de­\npends upon the ability of the athlete to withstand volume loads and the ability of his body \nfor recovery. The same volume loads in two athletes having the same qualifications can \nhave a different effect.\n>. \nIn the period of LDTE realization, volume concentrated loads are handled by the body rela­\ntively easily and without harm to the body, but intense loads negatively react to the work \nvolume. The latter is expressed by a slowdown in increases and even a decrease in the func­\ntional abilities. For example. Figure 8 shows that in triple jumpers, after the concentrated \nspecialized strength preparation loads in March-April, the LDTE acquired showed an in­\ncrease in speed-strength and increased sports results in May-June.\niowever, an insignificant increase in their volume in specialized strength preparation (by only a \new percent) in order to “maintain” the achieved level of specialized workability (as believed by \nhe athlete) led to a fast decrease in speed-strength and sports results. As a result of this, the ath- \nete lost his chance to be selected in the qualifying competitions for a place on the national team, \nfhe phenomenon of LDTE of concentrated loads should be looked at as an important base qual-\nFigure 7 - The principal scheme of the Long Lasting training effect of the concentrated strength loads:\nA - the stage of the concentrated loads; ti - duration of this stage;\nB - the stage of the specific training loads with the gradual increase of their intensity; U - duration of this stage; \nCurves fi, U and fs - the three possible variants of the athlete’s body response to the training loads influence \nthrough the change of the functional parameters level (f).\n525\n#### 305\n2-Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual worko \n3-but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has sho \n4:that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is \n5-gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle. Moreover, the effectiveness of the conjugate \n6-sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a90e8720-8e84-498c-852b-bb1762054c0b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
