ble-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state; do echo \"##### $f\"; sed -n '14,40p' $f.md; done","description":"Read five GO entries for spot check"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffpgJ6UaGFokwqNcCoNd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ff35e453-58ac-4eef-b690-ee2890d444cf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:03:18.844Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ff35e453-58ac-4eef-b690-ee2890d444cf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fdy8p7QKQuDWziDCUfhPWP","type":"tool_result","content":"##### framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading\n# Which Endurance Loads Help or Hurt the Load That Follows, and How Long Restoration Takes\n\n**What it is** — The authors first warn that for endurance, research on the interaction of loads of different emphasis is \"inadequate\" and \"often so contradictory that it is very difficult to discern definite trends\" (PDF pp. 371-372 / printed pp. 360-361, checked against the page images). The energy-system categories (aerobic, alactic-anaerobic, anaerobic-glycolytic, mixed) \"are so conditional that they permit very broad interpretation\" and \"cannot be strictly applied\" to judging a load's emphasis, and \"no research has analysed the energy processes occurring while the general activity is undoubtedly aerobic, but the postural muscles are strongly contracted\" (PDF p. 372). With that caveat, \"current experimental data indicates\" (PDF p. 372, checked against the page image):\n\nPositive interaction, where \"the previous loading creates favourable conditions for executing the subsequent loading and raises the effect of the training session as a whole\":\n- \"aerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training\"\n- \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"\n- \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training.\"\n\nNegative interaction (Volkov 1975):\n- \"ATP-CP exercises are done after a large amount of any glycolytic work\"\n- \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work.\"\n\nRestoration figures: after \"a large volume of aerobic work,\" restoration of energy resources and endocrine equilibrium \"is prolonged by 2-3 days,\" during which \"a small volume of anaerobic loading\" is appropriate and \"will not negatively influence the restoration of aerobic capacity.\" \"Restoration after moderate volumes of anaerobic loading usually takes 3-8 hours,\" \"very large volumes of anaerobic loading will delay restoration,\" and \"The training effect of ATP-CP workouts deteriorates if the sessions are done without allowing full restoration from the preceding loading\" (Nabatnikova 1972; Volkov 1975; Platonov 1980; PDF p. 372). On the next page they add: \"the rest pause is actually a training means which is just as important as muscular work\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 373 / printed p. 362, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' only stated reason is the one above: the earlier load either sets up or spoils the conditions for the later one. They give no physiological mechanism for each pairing. Inference: the pattern reads as \"keep the highest-power work fresh and put the longer, lower-power work after it,\" but that is a summary, not the book's explanation.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering conditioning within a session or across consecutive days: alactic work before glycolytic or aerobic work; aerobic work after a small glycolytic dose; no alactic speed work after a big glycolytic session; a small anaerobic dose is acceptable in the 2-3 days after a big aerobic session. Eric's baseline: \"All three energy systems ... are always active simultaneously; programming targets the adaptation, not the drill label\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 114, Explicit). Inference: the authors' warning that the categories are \"so conditional\" agrees with that line. Eric's baseline: the football priority stack is \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" (line 116, Explicit; Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the positive pairing \"aerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training\" fits that two-system stack in one session.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors themselves call the endurance evidence inadequate and contradictory, give no sample sizes, and cite only the negative list to one author (Volkov 1975). \"Large,\" \"small\" and \"moderate\" volumes are not quantified. Inference: the 3-8 hour and 2-3 day figures are general restoration estimates, not limits for a given athlete. The positive list is about order within or between sessions; it is not the same as the long-term sequence across stages ([[framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a football off-season day of short acceleration sprints followed by tempo running fits \"aerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training\"; reversing it, with a long glycolytic conditioning test before sprint work, fits the negative case \"ATP-CP exercises are done after a large amount of any glycolytic work.\" The authors give no worked session.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development]], [[heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]] (Issurin's Fig 2.4 also pairs alactic sprint with aerobic endurance), [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--two-a-day-workout-sequencing-by-dominant-ability]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--aerobic-anaerobic-energy-system-transfer-asymmetry]]\n##### case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers\n# High Jumpers (Fig 6.24): Barbell Then Depth Jumps Beat the Reverse; Concurrent Use Wins the First Stage, Then Fades\n\n**What it is** — To separate partial from cumulative training effects, the authors describe an experiment in which \"group 1 began by using barbell exercises, followed by depth-jumps ... Group 2 used the reverse sequence of means. Each group of means were used for three months each. Group 3 used the barbell exercises and the depth-jumps concurrently throughout both 3-month stages. The overall volume of the loading was equivalent in all groups\" (PDF p. 364 / printed p. 353, checked against the page image). The caption names the athletes as \"high jumpers of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 365 / printed p. 354). The three conclusions (PDF pp. 364-365, checked against the page images):\n1. \"The partial TE of the depth-jumping is greater than that of the barbell exercises.\"\n2. \"The sequence of using barbell exercises followed by depth-jumps (group 1) produced a higher level of speed-strength preparedness (a positive cumulative TE), than the reverse sequence (group 2). In group 2 the TE was essentially lower at the end of the second stage than after the first (a negative cumulative TE).\"\n3. \"The concurrent use of the above means in the first stage (group 3) yielded a larger cumulative effect than in groups 1 and 2. However, the TE noticeably decreased for group 3 during the second stage because the lengthy use of a single means decreases the training potential of the loading.\"\n\n**Figure 6.24 values** (read by eye from a 300 dpi crop of PDF p. 365; white bar after the first 3 months, shaded bar after the next 3): absolute strength GP1 about 36 then 54, GP2 about 50 then 45, GP3 about 63 then 53; explosive strength GP1 about 54 then 102, GP2 about 88 then 78, GP3 about 123 then 96; vertical jump GP1 about 14 then 25, GP2 about 15-16 then 14, GP3 about 33 then 23. At the end, group 1 is about level with or slightly above group 3 on all three measures. **Unclear units:** the caption says \"All values in % increase over initial values\" and describes graph 1 as plantarflexor absolute strength, graph 2 as \"maximal explosive effort in a vertical jump\" and graph 3 as \"the increase in height after a depth jump,\" but the panels are titled Absolute Strength (kgf), Explosive Strength (kgf) and Vertical Jump (cm). The numbers above are axis readings; which unit is correct is not stated.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for group 3's fall is the one quoted: \"lengthy use of a single means decreases the training potential of the loading.\" For the order effect, the companion passage on PDF p. 372 gives their mechanism: extensive resistance work first produces \"a rise in the general energy potential of the neuromuscular system,\" creating \"favourable conditions\" for the explosive work ([[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]]). Inference: group 3 used the two means \"concurrently,\" so \"a single means\" in conclusion 3 most likely refers to the same complex used for six months; the book does not say.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering an off-season for jumpers or other speed-strength athletes: heavy resistance stage first, depth-jump stage second; if both are used together, change the complex after one stage rather than running it unchanged for two. Eric's baseline: \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 180, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — No sample sizes, statistics or variance are given, and the unit mismatch above means the size of each change is uncertain. The design matches the Chapter 5 novice experiment 3 almost exactly (weights then depth jumps, the reverse, and both in both stages), but Chapter 5 labels those subjects novices (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]) while this figure says high jumpers of medium qualification. Inference: they may be the same study described twice or two studies; the book does not say, and the researcher names partly overlap (Khodykin and Chernousov are cited on PDF p. 364 and named in the Chapter 5 list). The two accounts agree on direction. Inference: group 3's first-stage advantage reads against the authors' advice elsewhere to concentrate rather than mix for highly qualified athletes, but these subjects were of medium qualification, for whom the authors say concurrent and distributed approaches both work (PDF p. 371).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after the second 3-month stage, the barbell-first group reached about 102 on explosive strength against about 78 for the depth-jump-first group, whose value had fallen from about 88 after stage 1 (Fig 6.24, PDF p. 365; axis readings, units unclear).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]], [[case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]]\n##### case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners\n# Sirenko (1980): Same Running, Two Concentrated Strength Peaks Instead of One, Better 800 m and 1500 m Gains\n\n**What it is** — To show that a runner's aerobic and anaerobic state \"depends on the contents and organisation of the training loads,\" the authors report Sirenko (1980): two groups of middle-distance runners over an annual cycle, each with \"the same volume and distribution of running means,\" but \"Group 1 used a traditional single-peak distribution of strength-means, whereas Group 2 used a double-peaked distribution of means with the emphasis concentrated in December and March-April\" (PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, checked against the page image). \"Group 2 proved superior in all tests of functional ability, including aerobic efficiency, 'anaerobic threshold' (onset of blood lactate accumulation) and the results of control runs of 400m and 1200m, as well as in their sporting performances. During the summer competition stage Group 2 improved the 800m time by an average of 4.8 seconds and the 1500m run by 6.3 seconds, whereas in Group 1, the improvements were 3.4 and 4.6 seconds, respectively\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present the study as evidence that content and organisation, not running volume alone, set the fitness state; they give no mechanism for why two concentrated strength peaks beat one. Inference: it fits their account of concentrated loading and the delayed rise that follows it ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]), and the LDTE of concentrated strength loading in middle-distance runners that they report from Zhdanovich (1980) on PDF p. 374.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning strength work for endurance athletes with two competition seasons: concentrate the strength means before each, rather than spreading them in one long wave. Inference: the design held running constant, so it isolates the strength distribution, which makes it a cleaner case than most in the chapter.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no group sizes, athlete level, statistics or description of the strength means themselves. \"Single-peak\" and \"double-peaked\" describe the shape of the strength-means curve over the year; the timing of the single peak is not given. Inference: the result is in middle-distance runners with winter and summer seasons; it does not directly address a sport with one long competitive season such as college football.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Group 2 (strength means concentrated in December and March-April) improved 800 m by an average of 4.8 s and 1500 m by 6.3 s in the summer competition stage, against 3.4 s and 4.6 s for Group 1 (PDF p. 378).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state]], [[claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]] (also cites Sirenko), [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]\n##### case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high\n# Triple Jumpers: Jumps Plus Barbell Work Depress the Plantarflexors While the Thigh Extensors Read High\n\n**What it is** — Observing annual dynamics, the authors report that \"the speed-strength of thigh extension, knee extension and plantarflexion can show different trends of change at certain training stages\" (italics in the original), with \"the dynamics of the explosive strength of triple jumpers\" as the example (Verkhoshansky 1988; PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, checked against the page image). \"The concurrent use of jumping and barbell exercises exerts a more intense local influence on the plantarflexors than other muscles of the lower extremity and, consequently, depresses their functional state. However, the loading is not as intense on the thigh extensors, so that their functional indicators reflect a high value. In this case, the athlete notices that movement coordination is difficult although, on the whole, he feels perfectly fit.\" Their prescription: when means are chosen without regard to \"the differences in their primary emphasis on the specific muscle groups,\" the muscles' state may not match the sport's needs, harming \"technical preparation\" and \"preparedness for competition,\" so \"it is necessary to regulate the emphasis of the loading on the plantarflexors, and periodically decrease the loading on them, especially during the technical and immediate pre-competition training stages\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is unequal local load: the jump-plus-barbell combination hits the plantarflexors harder than the thigh extensors (PDF p. 378). They give no data on the size or timing of the difference and no physiological mechanism beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — When an athlete reports that coordination feels off while overall fitness feels fine, or when a whole-leg test looks normal but jumping technique is deteriorating. Eric's baseline: daily CMJ mRSI is the department's readiness marker (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a two-leg CMJ is driven heavily by the hip and knee extensors, so by this case a depressed plantarflexor state could hide behind a normal CMJ; a calf-specific test would be the place to look. Eric's baseline: \"The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks 'Mid-Thigh Pull' setting\" (line 78, Inferred (protocol)). Inference: that existing test could check this case, though the book does not mention isometric testing for it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One cited observation in one event, with no numbers shown in the text; treat it as a hypothesis. Inference: whether a CMJ would miss the plantarflexor drop is untested here; the book does not discuss jump tests in this passage.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: triple jumpers doing concurrent jumping and barbell work, whose plantarflexors were depressed while thigh-extensor indicators read high, and who reported difficult coordination while feeling fit; the fix was to cut plantarflexor loading periodically, above all in technical and pre-competition stages (PDF p. 378).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification]], [[framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state]], [[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]] (plantarflexor strength as an outcome), [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]\n##### framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state\n# Three Levels of Change in Fitness State: Long-Term (Volume), Medium-Term (Content), Short-Term (Acute and Largely Random)\n\n**What it is** — From Sirenko's study and \"other studies of the influence of training loads on functional indicators,\" the authors identify \"three levels of change in an athlete's fitness state during training\" (PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, checked against the page image):\n1. \"A long-term level characterised by the steady rise in the athlete's special work-capacity,\" over \"one or more years,\" based on \"long-term adaptation, promoted chiefly by the volume of loading.\"\n2. \"A medium-term level characterised by less stable and readily reversible functional changes, the basis for which is compensatory adaptation,\" over \"lengthy training stages of up to 5-6 months,\" produced with volume by \"the characteristics of its contents, in particular, the proportion of intensive means in the total volume of loading.\"\n3. \"A short-term level characterised by brief, but not precisely measurable, changes in the functional indicators,\" the \"acute reaction to the training load\" within the microcycle, elicited by \"the magnitude of the load, the intensity of loading, the individual ability of the athlete to endure the loading and his restorative capabilities.\" \"At this level, the dynamics of the functional indicators are largely random and at times unpredictable. This level is not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels and is important only for organisation of the microcycle and achievement of the immediate effect of the current loading.\"\n\nTheir conclusion: \"the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state during the annual cycle are controllable,\" so training should be built \"by first of all taking into account the dynamics of the athlete's special physical preparedness (SPP)\" (PDF p. 378). \"The art of controlling the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state consists, firstly, of achieving the necessary level of conditioning and, secondly, achieving it at the required time\"; \"Research shows that achievement of the second requirement is not especially complex. However, the probability of successfully achieving the first requirement is much smaller\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368, text layer; page image not opened).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors say the dependence of fitness on load \"is determined by definite adaptive processes\" and name the basis of each level (long-term adaptation, compensatory adaptation, acute reaction). They do not explain why the short-term level is independent of the other two.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding which signal to act on: microcycle-level swings guide the week's organisation only; stage-level trends reflect the content and share of intensive means; year-on-year trends reflect volume. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that split maps onto the authors' levels 1-2 versus level 3. Eric's baseline: daily jumps \"build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week\" (line 255, Tension 12 status, resolved 2026-10-02 by Eric). Inference: using a rolling baseline and SWC is one way to separate the \"largely random\" short-term level from a real trend.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The time spans (one or more years, up to 5-6 months, microcycle) are given without data. The claim that timing a peak is \"not especially complex\" is cited to unnamed research (\"Research shows\"), with no study named. Inference: calling short-term changes \"not precisely measurable\" reflects the authors' tools; it does not by itself mean modern daily testing carries no signal, though the authors would limit its use to microcycle decisions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own anchor: Sirenko's (1980) middle-distance runners, where the same running volume with a different distribution of strength means changed the stage-level and competition results ([[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]]; PDF p. 378). Inference: a drop in one Wednesday mRSI read after a heavy Monday is level-3 noise unless it persists across reads; a decline across a six-week stage points to the stage's content.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification]], [[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]], [[framework--work-capacity-fitness-preparedness]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--three-training-effects]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9381c746-4330-4923-b0e3-1eba7dac2bfd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:03:19.061Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"##### framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading\n# Which Endurance Loads Help or Hurt the Load That Follows, and How Long Restoration Takes\n\n**What it is** — The authors first warn that for endurance, research on the interaction of loads of different emphasis is \"inadequate\" and \"often so contradictory that it is very difficult to discern definite trends\" (PDF pp. 371-372 / printed pp. 360-361, checked against the page images). The energy-system categories (aerobic, alactic-anaerobic, anaerobic-glycolytic, mixed) \"are so conditional that they permit very broad interpretation\" and \"cannot be strictly applied\" to judging a load's emphasis, and \"no research has analysed the energy processes occurring while the general activity is undoubtedly aerobic, but the postural muscles are strongly contracted\" (PDF p. 372). With that caveat, \"current experimental data indicates\" (PDF p. 372, checked against the page image):\n\nPositive interaction, where \"the previous loading creates favourable conditions for executing the subsequent loading and raises the effect of the training session as a whole\":\n- \"aerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training\"\n- \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"\n- \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training.\"\n\nNegative interaction (Volkov 1975):\n- \"ATP-CP exercises are done after a large amount of any glycolytic work\"\n- \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work.\"\n\nRestoration figures: after \"a large volume of aerobic work,\" restoration of energy resources and endocrine equilibrium \"is prolonged by 2-3 days,\" during which \"a small volume of anaerobic loading\" is appropriate and \"will not negatively influence the restoration of aerobic capacity.\" \"Restoration after moderate volumes of anaerobic loading usually takes 3-8 hours,\" \"very large volumes of anaerobic loading will delay restoration,\" and \"The training effect of ATP-CP workouts deteriorates if the sessions are done without allowing full restoration from the preceding loading\" (Nabatnikova 1972; Volkov 1975; Platonov 1980; PDF p. 372). On the next page they add: \"the rest pause is actually a training means which is just as important as muscular work\" (italics in the original; PDF p. 373 / printed p. 362, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' only stated reason is the one above: the earlier load either sets up or spoils the conditions for the later one. They give no physiological mechanism for each pairing. Inference: the pattern reads as \"keep the highest-power work fresh and put the longer, lower-power work after it,\" but that is a summary, not the book's explanation.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering conditioning within a session or across consecutive days: alactic work before glycolytic or aerobic work; aerobic work after a small glycolytic dose; no alactic speed work after a big glycolytic session; a small anaerobic dose is acceptable in the 2-3 days after a big aerobic session. Eric's baseline: \"All three energy systems ... are always active simultaneously; programming targets the adaptation, not the drill label\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 114, Explicit). Inference: the authors' warning that the categories are \"so conditional\" agrees with that line. Eric's baseline: the football priority stack is \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" (line 116, Explicit; Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: the positive pairing \"aerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training\" fits that two-system stack in one session.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors themselves call the endurance evidence inadequate and contradictory, give no sample sizes, and cite only the negative list to one author (Volkov 1975). \"Large,\" \"small\" and \"moderate\" volumes are not quantified. Inference: the 3-8 hour and 2-3 day figures are general restoration estimates, not limits for a given athlete. The positive list is about order within or between sessions; it is not the same as the long-term sequence across stages ([[framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a football off-season day of short acceleration sprints followed by tempo running fits \"aerobic exercises are done after ATP-CP training\"; reversing it, with a long glycolytic conditioning test before sprint work, fits the negative case \"ATP-CP exercises are done after a large amount of any glycolytic work.\" The authors give no worked session.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development]], [[heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout]] (Issurin's Fig 2.4 also pairs alactic sprint with aerobic endurance), [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--two-a-day-workout-sequencing-by-dominant-ability]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--aerobic-anaerobic-energy-system-transfer-asymmetry]]\n##### case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers\n# High Jumpers (Fig 6.24): Barbell Then Depth Jumps Beat the Reverse; Concurrent Use Wins the First Stage, Then Fades\n\n**What it is** — To separate partial from cumulative training effects, the authors describe an experiment in which \"group 1 began by using barbell exercises, followed by depth-jumps ... Group 2 used the reverse sequence of means. Each group of means were used for three months each. Group 3 used the barbell exercises and the depth-jumps concurrently throughout both 3-month stages. The overall volume of the loading was equivalent in all groups\" (PDF p. 364 / printed p. 353, checked against the page image). The caption names the athletes as \"high jumpers of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 365 / printed p. 354). The three conclusions (PDF pp. 364-365, checked against the page images):\n1. \"The partial TE of the depth-jumping is greater than that of the barbell exercises.\"\n2. \"The sequence of using barbell exercises followed by depth-jumps (group 1) produced a higher level of speed-strength preparedness (a positive cumulative TE), than the reverse sequence (group 2). In group 2 the TE was essentially lower at the end of the second stage than after the first (a negative cumulative TE).\"\n3. \"The concurrent use of the above means in the first stage (group 3) yielded a larger cumulative effect than in groups 1 and 2. However, the TE noticeably decreased for group 3 during the second stage because the lengthy use of a single means decreases the training potential of the loading.\"\n\n**Figure 6.24 values** (read by eye from a 300 dpi crop of PDF p. 365; white bar after the first 3 months, shaded bar after the next 3): absolute strength GP1 about 36 then 54, GP2 about 50 then 45, GP3 about 63 then 53; explosive strength GP1 about 54 then 102, GP2 about 88 then 78, GP3 about 123 then 96; vertical jump GP1 about 14 then 25, GP2 about 15-16 then 14, GP3 about 33 then 23. At the end, group 1 is about level with or slightly above group 3 on all three measures. **Unclear units:** the caption says \"All values in % increase over initial values\" and describes graph 1 as plantarflexor absolute strength, graph 2 as \"maximal explosive effort in a vertical jump\" and graph 3 as \"the increase in height after a depth jump,\" but the panels are titled Absolute Strength (kgf), Explosive Strength (kgf) and Vertical Jump (cm). The numbers above are axis readings; which unit is correct is not stated.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for group 3's fall is the one quoted: \"lengthy use of a single means decreases the training potential of the loading.\" For the order effect, the companion passage on PDF p. 372 gives their mechanism: extensive resistance work first produces \"a rise in the general energy potential of the neuromuscular system,\" creating \"favourable conditions\" for the explosive work ([[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]]). Inference: group 3 used the two means \"concurrently,\" so \"a single means\" in conclusion 3 most likely refers to the same complex used for six months; the book does not say.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering an off-season for jumpers or other speed-strength athletes: heavy resistance stage first, depth-jump stage second; if both are used together, change the complex after one stage rather than running it unchanged for two. Eric's baseline: \"The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 180, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — No sample sizes, statistics or variance are given, and the unit mismatch above means the size of each change is uncertain. The design matches the Chapter 5 novice experiment 3 almost exactly (weights then depth jumps, the reverse, and both in both stages), but Chapter 5 labels those subjects novices (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]) while this figure says high jumpers of medium qualification. Inference: they may be the same study described twice or two studies; the book does not say, and the researcher names partly overlap (Khodykin and Chernousov are cited on PDF p. 364 and named in the Chapter 5 list). The two accounts agree on direction. Inference: group 3's first-stage advantage reads against the authors' advice elsewhere to concentrate rather than mix for highly qualified athletes, but these subjects were of medium qualification, for whom the authors say concurrent and distributed approaches both work (PDF p. 371).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after the second 3-month stage, the barbell-first group reached about 102 on explosive strength against about 78 for the depth-jump-first group, whose value had fallen from about 88 after stage 1 (Fig 6.24, PDF p. 365; axis readings, units unclear).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive]], [[framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect]], [[case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]]\n##### case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners\n# Sirenko (1980): Same Running, Two Concentrated Strength Peaks Instead of One, Better 800 m and 1500 m Gains\n\n**What it is** — To show that a runner's aerobic and anaerobic state \"depends on the contents and organisation of the training loads,\" the authors report Sirenko (1980): two groups of middle-distance runners over an annual cycle, each with \"the same volume and distribution of running means,\" but \"Group 1 used a traditional single-peak distribution of strength-means, whereas Group 2 used a double-peaked distribution of means with the emphasis concentrated in December and March-April\" (PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, checked against the page image). \"Group 2 proved superior in all tests of functional ability, including aerobic efficiency, 'anaerobic threshold' (onset of blood lactate accumulation) and the results of control runs of 400m and 1200m, as well as in their sporting performances. During the summer competition stage Group 2 improved the 800m time by an average of 4.8 seconds and the 1500m run by 6.3 seconds, whereas in Group 1, the improvements were 3.4 and 4.6 seconds, respectively\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present the study as evidence that content and organisation, not running volume alone, set the fitness state; they give no mechanism for why two concentrated strength peaks beat one. Inference: it fits their account of concentrated loading and the delayed rise that follows it ([[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]]), and the LDTE of concentrated strength loading in middle-distance runners that they report from Zhdanovich (1980) on PDF p. 374.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning strength work for endurance athletes with two competition seasons: concentrate the strength means before each, rather than spreading them in one long wave. Inference: the design held running constant, so it isolates the strength distribution, which makes it a cleaner case than most in the chapter.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no group sizes, athlete level, statistics or description of the strength means themselves. \"Single-peak\" and \"double-peaked\" describe the shape of the strength-means curve over the year; the timing of the single peak is not given. Inference: the result is in middle-distance runners with winter and summer seasons; it does not directly address a sport with one long competitive season such as college football.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Group 2 (strength means concentrated in December and March-April) improved 800 m by an average of 4.8 s and 1500 m by 6.3 s in the summer competition stage, against 3.4 s and 4.6 s for Group 1 (PDF p. 378).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]], [[principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum]], [[framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state]], [[claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks]], [[claim--local-blood-flow-not-vo2max-tracks-competition-period-endurance-gains]] (also cites Sirenko), [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]]\n##### case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high\n# Triple Jumpers: Jumps Plus Barbell Work Depress the Plantarflexors While the Thigh Extensors Read High\n\n**What it is** — Observing annual dynamics, the authors report that \"the speed-strength of thigh extension, knee extension and plantarflexion can show different trends of change at certain training stages\" (italics in the original), with \"the dynamics of the explosive strength of triple jumpers\" as the example (Verkhoshansky 1988; PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, checked against the page image). \"The concurrent use of jumping and barbell exercises exerts a more intense local influence on the plantarflexors than other muscles of the lower extremity and, consequently, depresses their functional state. However, the loading is not as intense on the thigh extensors, so that their functional indicators reflect a high value. In this case, the athlete notices that movement coordination is difficult although, on the whole, he feels perfectly fit.\" Their prescription: when means are chosen without regard to \"the differences in their primary emphasis on the specific muscle groups,\" the muscles' state may not match the sport's needs, harming \"technical preparation\" and \"preparedness for competition,\" so \"it is necessary to regulate the emphasis of the loading on the plantarflexors, and periodically decrease the loading on them, especially during the technical and immediate pre-competition training stages\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is unequal local load: the jump-plus-barbell combination hits the plantarflexors harder than the thigh extensors (PDF p. 378). They give no data on the size or timing of the difference and no physiological mechanism beyond that.\n\n**When to apply** — When an athlete reports that coordination feels off while overall fitness feels fine, or when a whole-leg test looks normal but jumping technique is deteriorating. Eric's baseline: daily CMJ mRSI is the department's readiness marker (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 74, Explicit). Inference: a two-leg CMJ is driven heavily by the hip and knee extensors, so by this case a depressed plantarflexor state could hide behind a normal CMJ; a calf-specific test would be the place to look. Eric's baseline: \"The isometric seated calf raise is run on the ForceDecks 'Mid-Thigh Pull' setting\" (line 78, Inferred (protocol)). Inference: that existing test could check this case, though the book does not mention isometric testing for it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One cited observation in one event, with no numbers shown in the text; treat it as a hypothesis. Inference: whether a CMJ would miss the plantarflexor drop is untested here; the book does not discuss jump tests in this passage.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: triple jumpers doing concurrent jumping and barbell work, whose plantarflexors were depressed while thigh-extensor indicators read high, and who reported difficult coordination while feeling fit; the fix was to cut plantarflexor loading periodically, above all in technical and pre-competition stages (PDF p. 378).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification]], [[framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state]], [[case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers]] (plantarflexor strength as an outcome), [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[case-study--high-jumper-factor-structure-by-qualification]], [[heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window]]\n##### framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state\n# Three Levels of Change in Fitness State: Long-Term (Volume), Medium-Term (Content), Short-Term (Acute and Largely Random)\n\n**What it is** — From Sirenko's study and \"other studies of the influence of training loads on functional indicators,\" the authors identify \"three levels of change in an athlete's fitness state during training\" (PDF p. 378 / printed p. 367, checked against the page image):\n1. \"A long-term level characterised by the steady rise in the athlete's special work-capacity,\" over \"one or more years,\" based on \"long-term adaptation, promoted chiefly by the volume of loading.\"\n2. \"A medium-term level characterised by less stable and readily reversible functional changes, the basis for which is compensatory adaptation,\" over \"lengthy training stages of up to 5-6 months,\" produced with volume by \"the characteristics of its contents, in particular, the proportion of intensive means in the total volume of loading.\"\n3. \"A short-term level characterised by brief, but not precisely measurable, changes in the functional indicators,\" the \"acute reaction to the training load\" within the microcycle, elicited by \"the magnitude of the load, the intensity of loading, the individual ability of the athlete to endure the loading and his restorative capabilities.\" \"At this level, the dynamics of the functional indicators are largely random and at times unpredictable. This level is not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels and is important only for organisation of the microcycle and achievement of the immediate effect of the current loading.\"\n\nTheir conclusion: \"the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state during the annual cycle are controllable,\" so training should be built \"by first of all taking into account the dynamics of the athlete's special physical preparedness (SPP)\" (PDF p. 378). \"The art of controlling the dynamics of the athlete's fitness state consists, firstly, of achieving the necessary level of conditioning and, secondly, achieving it at the required time\"; \"Research shows that achievement of the second requirement is not especially complex. However, the probability of successfully achieving the first requirement is much smaller\" (PDF p. 379 / printed p. 368, text layer; page image not opened).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors say the dependence of fitness on load \"is determined by definite adaptive processes\" and name the basis of each level (long-term adaptation, compensatory adaptation, acute reaction). They do not explain why the short-term level is independent of the other two.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding which signal to act on: microcycle-level swings guide the week's organisation only; stage-level trends reflect the content and share of intensive means; year-on-year trends reflect volume. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that split maps onto the authors' levels 1-2 versus level 3. Eric's baseline: daily jumps \"build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week\" (line 255, Tension 12 status, resolved 2026-10-02 by Eric). Inference: using a rolling baseline and SWC is one way to separate the \"largely random\" short-term level from a real trend.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The time spans (one or more years, up to 5-6 months, microcycle) are given without data. The claim that timing a peak is \"not especially complex\" is cited to unnamed research (\"Research shows\"), with no study named. Inference: calling short-term changes \"not precisely measurable\" reflects the authors' tools; it does not by itself mean modern daily testing carries no signal, though the authors would limit its use to microcycle decisions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own anchor: Sirenko's (1980) middle-distance runners, where the same running volume with a different distribution of strength means changed the stage-level and competition results ([[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]]; PDF p. 378). Inference: a drop in one Wednesday mRSI read after a heavy Monday is level-3 noise unless it persists across reads; a decline across a six-week stage points to the stage's content.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification]], [[case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners]], [[framework--work-capacity-fitness-preparedness]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/heuristic--general-prep-stage-duration-must-match-sports-form-cycle]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--three-training-effects]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ff35e453-58ac-4eef-b690-ee2890d444cf","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
