{"parentUuid":"13646bf9-1cc1-44c9-a83e-03926d6290ba","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CLnktJi2WFqGLK3RZVtTV1","type":"tool_result","content":"headers per file (expect 6):\n  done\nem dashes outside headers:\n  done\nen dashes:\n  done\nwrapped links:\n  done\nreview words:\nclaim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md:22:**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or design for any of the cited studies, and gives no definition here of \"medium\" versus \"highly qualified.\" **Contested across the library:** the vault brief of Issurin (2016) records that he judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, that the reviewed combat and team-sport studies improved fitness measures but did not report team-practice or competition outcomes, and that he placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], brief citing review pp. 6, 11; paraphrase, not Issurin's wording). The Supertraining evidence above comes from sprinters, middle-distance runners, boxers and jumpers, Inference: its boxing study (Filimonov 1979) is a combat-sport case Issurin's review would put in doubt. Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names. Inference: for football, where many qualities must be held at once, the claim applies to a single quality inside an otherwise mixed programme rather than to the whole plan. Eric's baseline: \"incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" and \"the rest are trained at a retention dose\" (line 182, Inferred (transcript + slides)).\n  done\nblank line before headers:\n  done\nlinks:\n      83\n  links done","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"61981ce0-ed6e-4f81-86cd-8f8458a24e06","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:04:54.659Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"headers per file (expect 6):\n  done\nem dashes outside headers:\n  done\nen dashes:\n  done\nwrapped links:\n  done\nreview words:\nclaim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes.md:22:**When NOT to apply** — The book gives no sample sizes, effect sizes or design for any of the cited studies, and gives no definition here of \"medium\" versus \"highly qualified.\" **Contested across the library:** the vault brief of Issurin (2016) records that he judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, that the reviewed combat and team-sport studies improved fitness measures but did not report team-practice or competition outcomes, and that he placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]], brief citing review pp. 6, 11; paraphrase, not Issurin's wording). The Supertraining evidence above comes from sprinters, middle-distance runners, boxers and jumpers, Inference: its boxing study (Filimonov 1979) is a combat-sport case Issurin's review would put in doubt. Inference: this is a disagreement about scope and evidence sufficiency; the sprinter and jumper studies fall outside the sports Issurin names. Inference: for football, where many qualities must be held at once, the claim applies to a single quality inside an otherwise mixed programme rather than to the whole plan. Eric's baseline: \"incompatible qualities ... are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block\" and \"the rest are trained at a re