 bette\n142: - Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injur\n151: - Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a subs\n153: - Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q23): \"It's handle\n180: - The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands\n182: - Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) a\n185: - In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in foot\n186: - The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practi","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c55d85a1-698b-44e3-bd20-8a9e4e29a1ed","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:02.272Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"59: - Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust program\n74: - Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective \n75: - The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview \n76: - The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interv\n101: - The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundat\n104: - Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-\n105: - Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-stre\n106: - Undulating/non-linear periodization (Parker/Miller/Panariello), waving volume week to week (e.g., medium-high, medium, high, low over four weeks) is\n117: - Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It do\n122: - \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, ala\n123: - For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity — a bette\n142: - Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injur\n151: - Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first; recovery technology (Theragun, Normatec, cryo) is treated as useful but not a subs\n153: - Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q23): \"It's handle\n180: - The preparatory period is sequenced hypertrophy/work-capacity, then basic strength, then strength-to-power, each with defined intensity/volume bands\n182: - Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) a\n185: - In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in foot\n186: - The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday 