{"parentUuid":"e01c49d8-c2f4-477d-8073-f7cc7dad66fa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffppTVkYWjWn74t8hpSg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013w1PCpwYe4fYvQHmazBAgW","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs; sed -n '105p;185p;186p' method-baseline.md","description":"Read full baseline lines 105, 185, 186"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1494,"cache_read_input_tokens":197823,"output_tokens":193,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":66},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1494},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":193,"cache_read_input_tokens":197823,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1494,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1494,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013w1PCpwYe4fYvQHmazBAgW":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs; sed -n '105p;185p;186p' method-baseline.md","description":"Read full baseline lines 105, 185, 186"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffppKGAAz2C7ypqkM1W6","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"044f0550-cfb6-43de-80d8-fa527616c41a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:06.143Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"044f0550-cfb6-43de-80d8-fa527616c41a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013w1PCpwYe4fYvQHmazBAgW","type":"tool_result","content":"- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n- In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the postseason ... That's when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)** He did not say whether the postseason gets a distinct taper.\n- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d61e0a18-14e8-4e82-b742-9cacc679a546","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:06.317Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n- In football the target is a peak every Saturday, with November and the postseason as the priority window. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q14): \"in football you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November as you get closer to the postseason ... That's when players need to be at or near their peak as they head into the postseason and playoffs.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)** He did not say whether the postseason gets a distinct taper.\n- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"044f0550-cfb6-43de-80d8-fa527616c41a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d61e0a18-14e8-4e82-b742-9cacc679a546","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14800488 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2ff04d85-b949-42a7-9d26-49d69379aabd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:06.321Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14800488 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2ff04d85-b949-42a7-9d26-49d69379aabd","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffpptk342J1uTVJkrumV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":678,"cache_read_input_tokens":199317,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":678,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":9129,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffppcqKedhL7RzyQ6hga","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"204be9ea-ced6-4ff2-85b0-28c8f284f660","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:05:20.457Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"204be9ea-ced6-4ff2-85b0-28c8f284f660","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffpptk342J1uTVJkrumV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VqRY7Hm3EiHgyeYzXw7gsq","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 6c (6.8.3-6.11), 19 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 falsification pass), 2026-10-03, Mac. I did not write or fix these entries.\nInputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md`, fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md`, brief `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`, baseline `docs/method-baseline.md`.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt`, in `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf` (PDF idx = printed + 11).\n\n**Page images I opened** (renders in `staging/_renders/r2_6c_p*.png`): PDF 288, 299, 300, 301, 371, 372, 378, 515, 536 (130 dpi full pages); Fig 6.25 (PDF 365) and Figs 6.30-6.31 (PDF 375) at 300 dpi; Fig 6.24 (PDF 365) at 200 dpi. Text layer only: PDF 28-29, 35, 38, 305, 363, 364, 367-370, 373-377, 379, 438, 514.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum | FIX | **Round-1 fixes landed.** (1) Fig 6.30 block: on my 300 dpi crop the month ticks sit about 85-95 px apart. The shaded block runs from about month 1.6 (left edge 55 px past the month-1 tick) to about month 4.5-4.6, so the entry's \"about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\" is right. The curve values hold: vertical jump peaks at about 7 near month 3.6 and ends at about 4.6 at month 8; stride length ends at about 11.1 (peak about 11.5); ten jumps end at about 9.4 (peak about 9.7). (2) The three 4-12 week referents are accurate: PDF 305 \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\"; PDF 375 \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; and the PDF 536 image, \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\". The Addendum is attributed to Verkhoshansky (9.1, Yessis, PDF 515), which is correct. **New problem, from the round-1 wording:** \"which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity\" is the writer's reasoning, and it is not labelled. Neither page says the two readings reconcile. Minor point: PDF 536 says t2 \"is equal to\" t1, and the entry's \"about equal\" comes from PDF 375's \"approximately\". That is acceptable because both pages are cited together. | Change \"...(T2 about equal to T1), which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity.\" to \"...(T2 about equal to T1). Inference: that rule reconciles the two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity.\" |\n| heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window | GO | Round-1 fixes landed. The voice is now \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, ...)\" and \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\". The PDF 515 title block matches the image (\"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky / Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\"). The link to the 6d technique-work heuristic is present and resolves. The PDF 536 image matches every quote: the reversed sentence, \"insignificant increase\", \"(by only a few percent)\", \"maintain\", \"as believed by the athlete\", \"lost his chance to be selected ... national team\", March-April and May-June, and \"Figure 8 shows\". The page prints Figure 7, so \"the text says its Figure 8\" is the right hedge. Fixer disagreement 3 (record both plural and singular) is justified by the page. Optional polish, not blocking: the Inference sentence says \"translation slip\" twice (\"may be a translation slip: ... which makes a translation slip plausible\"). \"Its own example then agrees with Chapter 6\" is plain text but is the writer's comparison; it could move inside the Inference. | Checked: PDF 375 and 376 quotes (text layer); PDF 515 and 536 images; baseline lines 105 and 186. |\n| claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training | GO | The fix landed exactly as specified. The Susman item now says the authors do not call it an LDTE, quotes \"can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" (PDF 377), and labels the sea-level reading as Inference. Nothing new is wrong. | Checked against the PDF 376-377 text: 1700 m, 3 weeks, more than 4 weeks, peak about 1 week, 2250 m, 4-minute ergometer test, the hypobaric sentence, Susman 1978 and its indicators, \"supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction)\". Baseline line 74. |\n| claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes | GO | Fixes landed. On the PDF 371 image, only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is italic, so \"partly italicised\" is right. The two new Related links (6b framework, 6d complex-training claim) resolve. The added scope Inference is labelled. | Checked against the PDF 371 image: definitions, \"both methods are successful\", Kornelyuk 1980, Levchenko 1986, Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982, Filimonov 1979, Mironenko & Antonova 1981, the transient-reaction reason. Baseline lines 106, 117, 182. |\n| heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast | FIX | **The rewrite mostly holds.** On the images, PDF 371 is a hedge (\"fairly unimportant\" ... \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"). The PDF 300 quote and its population heading are exact. The PDF 301 bullets sit under the PDF 300 heading \"principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period\", so they are correctly moved out as stage-level. The barbell = strength / jumping = speed-strength link is labelled Inference. Fixer disagreement 1 is justified: no page equates them. Fig 5.13 does label the first-stage means \"Jumps\" and the barbell means \"Weights\" (PDF 300 image). The Figure 1.7 note checks: Fig 1.7 is the torque diagram in 1.2 (PDF 28-29). **Two problems remain.** (1) **PDF 288 is overstated.** The entry says resistance plus plyometrics \"works best 'provided ...'\". The page (PDF 288 image) says combined programmes \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\". That is a comparison with plyometrics alone, not \"best\". (2) **PDF 299 is reported only in part.** The entry gives the novice null result. On the same page (PDF 299 image), the book explains that result and limits it: \"any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person ... Within one workout, the traces of all the training tasks are summed\" (the same reasoning as p. 371's \"fairly unimportant\"). It also says \"These findings should not be considered to disprove ... A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect on the training effect of speed-strength and speed work ... the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation.\" So the book itself offers a qualification-based reading of the PDF 299-300 vs 288/371 tension. The flat sentence \"The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order\" should carry that. `contested` still stands, because PDF 300 vs 371 is a literal opposition under the labelled reading. | (1) Replace \"resistance plus plyometrics works best\" with \"resistance plus plyometrics develops explosive strength 'even more effectively than plyometrics alone,'\". (2) After the PDF 299 sentence, add: \"The same page explains the null result by the novices' low fitness ('any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person'; within one workout 'the traces of all the training tasks are summed', the same reasoning as p. 371) and says 'A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect' on later speed-strength work, a hypothesis it says is untested (PDF p. 299). Inference: the cross-chapter conflict may partly be one of qualification: PDF pp. 299-300 describe novices and average athletes, while p. 288 and p. 371 name no population.\" |\n| framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 372 image: \"so conditional ... very broad interpretation\", \"cannot be strictly applied\", the postural-muscle sentence, the three positive and two negative pairings and their wording, Volkov 1975 on the negative list only, \"raises the effect of the training session as a whole\", 2-3 days, 3-8 hours, the ATP-CP sentence and its three citations. \"Rest pause\" sentence on PDF 373 (text layer). The Example is labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive | GO | Fixes landed. Both Inferences are labelled (\"so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum\", \"Bondarchuk agrees\"). The PDF 300-301 stage-level rule is quoted exactly against the images. \"The list follows the research with novices and athletes of average qualification\" is a fair unquoted description: on PDF 300 that phrase introduces the three conclusions, and the \"principles ... emerge\" heading follows them. The Fig 5.13 \"Jumps\" label is confirmed on the image. | Checked against the PDF 372 image: 4-6 weeks, 2 weeks, \"transient accumulation\" (italic), the reverse sequence giving a negative effect, the energy-potential mechanism. PDF 300 and 301 images. Baseline lines 106 and 180. |\n| framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development | GO | Unchanged since round 1, and round 1 checked it against the PDF 373 image. | Not re-opened. |\n| claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality | GO | The fix landed and the logic is now consistent. Read literally, p. 370 (anaerobic before aerobic) fits both p. 372 lists (PDF 372 image: positive \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"; negative \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\"). It conflicts only with p. 373's aerobic-first sequential model. The slip reading is labelled Inference. Fixer disagreement 2 is justified: PDF 373 lists four citations (Naumenko 1978; Vavilov 1977; Serafimova 1974; Morzhevikov 1980), and p. 370 gives two of them. | Checked against the PDF 370 text: 3-4 months, up to 10 months, one month, HR 120-170, 2-3 months, about 4 months, \"not definitive\". PDF 372 image, PDF 373 text. |\n| principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue | GO | The added missing-idea passage is accurate: \"The training potential of the means employed decreases as the special work capacity increases\" and \"it is inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely, because the body is functionally unprepared for them ... disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\" (PDF 368-369, printed 357-358, text layer). Small wording point, not blocking: \"closes the preceding passage\" is loose, because the Fig 6.28 caption and a conjugate-sequence paragraph come between the warning and 6.9.4. All links resolve. | Checked against the PDF 369-370 text: the homeostasis sentence, the ligamentous-joint sentence, the four sports, the five qualification citations, the intensity sentence, \"only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading\". |\n| framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state | GO (spot-checked) | None. The \"page image not opened\" note for PDF 379 is still honest. I checked the PDF 379 text: \"not especially complex\" and \"much smaller\" match. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: the three levels (italic labels), \"one or more years\", \"up to 5-6 months\", \"proportion of intensive means\", \"largely random and at times unpredictable\", \"not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels\", \"controllable\". Baseline lines 64 and 255 not re-opened (round 1 checked them). |\n| claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification | GO | Unchanged since round 1. | PDF 377 text re-read: 3 to 4 times a year, Pollock 1973, twice a month, the medium-qualification cause, the monocycle and bi-cycle patterns. |\n| case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: the italic sentence (italic confirmed), Verkhoshansky 1988, the plantarflexor and thigh-extensor sentences, \"feels perfectly fit\", the prescription wording. `untested` fits. |\n| case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: same volume and distribution of running means, single vs double peak, December and March-April, OBLA, 400 m and 1200 m control runs, 800 m 4.8 vs 3.4 s, 1500 m 6.3 vs 4.6 s. The Zhdanovich link is labelled Inference. |\n| case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers | GO (spot-checked) | None blocking. My own Fig 6.24 readings (200 dpi crop, PDF 365) agree within about 2 units. Absolute strength: 36/54, 50/45, 63/53. Explosive: 53/102, 88/77, 122/95. Vertical jump: 14/26, 15/14, 34/24. Optional observation: on the Explosive Strength panel the printed tick labels (0, 40, 60, 100, 140) sit at roughly even spacing, so the \"40\" and \"60\" labels do not fit a linear 0-140 scale. My readings use the 0 and 140 ends and the 100 tick, which agree. The entry already flags units as unclear and could add \"the explosive-strength axis labels are unevenly printed\". | Checked: the design sentences and conclusion 1 (PDF 364 text); conclusions 2-3 and the caption (PDF 365 image); \"high jumpers of medium qualification\"; Khodykin and Chernousov at PDF 364 (text) and in the Ch 5 list on the PDF 299 image. |\n| case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers | GO | **Fix verified on the 300 dpi crop.** At about 10.5 px per point: absolute strength GP1 7.0, GP2 18.2, GP3 16.8; speed GP1 17.9, GP2 11.1, GP3 13.5; power GP1 6.0, GP2 14.2, GP3 20.0 (the bar top sits on the 20 gridline). The entry's values (7, 18, 17; 18, 11, 14; 6, 14, 20), its Example and its When NOT pair (17 vs 18) all match. No old value remains. | Checked: 7 weeks, the three load groups, \"metabolic cost\", the caption, \"Group 1, graph 2\" (PDF 365 text and crop). |\n| framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect | GO | Unchanged since round 1, and round 1 checked it against the images. | Not re-opened. |\n| heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation | GO | All three fixes landed: the \"signal\" gloss is labelled Inference; the 15% sentence now says the text does not specify the group, with the confound as Inference; and there is a blank line before Example (mechanical check). The fixer's extra Example change (\"without saying in which group\") is correct against PDF 368: \"At the same time, the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%\". | Checked against the PDF 368 text: 24.2/18.9 vs 7.7/4.9%, swimmers, Kuchnyev 1977, Platonov 1980, 2-4% and 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979), Yakovlev 1971, Siff & Yessis 1992, Yevgenyev et al 1975, \"strictly regulated\". Baseline lines 151 and 153. |\n| claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks | GO | The added missing ideas 2 and 3 are quoted accurately from the PDF 367 text (external similarity \"often overemphasized\", squats \"justified\", \"not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions ... strength component\") and the PDF 363 text (\"cannot simply be used in a very general way\", the rower or cyclist example, \"The emergence of specificity ... is not supported\"). The 6.9.3 heading is on PDF 367. The 1.3 vs 1.4 note checks (PDF 35 heading \"1.3 A Philosophy of Physical Training\", PDF 38 heading \"1.4 Specificity in Training\") and is labelled Inference. Optional: \"Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side\" is the writer's framing; it could start with \"Inference:\". | Checked: the 800 m benchmarks, 10.6-10.7 s, 9 m and 33-34 m, Zhdanovich 1980, Verkhoshansky & Sirenko 1982, \"archaic viewpoint\" (PDF 367-368 text). Baseline lines 122 and 123. |\n\n**Tally: GO 17, FIX 2, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Check 2: Addendum voice (PDF 514-590)\n\nI grepped all 19 files for \"Addendum\", \"PDF p. 5xx\" and \"p. 5xx\". Only two files carry Addendum material:\n- `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect...`: it names \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515)\", then \"the Addendum (PDF p. 536)\" in the same passage.\n- `heuristic--time-technique...`: it names \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, ...)\" and \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\".\n\nNo 6c statement from the Addendum is attributed to \"the authors\". PDF 514 is the \"9. ADDENDUM\" divider, and PDF 515 carries the 9.1 title block, which I confirmed on the image.\n\nOut of scope: the fix log flags `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md` (staging and vault) as citing PDF 536 with an unlabelled \"the Addendum says\". It is not one of the 19 and I did not review it. The coordinator should route it.\n\n## Check 3: the within-session contested paragraph\n\n| Page | What the entry says | What the page says (image) | Holds? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PDF 371 | order \"fairly unimportant\", then \"Nevertheless ... preserve the above order\"; a hedge | Same words; the italic sentence is italic | Yes |\n| PDF 288 | resistance plus plyometrics \"works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions'\" | \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided ...\" | Quote yes; \"works best\" overstates (FIX 1) |\n| PDF 299 | novices: \"no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout | Yes, groups ordered \"during the fundamental stage of their workouts\". The same page attributes the null result to novice status and predicts a positive strength-first effect for well-trained athletes | Accurate but incomplete (FIX 2) |\n| PDF 300 | \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" | Exact | Yes |\n| PDF 301 | bullets are stage-level, under the PDF 300 heading | Heading \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge:\" at the foot of PDF 300; bullets at the top of PDF 301 | Yes |\n\nStage-level and session-level rules are kept apart in both the within-session and the sequential entries. The barbell = strength / jumping = speed-strength link is labelled Inference in both.\n\n## Check 5: mechanical checks over all 19 files\n\n- Six section headers in every file, each in the `**X** —` vault form, each preceded by a blank line.\n- Em dashes appear only in section headers. No en dashes.\n- No wrapped `[[wikilinks]]`.\n- 83 unique wikilink targets; every one resolves in the staging library or the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`).\n- No review-history or process wording. The only grep hit is \"the reviewed combat and team-sport studies\", which refers to Issurin's review paper.\n- The baseline lines cited in the fixed entries (59, 74, 75, 76, 101, 104, 105, 106, 117, 122, 123, 142, 151, 153, 180, 182, 185, 186) match the quoted text.\n\n## Fix-log accuracy\n\nEvery change the fix log claims is present in the files. Its four disagreements with round 1 are each justified by the page:\n1. The barbell/jumping link labelled Inference: no page equates the terms.\n2. \"Appear among the four\": PDF 373 lists four citations, and p. 370 gives two of them.\n3. Plural and singular both kept: PDF 536 uses both.\n4. The PDF 438 / Fig 1.7 note not added. The PDF 438 text does say \"to perform endurance activities before strength and speed training (Fig 1.7)\", so the note would have been accurate. Leaving it out loses nothing required.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired, both one-sentence text edits with no re-reading of figures:\n1. **principle LDTE:** label \"that rule reconciles the two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity\" as Inference.\n2. **within-session heuristic:** (a) PDF 288: \"even more effectively than plyometrics alone\" in place of \"works best\"; (b) add PDF 299's own qualification caveat (novice traces summed; \"A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect\", untested), with the qualification-split reading labelled Inference.\n\nOptional polish (not blocking): the doubled \"translation slip\" in the technique heuristic; \"Inference:\" on \"Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side\" in the middle-distance claim; noting the uneven explosive-strength tick labels in Fig 6.24.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":678,"cache_read_input_tokens":199317,"output_tokens":9125,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":964},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":678},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":9125,"cache_read_input_tokens":199317,"cache_creation_input_tokens":678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":678,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VqRY7Hm3EiHgyeYzXw7gsq":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 6c (6.8.3-6.11), 19 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 falsification pass), 2026-10-03, Mac. I did not write or fix these entries.\nInputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md`, fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md`, brief `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`, baseline `docs/method-baseline.md`.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt`, in `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf` (PDF idx = printed + 11).\n\n**Page images I opened** (renders in `staging/_renders/r2_6c_p*.png`): PDF 288, 299, 300, 301, 371, 372, 378, 515, 536 (130 dpi full pages); Fig 6.25 (PDF 365) and Figs 6.30-6.31 (PDF 375) at 300 dpi; Fig 6.24 (PDF 365) at 200 dpi. Text layer only: PDF 28-29, 35, 38, 305, 363, 364, 367-370, 373-377, 379, 438, 514.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum | FIX | **Round-1 fixes landed.** (1) Fig 6.30 block: on my 300 dpi crop the month ticks sit about 85-95 px apart. The shaded block runs from about month 1.6 (left edge 55 px past the month-1 tick) to about month 4.5-4.6, so the entry's \"about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\" is right. The curve values hold: vertical jump peaks at about 7 near month 3.6 and ends at about 4.6 at month 8; stride length ends at about 11.1 (peak about 11.5); ten jumps end at about 9.4 (peak about 9.7). (2) The three 4-12 week referents are accurate: PDF 305 \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\"; PDF 375 \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; and the PDF 536 image, \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\". The Addendum is attributed to Verkhoshansky (9.1, Yessis, PDF 515), which is correct. **New problem, from the round-1 wording:** \"which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity\" is the writer's reasoning, and it is not labelled. Neither page says the two readings reconcile. Minor point: PDF 536 says t2 \"is equal to\" t1, and the entry's \"about equal\" comes from PDF 375's \"approximately\". That is acceptable because both pages are cited together. | Change \"...(T2 about equal to T1), which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity.\" to \"...(T2 about equal to T1). Inference: that rule reconciles the two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity.\" |\n| heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window | GO | Round-1 fixes landed. The voice is now \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, ...)\" and \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\". The PDF 515 title block matches the image (\"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky / Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\"). The link to the 6d technique-work heuristic is present and resolves. The PDF 536 image matches every quote: the reversed sentence, \"insignificant increase\", \"(by only a few percent)\", \"maintain\", \"as believed by the athlete\", \"lost his chance to be selected ... national team\", March-April and May-June, and \"Figure 8 shows\". The page prints Figure 7, so \"the text says its Figure 8\" is the right hedge. Fixer disagreement 3 (record both plural and singular) is justified by the page. Optional polish, not blocking: the Inference sentence says \"translation slip\" twice (\"may be a translation slip: ... which makes a translation slip plausible\"). \"Its own example then agrees with Chapter 6\" is plain text but is the writer's comparison; it could move inside the Inference. | Checked: PDF 375 and 376 quotes (text layer); PDF 515 and 536 images; baseline lines 105 and 186. |\n| claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training | GO | The fix landed exactly as specified. The Susman item now says the authors do not call it an LDTE, quotes \"can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" (PDF 377), and labels the sea-level reading as Inference. Nothing new is wrong. | Checked against the PDF 376-377 text: 1700 m, 3 weeks, more than 4 weeks, peak about 1 week, 2250 m, 4-minute ergometer test, the hypobaric sentence, Susman 1978 and its indicators, \"supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction)\". Baseline line 74. |\n| claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes | GO | Fixes landed. On the PDF 371 image, only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is italic, so \"partly italicised\" is right. The two new Related links (6b framework, 6d complex-training claim) resolve. The added scope Inference is labelled. | Checked against the PDF 371 image: definitions, \"both methods are successful\", Kornelyuk 1980, Levchenko 1986, Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982, Filimonov 1979, Mironenko & Antonova 1981, the transient-reaction reason. Baseline lines 106, 117, 182. |\n| heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast | FIX | **The rewrite mostly holds.** On the images, PDF 371 is a hedge (\"fairly unimportant\" ... \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"). The PDF 300 quote and its population heading are exact. The PDF 301 bullets sit under the PDF 300 heading \"principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period\", so they are correctly moved out as stage-level. The barbell = strength / jumping = speed-strength link is labelled Inference. Fixer disagreement 1 is justified: no page equates them. Fig 5.13 does label the first-stage means \"Jumps\" and the barbell means \"Weights\" (PDF 300 image). The Figure 1.7 note checks: Fig 1.7 is the torque diagram in 1.2 (PDF 28-29). **Two problems remain.** (1) **PDF 288 is overstated.** The entry says resistance plus plyometrics \"works best 'provided ...'\". The page (PDF 288 image) says combined programmes \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\". That is a comparison with plyometrics alone, not \"best\". (2) **PDF 299 is reported only in part.** The entry gives the novice null result. On the same page (PDF 299 image), the book explains that result and limits it: \"any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person ... Within one workout, the traces of all the training tasks are summed\" (the same reasoning as p. 371's \"fairly unimportant\"). It also says \"These findings should not be considered to disprove ... A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect on the training effect of speed-strength and speed work ... the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation.\" So the book itself offers a qualification-based reading of the PDF 299-300 vs 288/371 tension. The flat sentence \"The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order\" should carry that. `contested` still stands, because PDF 300 vs 371 is a literal opposition under the labelled reading. | (1) Replace \"resistance plus plyometrics works best\" with \"resistance plus plyometrics develops explosive strength 'even more effectively than plyometrics alone,'\". (2) After the PDF 299 sentence, add: \"The same page explains the null result by the novices' low fitness ('any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person'; within one workout 'the traces of all the training tasks are summed', the same reasoning as p. 371) and says 'A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect' on later speed-strength work, a hypothesis it says is untested (PDF p. 299). Inference: the cross-chapter conflict may partly be one of qualification: PDF pp. 299-300 describe novices and average athletes, while p. 288 and p. 371 name no population.\" |\n| framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 372 image: \"so conditional ... very broad interpretation\", \"cannot be strictly applied\", the postural-muscle sentence, the three positive and two negative pairings and their wording, Volkov 1975 on the negative list only, \"raises the effect of the training session as a whole\", 2-3 days, 3-8 hours, the ATP-CP sentence and its three citations. \"Rest pause\" sentence on PDF 373 (text layer). The Example is labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive | GO | Fixes landed. Both Inferences are labelled (\"so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum\", \"Bondarchuk agrees\"). The PDF 300-301 stage-level rule is quoted exactly against the images. \"The list follows the research with novices and athletes of average qualification\" is a fair unquoted description: on PDF 300 that phrase introduces the three conclusions, and the \"principles ... emerge\" heading follows them. The Fig 5.13 \"Jumps\" label is confirmed on the image. | Checked against the PDF 372 image: 4-6 weeks, 2 weeks, \"transient accumulation\" (italic), the reverse sequence giving a negative effect, the energy-potential mechanism. PDF 300 and 301 images. Baseline lines 106 and 180. |\n| framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development | GO | Unchanged since round 1, and round 1 checked it against the PDF 373 image. | Not re-opened. |\n| claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality | GO | The fix landed and the logic is now consistent. Read literally, p. 370 (anaerobic before aerobic) fits both p. 372 lists (PDF 372 image: positive \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"; negative \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\"). It conflicts only with p. 373's aerobic-first sequential model. The slip reading is labelled Inference. Fixer disagreement 2 is justified: PDF 373 lists four citations (Naumenko 1978; Vavilov 1977; Serafimova 1974; Morzhevikov 1980), and p. 370 gives two of them. | Checked against the PDF 370 text: 3-4 months, up to 10 months, one month, HR 120-170, 2-3 months, about 4 months, \"not definitive\". PDF 372 image, PDF 373 text. |\n| principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue | GO | The added missing-idea passage is accurate: \"The training potential of the means employed decreases as the special work capacity increases\" and \"it is inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely, because the body is functionally unprepared for them ... disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\" (PDF 368-369, printed 357-358, text layer). Small wording point, not blocking: \"closes the preceding passage\" is loose, because the Fig 6.28 caption and a conjugate-sequence paragraph come between the warning and 6.9.4. All links resolve. | Checked against the PDF 369-370 text: the homeostasis sentence, the ligamentous-joint sentence, the four sports, the five qualification citations, the intensity sentence, \"only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading\". |\n| framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state | GO (spot-checked) | None. The \"page image not opened\" note for PDF 379 is still honest. I checked the PDF 379 text: \"not especially complex\" and \"much smaller\" match. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: the three levels (italic labels), \"one or more years\", \"up to 5-6 months\", \"proportion of intensive means\", \"largely random and at times unpredictable\", \"not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels\", \"controllable\". Baseline lines 64 and 255 not re-opened (round 1 checked them). |\n| claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification | GO | Unchanged since round 1. | PDF 377 text re-read: 3 to 4 times a year, Pollock 1973, twice a month, the medium-qualification cause, the monocycle and bi-cycle patterns. |\n| case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: the italic sentence (italic confirmed), Verkhoshansky 1988, the plantarflexor and thigh-extensor sentences, \"feels perfectly fit\", the prescription wording. `untested` fits. |\n| case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: same volume and distribution of running means, single vs double peak, December and March-April, OBLA, 400 m and 1200 m control runs, 800 m 4.8 vs 3.4 s, 1500 m 6.3 vs 4.6 s. The Zhdanovich link is labelled Inference. |\n| case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers | GO (spot-checked) | None blocking. My own Fig 6.24 readings (200 dpi crop, PDF 365) agree within about 2 units. Absolute strength: 36/54, 50/45, 63/53. Explosive: 53/102, 88/77, 122/95. Vertical jump: 14/26, 15/14, 34/24. Optional observation: on the Explosive Strength panel the printed tick labels (0, 40, 60, 100, 140) sit at roughly even spacing, so the \"40\" and \"60\" labels do not fit a linear 0-140 scale. My readings use the 0 and 140 ends and the 100 tick, which agree. The entry already flags units as unclear and could add \"the explosive-strength axis labels are unevenly printed\". | Checked: the design sentences and conclusion 1 (PDF 364 text); conclusions 2-3 and the caption (PDF 365 image); \"high jumpers of medium qualification\"; Khodykin and Chernousov at PDF 364 (text) and in the Ch 5 list on the PDF 299 image. |\n| case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers | GO | **Fix verified on the 300 dpi crop.** At about 10.5 px per point: absolute strength GP1 7.0, GP2 18.2, GP3 16.8; speed GP1 17.9, GP2 11.1, GP3 13.5; power GP1 6.0, GP2 14.2, GP3 20.0 (the bar top sits on the 20 gridline). The entry's values (7, 18, 17; 18, 11, 14; 6, 14, 20), its Example and its When NOT pair (17 vs 18) all match. No old value remains. | Checked: 7 weeks, the three load groups, \"metabolic cost\", the caption, \"Group 1, graph 2\" (PDF 365 text and crop). |\n| framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect | GO | Unchanged since round 1, and round 1 checked it against the images. | Not re-opened. |\n| heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation | GO | All three fixes landed: the \"signal\" gloss is labelled Inference; the 15% sentence now says the text does not specify the group, with the confound as Inference; and there is a blank line before Example (mechanical check). The fixer's extra Example change (\"without saying in which group\") is correct against PDF 368: \"At the same time, the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%\". | Checked against the PDF 368 text: 24.2/18.9 vs 7.7/4.9%, swimmers, Kuchnyev 1977, Platonov 1980, 2-4% and 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979), Yakovlev 1971, Siff & Yessis 1992, Yevgenyev et al 1975, \"strictly regulated\". Baseline lines 151 and 153. |\n| claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks | GO | The added missing ideas 2 and 3 are quoted accurately from the PDF 367 text (external similarity \"often overemphasized\", squats \"justified\", \"not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions ... strength component\") and the PDF 363 text (\"cannot simply be used in a very general way\", the rower or cyclist example, \"The emergence of specificity ... is not supported\"). The 6.9.3 heading is on PDF 367. The 1.3 vs 1.4 note checks (PDF 35 heading \"1.3 A Philosophy of Physical Training\", PDF 38 heading \"1.4 Specificity in Training\") and is labelled Inference. Optional: \"Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side\" is the writer's framing; it could start with \"Inference:\". | Checked: the 800 m benchmarks, 10.6-10.7 s, 9 m and 33-34 m, Zhdanovich 1980, Verkhoshansky & Sirenko 1982, \"archaic viewpoint\" (PDF 367-368 text). Baseline lines 122 and 123. |\n\n**Tally: GO 17, FIX 2, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Check 2: Addendum voice (PDF 514-590)\n\nI grepped all 19 files for \"Addendum\", \"PDF p. 5xx\" and \"p. 5xx\". Only two files carry Addendum material:\n- `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect...`: it names \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515)\", then \"the Addendum (PDF p. 536)\" in the same passage.\n- `heuristic--time-technique...`: it names \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, ...)\" and \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\".\n\nNo 6c statement from the Addendum is attributed to \"the authors\". PDF 514 is the \"9. ADDENDUM\" divider, and PDF 515 carries the 9.1 title block, which I confirmed on the image.\n\nOut of scope: the fix log flags `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md` (staging and vault) as citing PDF 536 with an unlabelled \"the Addendum says\". It is not one of the 19 and I did not review it. The coordinator should route it.\n\n## Check 3: the within-session contested paragraph\n\n| Page | What the entry says | What the page says (image) | Holds? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PDF 371 | order \"fairly unimportant\", then \"Nevertheless ... preserve the above order\"; a hedge | Same words; the italic sentence is italic | Yes |\n| PDF 288 | resistance plus plyometrics \"works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions'\" | \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided ...\" | Quote yes; \"works best\" overstates (FIX 1) |\n| PDF 299 | novices: \"no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout | Yes, groups ordered \"during the fundamental stage of their workouts\". The same page attributes the null result to novice status and predicts a positive strength-first effect for well-trained athletes | Accurate but incomplete (FIX 2) |\n| PDF 300 | \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" | Exact | Yes |\n| PDF 301 | bullets are stage-level, under the PDF 300 heading | Heading \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge:\" at the foot of PDF 300; bullets at the top of PDF 301 | Yes |\n\nStage-level and session-level rules are kept apart in both the within-session and the sequential entries. The barbell = strength / jumping = speed-strength link is labelled Inference in both.\n\n## Check 5: mechanical checks over all 19 files\n\n- Six section headers in every file, each in the `**X** —` vault form, each preceded by a blank line.\n- Em dashes appear only in section headers. No en dashes.\n- No wrapped `[[wikilinks]]`.\n- 83 unique wikilink targets; every one resolves in the staging library or the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`).\n- No review-history or process wording. The only grep hit is \"the reviewed combat and team-sport studies\", which refers to Issurin's review paper.\n- The baseline lines cited in the fixed entries (59, 74, 75, 76, 101, 104, 105, 106, 117, 122, 123, 142, 151, 153, 180, 182, 185, 186) match the quoted text.\n\n## Fix-log accuracy\n\nEvery change the fix log claims is present in the files. Its four disagreements with round 1 are each justified by the page:\n1. The barbell/jumping link labelled Inference: no page equates the terms.\n2. \"Appear among the four\": PDF 373 lists four citations, and p. 370 gives two of them.\n3. Plural and singular both kept: PDF 536 uses both.\n4. The PDF 438 / Fig 1.7 note not added. The PDF 438 text does say \"to perform endurance activities before strength and speed training (Fig 1.7)\", so the note would have been accurate. Leaving it out loses nothing required.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired, both one-sentence text edits with no re-reading of figures:\n1. **principle LDTE:** label \"that rule reconciles the two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity\" as Inference.\n2. **within-session heuristic:** (a) PDF 288: \"even more effectively than plyometrics alone\" in place of \"works best\"; (b) add PDF 299's own qualification caveat (novice traces summed; \"A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect\", untested), with the qualification-split reading labelled Inference.\n\nOptional polish (not blocking): the doubled \"translation slip\" in the technique heuristic; \"Inference:\" on \"Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side\" in the middle-distance claim; noting the uneven explosive-strength tick labels in Fig 6.24.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffppcqKedhL7RzyQ6hga","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b4c200f1-26d2-4977-8b9f-7a8c5316e34c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:28.640Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b4c200f1-26d2-4977-8b9f-7a8c5316e34c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a2b72ded-2f77-4788-be97-76a7696d93d6","agentId":"a4595b0bc175c26cf","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VqRY7Hm3EiHgyeYzXw7gsq","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus_round2.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"b8383eec-ae53-43d6-82ac-0887f45fa131","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:06:28.765Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus_round2.md","content":"# Review round 2: Supertraining Chapter 6c (6.8.3-6.11), 19 entries\n\nReviewer: Claude Opus (independent round-2 falsification pass), 2026-10-03, Mac. I did not write or fix these entries.\nInputs: round-1 review `staging/review_supertraining_ch6c_opus.md`, fix log `staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6c_applied.md`, brief `docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md`, baseline `docs/method-baseline.md`.\nScope: the 19 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6c.txt`, in `staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`.\nSource: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf` (PDF idx = printed + 11).\n\n**Page images I opened** (renders in `staging/_renders/r2_6c_p*.png`): PDF 288, 299, 300, 301, 371, 372, 378, 515, 536 (130 dpi full pages); Fig 6.25 (PDF 365) and Figs 6.30-6.31 (PDF 375) at 300 dpi; Fig 6.24 (PDF 365) at 200 dpi. Text layer only: PDF 28-29, 35, 38, 305, 363, 364, 367-370, 373-377, 379, 438, 514.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, with page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect-from-volume-above-the-optimum | FIX | **Round-1 fixes landed.** (1) Fig 6.30 block: on my 300 dpi crop the month ticks sit about 85-95 px apart. The shaded block runs from about month 1.6 (left edge 55 px past the month-1 tick) to about month 4.5-4.6, so the entry's \"about month 1.6 to about month 4.5\" is right. The curve values hold: vertical jump peaks at about 7 near month 3.6 and ends at about 4.6 at month 8; stride length ends at about 11.1 (peak about 11.5); ten jumps end at about 9.4 (peak about 9.7). (2) The three 4-12 week referents are accurate: PDF 305 \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\"; PDF 375 \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks\"; and the PDF 536 image, \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks\". The Addendum is attributed to Verkhoshansky (9.1, Yessis, PDF 515), which is correct. **New problem, from the round-1 wording:** \"which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity\" is the writer's reasoning, and it is not labelled. Neither page says the two readings reconcile. Minor point: PDF 536 says t2 \"is equal to\" t1, and the entry's \"about equal\" comes from PDF 375's \"approximately\". That is acceptable because both pages are cited together. | Change \"...(T2 about equal to T1), which reconciles those two readings; the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity.\" to \"...(T2 about equal to T1). Inference: that rule reconciles the two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity.\" |\n| heuristic--time-technique-and-competition-strength-work-to-the-ldte-window | GO | Round-1 fixes landed. The voice is now \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, ...)\" and \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\". The PDF 515 title block matches the image (\"Dr. Yuri Verkhoshansky / Translated from the Russian by Dr. Michael Yessis\"). The link to the 6d technique-work heuristic is present and resolves. The PDF 536 image matches every quote: the reversed sentence, \"insignificant increase\", \"(by only a few percent)\", \"maintain\", \"as believed by the athlete\", \"lost his chance to be selected ... national team\", March-April and May-June, and \"Figure 8 shows\". The page prints Figure 7, so \"the text says its Figure 8\" is the right hedge. Fixer disagreement 3 (record both plural and singular) is justified by the page. Optional polish, not blocking: the Inference sentence says \"translation slip\" twice (\"may be a translation slip: ... which makes a translation slip plausible\"). \"Its own example then agrees with Chapter 6\" is plain text but is the writer's comparison; it could move inside the Inference. | Checked: PDF 375 and 376 quotes (text layer); PDF 515 and 536 images; baseline lines 105 and 186. |\n| claim--ldte-in-endurance-training-documented-only-after-altitude-training | GO | The fix landed exactly as specified. The Susman item now says the authors do not call it an LDTE, quotes \"can be detected in the dynamics of a number of psycho-physiological indicators\" (PDF 377), and labels the sea-level reading as Inference. Nothing new is wrong. | Checked against the PDF 376-377 text: 1700 m, 3 weeks, more than 4 weeks, peak about 1 week, 2250 m, 4-minute ergometer test, the hypobaric sentence, Susman 1978 and its indicators, \"supercompensation (adaptive reconstruction)\". Baseline line 74. |\n| claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes | GO | Fixes landed. On the PDF 371 image, only \"concentration of the volume of uni-directional loading produces more extensive functional changes and greater improvement\" is italic, so \"partly italicised\" is right. The two new Related links (6b framework, 6d complex-training claim) resolve. The added scope Inference is labelled. | Checked against the PDF 371 image: definitions, \"both methods are successful\", Kornelyuk 1980, Levchenko 1986, Sirenko & Zhdanovich 1982, Filimonov 1979, Mironenko & Antonova 1981, the transient-reaction reason. Baseline lines 106, 117, 182. |\n| heuristic--within-session-order-of-explosive-means-matters-less-than-volume-and-contrast | FIX | **The rewrite mostly holds.** On the images, PDF 371 is a hedge (\"fairly unimportant\" ... \"Nevertheless, one should still preserve the above order\"). The PDF 300 quote and its population heading are exact. The PDF 301 bullets sit under the PDF 300 heading \"principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period\", so they are correctly moved out as stage-level. The barbell = strength / jumping = speed-strength link is labelled Inference. Fixer disagreement 1 is justified: no page equates them. Fig 5.13 does label the first-stage means \"Jumps\" and the barbell means \"Weights\" (PDF 300 image). The Figure 1.7 note checks: Fig 1.7 is the torque diagram in 1.2 (PDF 28-29). **Two problems remain.** (1) **PDF 288 is overstated.** The entry says resistance plus plyometrics \"works best 'provided ...'\". The page (PDF 288 image) says combined programmes \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\". That is a comparison with plyometrics alone, not \"best\". (2) **PDF 299 is reported only in part.** The entry gives the novice null result. On the same page (PDF 299 image), the book explains that result and limits it: \"any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person ... Within one workout, the traces of all the training tasks are summed\" (the same reasoning as p. 371's \"fairly unimportant\"). It also says \"These findings should not be considered to disprove ... A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect on the training effect of speed-strength and speed work ... the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation.\" So the book itself offers a qualification-based reading of the PDF 299-300 vs 288/371 tension. The flat sentence \"The book contradicts itself across chapters on within-session order\" should carry that. `contested` still stands, because PDF 300 vs 371 is a literal opposition under the labelled reading. | (1) Replace \"resistance plus plyometrics works best\" with \"resistance plus plyometrics develops explosive strength 'even more effectively than plyometrics alone,'\". (2) After the PDF 299 sentence, add: \"The same page explains the null result by the novices' low fitness ('any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person'; within one workout 'the traces of all the training tasks are summed', the same reasoning as p. 371) and says 'A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect' on later speed-strength work, a hypothesis it says is untested (PDF p. 299). Inference: the cross-chapter conflict may partly be one of qualification: PDF pp. 299-300 describe novices and average athletes, while p. 288 and p. 371 name no population.\" |\n| framework--positive-and-negative-sequences-of-endurance-loading | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 372 image: \"so conditional ... very broad interpretation\", \"cannot be strictly applied\", the postural-muscle sentence, the three positive and two negative pairings and their wording, Volkov 1975 on the negative list only, \"raises the effect of the training session as a whole\", 2-3 days, 3-8 hours, the ATP-CP sentence and its three citations. \"Rest pause\" sentence on PDF 373 (text layer). The Example is labelled Inference. |\n| heuristic--sequential-accumulation-needs-4-6-week-stages-extensive-before-explosive | GO | Fixes landed. Both Inferences are labelled (\"so the 4-6 weeks is a minimum\", \"Bondarchuk agrees\"). The PDF 300-301 stage-level rule is quoted exactly against the images. \"The list follows the research with novices and athletes of average qualification\" is a fair unquoted description: on PDF 300 that phrase introduces the three conclusions, and the \"principles ... emerge\" heading follows them. The Fig 5.13 \"Jumps\" label is confirmed on the image. | Checked against the PDF 372 image: 4-6 weeks, 2 weeks, \"transient accumulation\" (italic), the reverse sequence giving a negative effect, the energy-potential mechanism. PDF 300 and 301 images. Baseline lines 106 and 180. |\n| framework--three-viewpoints-on-organising-endurance-development | GO | Unchanged since round 1, and round 1 checked it against the PDF 373 image. | Not re-opened. |\n| claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality | GO | The fix landed and the logic is now consistent. Read literally, p. 370 (anaerobic before aerobic) fits both p. 372 lists (PDF 372 image: positive \"aerobic exercises are done after glycolytic-anaerobic training (in small volume)\"; negative \"glycolytic-anaerobic exercises follow large volumes of aerobic work\"). It conflicts only with p. 373's aerobic-first sequential model. The slip reading is labelled Inference. Fixer disagreement 2 is justified: PDF 373 lists four citations (Naumenko 1978; Vavilov 1977; Serafimova 1974; Morzhevikov 1980), and p. 370 gives two of them. | Checked against the PDF 370 text: 3-4 months, up to 10 months, one month, HR 120-170, 2-3 months, about 4 months, \"not definitive\". PDF 372 image, PDF 373 text. |\n| principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue | GO | The added missing-idea passage is accurate: \"The training potential of the means employed decreases as the special work capacity increases\" and \"it is inappropriate to use highly stimulating means prematurely, because the body is functionally unprepared for them ... disturbance of the natural course of adaptation\" (PDF 368-369, printed 357-358, text layer). Small wording point, not blocking: \"closes the preceding passage\" is loose, because the Fig 6.28 caption and a conjugate-sequence paragraph come between the warning and 6.9.4. All links resolve. | Checked against the PDF 369-370 text: the homeostasis sentence, the ligamentous-joint sentence, the four sports, the five qualification citations, the intensity sentence, \"only after preliminary preparation with extensive low intensity loading\". |\n| framework--three-levels-of-change-in-fitness-state | GO (spot-checked) | None. The \"page image not opened\" note for PDF 379 is still honest. I checked the PDF 379 text: \"not especially complex\" and \"much smaller\" match. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: the three levels (italic labels), \"one or more years\", \"up to 5-6 months\", \"proportion of intensive means\", \"largely random and at times unpredictable\", \"not determined by the adaptation of the first two levels\", \"controllable\". Baseline lines 64 and 255 not re-opened (round 1 checked them). |\n| claim--annual-speed-strength-dynamics-depend-on-qualification | GO | Unchanged since round 1. | PDF 377 text re-read: 3 to 4 times a year, Pollock 1973, twice a month, the medium-qualification cause, the monocycle and bi-cycle patterns. |\n| case-study--triple-jumper-plantarflexors-depressed-while-thigh-extensors-high | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: the italic sentence (italic confirmed), Verkhoshansky 1988, the plantarflexor and thigh-extensor sentences, \"feels perfectly fit\", the prescription wording. `untested` fits. |\n| case-study--sirenko-double-peak-strength-means-for-middle-distance-runners | GO (spot-checked) | None. | Checked against the PDF 378 image: same volume and distribution of running means, single vs double peak, December and March-April, OBLA, 400 m and 1200 m control runs, 800 m 4.8 vs 3.4 s, 1500 m 6.3 vs 4.6 s. The Zhdanovich link is labelled Inference. |\n| case-study--barbell-and-depth-jump-sequences-in-high-jumpers | GO (spot-checked) | None blocking. My own Fig 6.24 readings (200 dpi crop, PDF 365) agree within about 2 units. Absolute strength: 36/54, 50/45, 63/53. Explosive: 53/102, 88/77, 122/95. Vertical jump: 14/26, 15/14, 34/24. Optional observation: on the Explosive Strength panel the printed tick labels (0, 40, 60, 100, 140) sit at roughly even spacing, so the \"40\" and \"60\" labels do not fit a linear 0-140 scale. My readings use the 0 and 140 ends and the 100 tick, which agree. The entry already flags units as unclear and could add \"the explosive-strength axis labels are unevenly printed\". | Checked: the design sentences and conclusion 1 (PDF 364 text); conclusions 2-3 and the caption (PDF 365 image); \"high jumpers of medium qualification\"; Khodykin and Chernousov at PDF 364 (text) and in the Ch 5 list on the PDF 299 image. |\n| case-study--nedobyvailov-light-heavy-and-combined-loads-in-jumpers | GO | **Fix verified on the 300 dpi crop.** At about 10.5 px per point: absolute strength GP1 7.0, GP2 18.2, GP3 16.8; speed GP1 17.9, GP2 11.1, GP3 13.5; power GP1 6.0, GP2 14.2, GP3 20.0 (the bar top sits on the 20 gridline). The entry's values (7, 18, 17; 18, 11, 14; 6, 14, 20), its Example and its When NOT pair (17 vs 18) all match. No old value remains. | Checked: 7 weeks, the three load groups, \"metabolic cost\", the caption, \"Group 1, graph 2\" (PDF 365 text and crop). |\n| framework--temporal-and-qualitative-forms-of-the-training-effect | GO | Unchanged since round 1, and round 1 checked it against the images. | Not re-opened. |\n| heuristic--routine-artificial-restoration-can-blunt-adaptation | GO | All three fixes landed: the \"signal\" gloss is labelled Inference; the 15% sentence now says the text does not specify the group, with the confound as Inference; and there is a blank line before Example (mechanical check). The fixer's extra Example change (\"without saying in which group\") is correct against PDF 368: \"At the same time, the volume of the training load was increased by an average of 15%\". | Checked against the PDF 368 text: 24.2/18.9 vs 7.7/4.9%, swimmers, Kuchnyev 1977, Platonov 1980, 2-4% and 12-20% (Potapchenko 1979), Yakovlev 1971, Siff & Yessis 1992, Yevgenyev et al 1975, \"strictly regulated\". Baseline lines 151 and 153. |\n| claim--middle-distance-runners-need-strength-and-jump-training-benchmarks | GO | The added missing ideas 2 and 3 are quoted accurately from the PDF 367 text (external similarity \"often overemphasized\", squats \"justified\", \"not only conform to, but exceed, competition conditions ... strength component\") and the PDF 363 text (\"cannot simply be used in a very general way\", the rower or cyclist example, \"The emergence of specificity ... is not supported\"). The 6.9.3 heading is on PDF 367. The 1.3 vs 1.4 note checks (PDF 35 heading \"1.3 A Philosophy of Physical Training\", PDF 38 heading \"1.4 Specificity in Training\") and is labelled Inference. Optional: \"Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side\" is the writer's framing; it could start with \"Inference:\". | Checked: the 800 m benchmarks, 10.6-10.7 s, 9 m and 33-34 m, Zhdanovich 1980, Verkhoshansky & Sirenko 1982, \"archaic viewpoint\" (PDF 367-368 text). Baseline lines 122 and 123. |\n\n**Tally: GO 17, FIX 2, NO-GO 0.**\n\n## Check 2: Addendum voice (PDF 514-590)\n\nI grepped all 19 files for \"Addendum\", \"PDF p. 5xx\" and \"p. 5xx\". Only two files carry Addendum material:\n- `principle--long-term-delayed-training-effect...`: it names \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, translated by Yessis; PDF p. 515)\", then \"the Addendum (PDF p. 536)\" in the same passage.\n- `heuristic--time-technique...`: it names \"Verkhoshansky's Addendum paper (9.1, ...)\" and \"Verkhoshansky's own, from the Addendum\".\n\nNo 6c statement from the Addendum is attributed to \"the authors\". PDF 514 is the \"9. ADDENDUM\" divider, and PDF 515 carries the 9.1 title block, which I confirmed on the image.\n\nOut of scope: the fix log flags `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md` (staging and vault) as citing PDF 536 with an unlabelled \"the Addendum says\". It is not one of the 19 and I did not review it. The coordinator should route it.\n\n## Check 3: the within-session contested paragraph\n\n| Page | What the entry says | What the page says (image) | Holds? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PDF 371 | order \"fairly unimportant\", then \"Nevertheless ... preserve the above order\"; a hedge | Same words; the italic sentence is italic | Yes |\n| PDF 288 | resistance plus plyometrics \"works best 'provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions'\" | \"can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided ...\" | Quote yes; \"works best\" overstates (FIX 1) |\n| PDF 299 | novices: \"no statistically significant difference\" between strength-first and speed-strength-first within the workout | Yes, groups ordered \"during the fundamental stage of their workouts\". The same page attributes the null result to novice status and predicts a positive strength-first effect for well-trained athletes | Accurate but incomplete (FIX 2) |\n| PDF 300 | \"Research with novices and athletes of average qualification\": \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" | Exact | Yes |\n| PDF 301 | bullets are stage-level, under the PDF 300 heading | Heading \"The following principles for the sequential use of training means over a given period emerge:\" at the foot of PDF 300; bullets at the top of PDF 301 | Yes |\n\nStage-level and session-level rules are kept apart in both the within-session and the sequential entries. The barbell = strength / jumping = speed-strength link is labelled Inference in both.\n\n## Check 5: mechanical checks over all 19 files\n\n- Six section headers in every file, each in the `**X** —` vault form, each preceded by a blank line.\n- Em dashes appear only in section headers. No en dashes.\n- No wrapped `[[wikilinks]]`.\n- 83 unique wikilink targets; every one resolves in the staging library or the vault (`/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`).\n- No review-history or process wording. The only grep hit is \"the reviewed combat and team-sport studies\", which refers to Issurin's review paper.\n- The baseline lines cited in the fixed entries (59, 74, 75, 76, 101, 104, 105, 106, 117, 122, 123, 142, 151, 153, 180, 182, 185, 186) match the quoted text.\n\n## Fix-log accuracy\n\nEvery change the fix log claims is present in the files. Its four disagreements with round 1 are each justified by the page:\n1. The barbell/jumping link labelled Inference: no page equates the terms.\n2. \"Appear among the four\": PDF 373 lists four citations, and p. 370 gives two of them.\n3. Plural and singular both kept: PDF 536 uses both.\n4. The PDF 438 / Fig 1.7 note not added. The PDF 438 text does say \"to perform endurance activities before strength and speed training (Fig 1.7)\", so the note would have been accurate. Leaving it out loses nothing required.\n\n## Overall verdict: GO AFTER FIXES\n\nRequired, both one-sentence text edits with no re-reading of figures:\n1. **principle LDTE:** label \"that rule reconciles the two readings, and the PDF p. 305 figure is a separate quantity\" as Inference.\n2. **within-session heuristic:** (a) PDF 288: \"even more effectively than plyometrics alone\" in place of \"works best\"; (b) add PDF 299's own qualification caveat (novice traces summed; \"A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect\", untested), with the qualification-split reading labelled Inference.\n\nOptional polish (not blocking): the doubled \"translation slip\" in the technique heuristic; \"Inference:\" on \"Section 6.8.3 sets the same frame from the other side\" in the middle-distance claim; noting the uneven explosive-strength tick labels in Fig 6.24.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b4c200f1-26d2-4977-8b9f-7a8c5316e34c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
